(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e3b.d6025baa4a.js"]={"29":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for personality disorders using safety, alliance, boundaries, psychoeducation, psychotherapy, and comorbidity management.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a personality disorder presents with acute, unstable affect and self-destructive impulses at the start of treatment. Her psychiatrist prioritizes pharmacotherapy over psychotherapy for the first several weeks. What is the rationale for this sequencing?","choices":{"A":"Pharmacotherapy provides prompt control of affect and behavior, creating a platform for later psychotherapy","B":"Psychotherapy is contraindicated until a full course of pharmacotherapy has been completed","C":"Pharmacotherapy permanently resolves personality pathology, making psychotherapy unnecessary","D":"Psychotherapy is ineffective while the personality disorder diagnosis is still active","E":"Pharmacotherapy is the primary treatment for personality disorders, with psychotherapy as an adjunct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Psychotherapy is the primary treatment for personality disorders since it promotes character maturation, but it is very hard to implement when unstable affect and self-destructive behaviors dominate the early clinical picture, so pharmacotherapy is used early to achieve prompt control of affect and behavior, setting a more suitable platform for psychotherapeutic work. Psychotherapy is not contraindicated during pharmacotherapy; the two are meant to work in sequence and combination, not as mutually exclusive phases. Pharmacotherapy does not permanently resolve personality pathology on its own; psychotherapy remains necessary for lasting character change. Psychotherapy is useful throughout treatment, not only after the disorder has resolved, which would be circular. Psychotherapy, not pharmacotherapy, is described as the primary treatment overall, with pharmacotherapy serving an adjunctive, stabilizing role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Most personality disorder patients need combined psychotherapy and pharmacotherapy; pharmacotherapy stabilizes acute affect/behavior early so psychotherapy can proceed effectively. COMLEX: This sequencing principle applies broadly, though the specific balance of psychotherapy versus pharmacotherapy differs by personality disorder and target symptom.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — extends the specific diagnostic features of paranoid/schizoid/schizotypal/antisocial/borderline/histrionic/narcissistic PD to their treatment principles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Psychotherapy is not contraind","D":"Psychotherapy is useful throug"}},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for personality disorders using safety, alliance, boundaries, psychoeducation, psychotherapy, and comorbidity management.","batch":1,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with borderline personality disorder is also diagnosed with a co-occurring eating disorder and substance use disorder during her intake evaluation. Which principle should guide her overall treatment plan?","choices":{"A":"The comorbid conditions should wait until the personality disorder itself has fully resolved","B":"Comorbid conditions should be managed alongside the personality disorder, which often predisposes to them","C":"A personality disorder diagnosis excludes any other concurrent psychiatric diagnosis","D":"Comorbidity in personality disorders is rare, so the personality disorder diagnosis should be reconsidered","E":"The most recently diagnosed condition should be treated first, with earlier diagnoses deferred"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Personality disorders are frequently comorbid with other clinical syndromes and are predisposing factors for conditions such as substance use, eating disorders, and affective disorders, so comorbidities should be actively identified and managed alongside the personality disorder itself, particularly since borderline personality disorder specifically is associated with increased risk for eating disorders (notably bulimia) and substance use disorders. Ignoring comorbid conditions until the personality disorder resolves delays necessary treatment and ignores how intertwined these conditions typically are. A personality disorder diagnosis does not exclude other concurrent diagnoses; comorbidity is the norm, not the exception, occurring in roughly half of psychiatric inpatients and outpatients with a personality disorder. Comorbidity is common, not rare, in personality disorders, so this presentation does not call the underlying diagnosis into question. Prioritizing only the most recently diagnosed condition has no clinical basis; all active, clinically significant conditions warrant coordinated management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Personality disorders occur in 10-20% of the general population and about half of psychiatric inpatients/outpatients, with substantial comorbidity (substance use, eating disorders, affective/anxiety disorders). COMLEX: Borderline PD specifically carries elevated risk for bulimia, PTSD, ADHD, and somatic symptom disorder — know the pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — extends the specific diagnostic features of paranoid/schizoid/schizotypal/antisocial/borderline/histrionic/narcissistic PD to their treatment principles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for personality disorders using safety, alliance, boundaries, psychoeducation, psychotherapy, and comorbidity management.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician treating a patient with avoidant personality disorder focuses early sessions on building trust and conveying acceptance of the patient's fear of rejection before encouraging the patient to take social risks. Which core treatment principle does this approach illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Feared situations should be confronted immediately, before any alliance is established","B":"Trust-building is unnecessary, since medication resolves avoidant symptoms on its own","C":"A skeptical, confrontational stance toward the patient's fears is the most effective first step","D":"Avoidant personality disorder does not respond to psychotherapy of any kind","E":"An alliance comes first, with the fear accepted before feared situations are challenged"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For avoidant personality disorder, psychotherapeutic treatment depends on solidifying an alliance with the patient first; as trust develops, the therapist conveys an accepting attitude toward the patient's fears, particularly fear of rejection, before eventually encouraging the patient to take social risks, exactly the sequencing illustrated here. Challenging a patient to confront feared situations before any alliance exists risks reinforcing the patient's already weak self-esteem through premature failure. Trust-building remains essential even when medication is also used; pharmacotherapy is adjunctive, not a substitute for the therapeutic relationship. A skeptical, confrontational stance runs counter to the accepting, alliance-building approach specifically recommended for avoidant personality disorder. Avoidant personality disorder is treatable with psychotherapy, including insight-oriented approaches, group therapy, and assertiveness training, so claiming it cannot be treated with psychotherapy is incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alliance-building before exposure/challenge is a general treatment principle, particularly emphasized for avoidant personality disorder given the central fear of rejection and humiliation. COMLEX: Group therapy and assertiveness training are useful adjuncts once a foundational alliance is established.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — extends the specific diagnostic features of paranoid/schizoid/schizotypal/antisocial/borderline/histrionic/narcissistic PD to their treatment principles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Challenging a patient to confr","B":"Trust-building remains essenti","D":"Avoidant personality disorder "}},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate psychotherapy approaches relevant to personality pathology, including DBT, CBT, psychodynamic therapy, mentalization-based treatment, and supportive therapy.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with borderline personality disorder is enrolled in a psychotherapy program that includes weekly individual therapy, group skills training, phone consultation, and a therapist consultation team, with the explicit goals of improving interpersonal skills and decreasing self-destructive behavior. Which psychotherapy modality is this?","choices":{"A":"Transference-focused psychotherapy","B":"Mentalization-based treatment","C":"Supportive psychotherapy","D":"Dialectical behavior therapy","E":"Standard cognitive behavioral therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dialectical behavior therapy has four primary modes of treatment — group skill training, individual therapy, phone consultation, and consultation team — matching the structure described, and it has received the most empirical support of any psychosocial treatment for borderline personality disorder. Transference-focused psychotherapy is a modified psychodynamic approach centered on clarifying and confronting transference distortions, a different mechanism from DBT's skills-based structure. Mentalization-based treatment centers on rebuilding the capacity to be attentive to one's own and others' mental states, a distinct theoretical target. Supportive psychotherapy alone offers empathy and reassurance without dedicated skills training or crisis coaching. Standard cognitive behavioral therapy alone restructures maladaptive thoughts but lacks a dedicated skills-training or crisis-coaching component, though DBT does draw on CBT concepts as part of its eclectic approach.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DBT's four modes — individual therapy, group skills training, phone consultation, consultation team — is the classic testable structure, and DBT is the best-empirically-supported psychotherapy for borderline personality disorder specifically. COMLEX: DBT assumes all behavior, including thoughts and feelings, is learned, and treats maladaptive behaviors as reinforced/rewarded patterns to be unlearned.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — links the defenses (splitting, projective identification) seen in Cluster A/B presentations to the psychotherapy modalities designed to address them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate psychotherapy approaches relevant to personality pathology, including DBT, CBT, psychodynamic therapy, mentalization-based treatment, and supportive therapy.","batch":1,"stem":"A therapist explains to a colleague that a patient's difficulty regulating emotions and managing impulsivity stems from a reduced capacity to be attentive to the mental states of both herself and others, and that therapy will focus on rebuilding this specific capacity. Which psychotherapy modality is being described?","choices":{"A":"Dialectical behavior therapy","B":"Mentalization-based treatment","C":"Transference-focused psychotherapy","D":"Standard cognitive behavioral therapy","E":"Group psychoeducation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mentalization-based treatment is grounded in the theory that borderline personality symptoms, including difficulty regulating emotions and managing impulsivity, result from a reduced capacity to mentalize (to be attentive to one's own and others' mental states), and treatment focuses on rebuilding this capacity to improve relationship skills and emotional regulation, exactly as described. Dialectical behavior therapy centers on its four-mode structure (individual, group skills, phone consultation, consultation team) targeting self-destructive behavior and interpersonal skills, not specifically the mentalization construct. Transference-focused psychotherapy centers on analyzing and confronting transference distortions using object relations theory, a different theoretical framework from mentalization. Standard cognitive behavioral therapy centers on identifying and restructuring maladaptive thought patterns, not specifically rebuilding mentalization capacity. Group psychoeducation alone does not represent a distinct, theory-driven modality targeting mentalization capacity specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mentalization-based treatment (MBT) targets the specific deficit of reduced mentalization capacity underlying borderline personality disorder's emotional dysregulation and impulsivity. COMLEX: MBT has shown effectiveness in several randomized controlled trials for borderline personality disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — links the defenses (splitting, projective identification) seen in Cluster A/B presentations to the psychotherapy modalities designed to address them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate psychotherapy approaches relevant to personality pathology, including DBT, CBT, psychodynamic therapy, mentalization-based treatment, and supportive therapy.","batch":1,"stem":"A therapist treating a patient with borderline personality disorder uses two major techniques: first directly analyzing the patient's distortions about the therapist as they arise, and second, pointing out how these distortions interfere with the patient's relationships with others, grounded in object relations theory. Which psychotherapy modality is this?","choices":{"A":"Transference-focused psychotherapy","B":"Dialectical behavior therapy","C":"Mentalization-based treatment","D":"Supportive psychotherapy","E":"Assertiveness training"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Transference-focused psychotherapy is a modified psychodynamic approach grounded in object relations theory that relies on two major processes — clarification (directly analyzing transference distortions as they arise) and confrontation (pointing out how these distortions interfere with relationships toward others) — matching this description exactly, and it specifically addresses the splitting defense (good object/bad object) common in borderline personality disorder. Dialectical behavior therapy relies on its structured four-mode format rather than clarification/confrontation of transference specifically. Mentalization-based treatment centers on rebuilding mentalization capacity, not this clarification/confrontation transference-analysis structure. Standard supportive psychotherapy generally avoids in-depth interpretation of transference, favoring a more reality-oriented, supportive stance instead. Assertiveness training alone is a behavioral technique, not a comprehensive psychodynamic modality built around transference analysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transference-focused psychotherapy (TFP) = clarification + confrontation of transference distortions, grounded in object relations theory, specifically targeting the splitting defense in borderline personality disorder. COMLEX: Studies comparing TFP, DBT, psychodynamic psychotherapy, and supportive psychotherapy show all are useful with varying degrees of success — no single modality is universally superior.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — links the defenses (splitting, projective identification) seen in Cluster A/B presentations to the psychotherapy modalities designed to address them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze when pharmacotherapy may be used symptom-targetedly for mood lability, impulsivity, aggression, anxiety, depression, or psychosis-like symptoms.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a personality disorder has chronic cognitive anxiety symptoms. Per the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework, which class of medication should generally be avoided for this specific symptom domain?","choices":{"A":"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors","B":"Benzodiazepines such as alprazolam","C":"Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors","D":"Low-dose atypical antipsychotics","E":"Anticonvulsant mood stabilizers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For chronic cognitive anxiety in personality disorder patients, the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework favors psychotherapy, SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs, low-dose novel psychotropics like aripiprazole or quetiapine, and GABA analogs like valproate or clonazepam, while specifically avoiding benzodiazepines because of their abuse potential and risk of disinhibition in this population. SSRIs target the serotonergic component underlying chronic cognitive anxiety. SNRIs add noradrenergic action when SSRIs alone are insufficient. Low-dose novel psychotropics like aripiprazole or quetiapine are used as an adjunct for refractory cognitive anxiety. GABA analogs such as valproate and clonazepam are used when anxiety symptoms accompany mood instability, in contrast to standard benzodiazepines as a class.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Benzodiazepines are generally avoided across most personality disorder target-symptom domains (anxiety, aggression) due to disinhibition and abuse-potential concerns. COMLEX: Matching a drug of choice, and the drugs to avoid, to each target symptom domain is a classic testable pharmacotherapy framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — extends the lithium/valproate/carbamazepine/lamotrigine/antipsychotic comparison framework to personality-disorder symptom-domain-targeted use","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"SSRIs target the serotonergic ","C":"SNRIs add noradrenergic action","D":"Low-dose novel psychotropics l","E":"GABA analogs such as valproate"}},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze when pharmacotherapy may be used symptom-targetedly for mood lability, impulsivity, aggression, anxiety, depression, or psychosis-like symptoms.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a personality disorder has affective aggression (\"hot temper\" with a normal EEG). Per the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework, which class of medication should be avoided for this specific presentation due to a risk of disinhibition?","choices":{"A":"Lithium carbonate","B":"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors","C":"Anticonvulsant mood stabilizers","D":"Low-dose antipsychotics","E":"Benzodiazepines such as lorazepam"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For affective aggression with a normal EEG (\"hot temper\"), the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework favors lithium, SSRIs, anticonvulsants, or low-dose antipsychotics, while avoiding benzodiazepines because disinhibition from this class can worsen rather than calm aggressive behavior. Lithium is valued here for its mood-stabilizing, anti-aggressive properties. SSRIs act on the serotonergic pathways linked to irritability and impulsive aggression. Anticonvulsants are used specifically for the \"hot temper with normal EEG\" presentation. Low-dose antipsychotics are used to blunt affective aggression through dopaminergic modulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Benzodiazepines are avoided across nearly every aggression-related target symptom (affective aggression, predatory aggression, organic-like aggression) due to disinhibition risk — a consistent, testable pattern across the aggression target symptoms. COMLEX: Ictal aggression (abnormal EEG) is the one aggression subtype where benzodiazepines like clonazepam ARE recommended, since seizure-related aggression responds to anticonvulsant-class benzodiazepines.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — extends the lithium/valproate/carbamazepine/lamotrigine/antipsychotic comparison framework to personality-disorder symptom-domain-targeted use","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"SSRIs act on the serotonergic ","D":"Low-dose antipsychotics are us"}},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze when pharmacotherapy may be used symptom-targetedly for mood lability, impulsivity, aggression, anxiety, depression, or psychosis-like symptoms.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a personality disorder has brief, transient psychotic-like symptoms during periods of stress. Per the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework, which class of medication is the recommended treatment for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Benzodiazepines as monotherapy, used until the perceptual distortions resolve","B":"Tricyclic antidepressants as monotherapy, used through the stressful period","C":"Novel or typical antipsychotics, used for the duration of the episode","D":"Beta-adrenergic receptor antagonists as monotherapy, used during each episode","E":"Stimulant medications as monotherapy, used to sharpen reality testing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For acute and brief psychotic episodes, the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework recommends novel (atypical) antipsychotics such as risperidone or olanzapine, or typical neuroleptics used for the duration of the psychotic episode, directly targeting these cognitive-perceptual distortions. Benzodiazepines as monotherapy can calm acute agitation but lack antipsychotic efficacy for the perceptual distortions themselves. Tricyclic antidepressants as monotherapy target mood symptoms rather than psychotic-level distortions. Beta-adrenergic receptor antagonists are used in this framework for other target domains, such as avoidant personality disorder's autonomic hyperactivity or aggression, not psychotic-like symptoms. Stimulant medications can precipitate or worsen psychotic-like symptoms in vulnerable patients rather than treat them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cognitive-perceptual distortions/psychotic-like symptoms in personality disorders (e.g., transient stress-related psychosis in schizotypal or borderline PD) are treated with novel antipsychotics acutely, or chronically with low-dose antipsychotics for persistent low-level symptoms. COMLEX: This mirrors the general principle that antipsychotics, not benzodiazepines, are first-line for psychotic symptoms regardless of the underlying personality disorder diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — extends the lithium/valproate/carbamazepine/lamotrigine/antipsychotic comparison framework to personality-disorder symptom-domain-targeted use","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret countertransference, splitting, dependency, boundary testing, and therapeutic rupture as clinical factors that affect care delivery.","batch":1,"stem":"A therapist treating a patient with borderline personality disorder notices they are being unconsciously pressured to act out a particular behavior the patient is unable to acknowledge directly. The therapist recognizes this as a countertransference reaction to which specific defense mechanism used by the patient?","choices":{"A":"Rationalization","B":"Reaction formation","C":"Displacement","D":"Projective identification","E":"Regression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Projective identification can cause countertransference problems specifically when therapists are unaware that patients are unconsciously trying to coerce them into acting out a particular behavior, exactly the phenomenon described, and this defense appears mainly in borderline personality disorder. Rationalization offers logical-sounding justifications for one's own behavior, not coercive pressure on another person to enact a role. Reaction formation involves converting an unacceptable impulse into its opposite, not pressuring another person into a role. Displacement involves redirecting an emotion onto a substitute target, not this specific coercive identification process. Regression involves reverting to an earlier developmental stage, unrelated to this interpersonal coercion dynamic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Projective identification's coercive interpersonal quality is precisely what makes it a common source of countertransference confusion in treating borderline personality disorder. COMLEX: Recognizing this dynamic in oneself as a clinician is the first step to responding therapeutically rather than acting out the coerced role unconsciously.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — extends the transference/countertransference framework to the broader clinical factors (splitting, dependency, boundary testing) affecting care delivery","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret countertransference, splitting, dependency, boundary testing, and therapeutic rupture as clinical factors that affect care delivery.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with borderline personality disorder alternately idealizes and devalues her therapist within the same treatment episode, causing the therapist significant confusion about the therapeutic relationship. Which defense mechanism is directly responsible for this alternating pattern, and what therapeutic approach is generally more effective than deep unconscious interpretation in managing it?","choices":{"A":"Splitting; in-depth interpretation of the unconscious works better than a reality-oriented approach","B":"Projection; a reality-oriented approach works better than in-depth interpretation","C":"Splitting; a reality-oriented approach works better than in-depth interpretation","D":"Displacement; in-depth interpretation of the unconscious works better than a reality-oriented approach","E":"Isolation of affect; in-depth interpretation of the unconscious works better than a reality-oriented approach"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The splitting defense mechanism causes patients with borderline personality disorder to alternately love and hate their therapists, and a reality-oriented approach is generally more effective than in-depth interpretations of the unconscious in managing this dynamic, since patients regress quickly and show labile transferences that are difficult to analyze in depth. Claiming in-depth interpretation of the unconscious is more effective than a reality-oriented approach gets the clinical guidance backward. Projection involves attributing one's own feelings to another person, not this alternating idealization/devaluation pattern of splitting. Displacement involves redirecting an emotion onto a substitute target, not this specific alternating pattern toward the same person. Isolation of affect involves detached factual recall without emotion, the opposite of this emotionally intense alternating pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Splitting drives the idealize/devalue alternation seen with borderline personality disorder patients; a reality-oriented, rather than deeply interpretive, therapeutic stance is generally recommended given how quickly these patients regress. COMLEX: Anticipating and normalizing this pattern with the treatment team helps prevent staff splitting in inpatient or multidisciplinary settings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — extends the transference/countertransference framework to the broader clinical factors (splitting, dependency, boundary testing) affecting care delivery","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Claiming in-depth interpretati"}},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret countertransference, splitting, dependency, boundary testing, and therapeutic rupture as clinical factors that affect care delivery.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with dependent personality disorder repeatedly calls her therapist between sessions for reassurance about minor decisions and becomes distressed when the therapist maintains standard session boundaries. Which clinical factor is most directly illustrated by this pattern, and how should it be addressed in treatment?","choices":{"A":"Dependency and boundary testing; allow unlimited between-session contact to ease her distress","B":"Splitting; foster independence while keeping consistent boundaries","C":"Acting out; allow unlimited between-session contact to ease her distress","D":"Countertransference; allow unlimited between-session contact to ease her distress","E":"Dependency and boundary testing; foster independence while keeping consistent boundaries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This pattern of excessive reassurance-seeking and distress at standard boundaries illustrates dependency and boundary testing characteristic of dependent personality disorder, and treatment (often successful with insight-oriented, behavioral, or assertiveness-focused therapy) should support the patient in becoming more independent, assertive, and self-reliant while the therapist maintains consistent boundaries rather than reinforcing the dependent pattern. Accommodating unlimited between-session contact would reinforce rather than treat the underlying dependency pattern. Splitting involves alternating idealization and devaluation of the same person, not this dependency and reassurance-seeking pattern. Acting out involves directly expressing an unconscious conflict through action, not this dependency-driven boundary testing. Countertransference refers to the clinician's own reactions to the patient, not the patient's dependency behavior itself, though the clinician's response to it is clinically important.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dependent personality disorder treatment focuses on fostering independence and self-reliance through insight-oriented and behavioral approaches while maintaining firm, consistent boundaries. COMLEX: Consistent boundary-setting across all treatment team members prevents a dependent patient from being inadvertently reinforced by inconsistent responses to boundary testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — extends the transference/countertransference framework to the broader clinical factors (splitting, dependency, boundary testing) affecting care delivery","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Evaluate suicide risk, violence risk, substance use, trauma history, and level-of-care needs in patients with personality disorder features.","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with borderline personality disorder is being evaluated in the emergency department. Per the known course of the disorder, during which period of life is her risk of suicide and impairment generally highest?","choices":{"A":"Young adulthood, with symptom severity gradually easing by her 40s and 50s","B":"Adolescence, with risk peaking before age 18 and disappearing entirely after that","C":"A constant, unchanging level of risk across the entire lifespan","D":"Risk highest after age 60, increasing progressively with advancing age","E":"Risk confined to formal inpatient hospitalization and otherwise negligible"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The impairment and risk of suicide in borderline personality disorder are highest in the young adult years and gradually wane with advancing age, with individuals in the fourth and fifth decades of life tending to attain greater stability in relationships and functioning, matching this patient's presentation as a young adult. Risk is not confined only to adolescence with complete disappearance after 18; the described course extends the elevated risk period into young adulthood specifically. Risk does not remain constant across the lifespan; it follows this described wane-with-age pattern. Risk is not described as highest after age 60 and increasing with age; the pattern is the opposite, with risk being highest earlier and decreasing later. Suicide risk in borderline personality disorder is not confined to inpatient settings; it is a concern across the course of the illness, particularly in young adulthood regardless of care setting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Borderline personality disorder's suicide/impairment risk peaks in young adulthood and generally decreases by the 4th-5th decades — an important prognostic pattern for risk stratification. COMLEX: This age-related risk pattern should directly inform level-of-care decisions for young adult patients presenting acutely.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5 — applies the suicide/violence risk-factor framework specifically to patients with personality disorder features","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Risk is not described as highe","E":"Suicide risk in borderline per"}},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Evaluate suicide risk, violence risk, substance use, trauma history, and level-of-care needs in patients with personality disorder features.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with antisocial personality disorder is being evaluated for long-term prognosis. Which complication is specifically associated with the natural course of this disorder as antisocial behavior \"burns out\" with age?","choices":{"A":"Complete resolution of all personality features, with no residual irritability, impulsivity, or dysphoria","B":"Universal progression to a formal psychotic disorder, with hallucinations, delusions, and disorganized behavior","C":"Continued irritability, impulsivity, and detachment, along with dysphoria and risk of premature, violent death","D":"Guaranteed development of a comorbid eating disorder, with binge eating, purging, or restrictive patterns","E":"Elimination of any elevated risk of premature death, despite continued impulsivity and antisocial risk-taking"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Even after the height of antisocial behavior \"burns out,\" typically after late adolescence, individuals with antisocial personality disorder usually continue to be irritable, impulsive, and detached, with complications including dysphoria, tension, low tolerance for boredom, depressed mood, and premature, violent death. Complete resolution with no residual symptoms misstates the actual course, which involves continued impairment even as overt antisocial behavior decreases. Antisocial personality disorder does not universally progress to a formal psychotic disorder; that is not part of its described natural course. A comorbid eating disorder is not a guaranteed or specifically associated complication of antisocial personality disorder (unlike, for example, borderline personality disorder's association with bulimia). Premature death risk is not eliminated once antisocial behavior decreases; it remains a described long-term complication of the disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antisocial personality disorder's course = unremitting, peaking in late adolescence, with some symptom decrease with age but continued irritability/impulsivity/detachment and ongoing risk of premature violent death. COMLEX: This informs long-term risk assessment and safety planning even once acute antisocial behaviors appear to lessen.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5 — applies the suicide/violence risk-factor framework specifically to patients with personality disorder features","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Evaluate suicide risk, violence risk, substance use, trauma history, and level-of-care needs in patients with personality disorder features.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with borderline personality disorder and a history of childhood trauma presents with active self-injurious behavior, impulsivity, and a home environment marked by ongoing intrafamilial conflict and abuse. Which level-of-care recommendation is most consistent with this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Outpatient treatment with weekly visits, regardless of the safety of her home environment","B":"Hospital-based treatment, which protects her from self-harm and an unsafe home","C":"No formal treatment until the patient requests it directly and commits to attending","D":"Solitary, self-directed treatment using workbooks in place of any structured setting","E":"Family therapy in the home, with the family taking responsibility for her safety"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patients with borderline personality disorder often do well in a hospital setting where intensive individual and group psychotherapy can be combined with a protected environment that allows limits to be placed on excessively impulsive, self-destructive, or self-mutilating behavior, and such programs are especially helpful when the home environment is detrimental to rehabilitation due to intrafamilial conflict or abuse, exactly this patient's situation. Outpatient treatment only, without considering the described unsafe home environment and active self-injurious behavior, does not adequately address her acute safety needs. Withholding treatment until the patient requests it directly ignores the urgency of active self-injurious behavior and an unsafe home environment. Solitary, unsupervised treatment would remove the protective structure and staff limit-setting that this level of acuity requires. Family therapy alone, without addressing the safety of the current home environment, does not adequately account for the described abuse and conflict at home.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Level-of-care decisions for borderline personality disorder weigh acuity of self-harm/impulsivity against home environment safety; a detrimental home environment (abuse, conflict) is a specific indication favoring hospital-based or protected structured care. COMLEX: Discharge planning to specialized support systems (day hospitals, halfway houses) follows once marked improvement is achieved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5 — applies the suicide/violence risk-factor framework specifically to patients with personality disorder features","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select patient-centered management strategies that maintain professionalism, ethics, continuity, and team-based consistency.","batch":1,"stem":"A treatment team caring for a patient with borderline personality disorder on an inpatient unit notices the patient idealizing some staff members while uniformly disparaging others. Which team-based management strategy best addresses this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Each staff member should respond independently, without discussing the pattern with the team","B":"The idealized staff members should be permanently reassigned away from the patient","C":"The team should discuss the splitting and remind the patient no one is all good or all bad","D":"The devalued staff members should defer to the idealized ones in all decisions about her care","E":"Staff should argue with the patient in the moment about which staff are actually good or bad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When staff members anticipate the splitting process, discuss it at team meetings, and gently confront the patient with the fact that no one is entirely good or entirely bad, this defense behavior can be dealt with effectively, preventing the disruption and staff conflict that unaddressed splitting can provoke on an inpatient unit. Each staff member independently deciding their own response without team coordination is exactly the fragmented approach that allows splitting to become disruptive and provoke staff conflict. Permanently reassigning idealized staff does not address the underlying pattern and may simply shift idealization to new staff members. Permanently reassigning devalued staff to validate the patient's perception reinforces the splitting distortion rather than addressing it therapeutically. Openly arguing with the patient about which staff are good or bad engages with the split at face value rather than gently reframing it, which is the recommended therapeutic approach.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Team-based consistency and open communication among staff is the standard, evidence-informed response to inpatient splitting behavior in borderline personality disorder. COMLEX: This principle extends the broader countertransference/splitting framework into a concrete, team-based professionalism and continuity-of-care strategy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn framework to the broader professionalism/ethics/team-consistency principles needed in personality disorder care","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Permanently reassigning ideali","E":"Openly arguing with the patien"}},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select patient-centered management strategies that maintain professionalism, ethics, continuity, and team-based consistency.","batch":1,"stem":"A resident physician treating a patient with paranoid personality disorder finds the patient's frequent complaints about perceived injustices frustrating and is tempted to argue directly against the patient's suspicions. Which professional approach is recommended instead?","choices":{"A":"Argue firmly against the patient's suspicions each time they arise in a session","B":"Agree with every suspicion the patient raises in order to strengthen the alliance","C":"Avoid any discussion of the patient's complaints so as to prevent open conflict","D":"Stay honest, admit mistakes, and neither dispute nor reinforce the complaints","E":"Adopt a warm, informal, and personally disclosing style to build rapport quickly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With paranoid personality disorder, clinicians should maintain strict honesty, concern for the patient's rights, acknowledge even minor mistakes, and neither dispute the patient's complaints with confrontation nor reinforce them outright, instead discussing the possibility of future difficulties together, since confrontation guarantees a lasting enemy and early termination of the interview. Directly and firmly arguing against the patient's suspicions is explicitly counterproductive and risks damaging the relationship irreparably. Enthusiastically agreeing with every suspicion regardless of plausibility reinforces the paranoid distortions rather than maintaining appropriate professional honesty. Avoiding discussion of complaints altogether fails to engage therapeutically and does not build the alliance needed for treatment. An overly warm, informal style is specifically discouraged with paranoid personality disorder, which instead requires a professional, not overly warm, therapeutic style.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paranoid personality disorder management = professional, honest, non-confrontational stance; avoid both direct dispute and uncritical reinforcement of the patient's suspicions. COMLEX: This professionalism principle directly supports continuity of care, since confrontation is described as reliably ending the therapeutic relationship.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn framework to the broader professionalism/ethics/team-consistency principles needed in personality disorder care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select patient-centered management strategies that maintain professionalism, ethics, continuity, and team-based consistency.","batch":1,"stem":"A multidisciplinary team is coordinating long-term outpatient care for a patient with a personality disorder, involving a psychiatrist, therapist, and case manager. Which principle best supports continuity and ethical, patient-centered care across this team?","choices":{"A":"All team members maintain consistent boundaries and communicate regularly, giving the patient a coordinated approach","B":"Each provider sets boundaries independently, without coordinating with the rest of the care team","C":"The psychiatrist's treatment decisions are what carry weight, with the therapist and case manager simply deferring to them","D":"Team members compete to become the patient's preferred provider, in order to maximize individual therapeutic rapport","E":"The patient eventually receiving some form of treatment from someone matters more than consistency of care across providers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Consistent boundaries and regular communication across all team members support continuity of care and prevent the patient from receiving conflicting responses from different providers, which is particularly important in personality disorder treatment given the risk of splitting and boundary testing across a care team. Each provider independently setting boundaries without coordination directly risks the disruptive team-splitting dynamic that unified, communicating teams are specifically meant to prevent. Deferring all decisions to only the psychiatrist without input from the therapist or case manager undermines the collaborative, team-based approach that supports comprehensive care. Providers competing to be the patient's preferred provider actively works against consistent, professional boundaries and can worsen dependency or splitting dynamics. Continuity of care is clinically important, particularly for personality disorder patients whose treatment often benefits from long-term, coordinated relationships with a stable team.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Coordinated, communicating multidisciplinary teams with consistent boundaries are the standard for personality disorder management, directly preventing the splitting and inconsistency that can otherwise undermine care. COMLEX: This principle applies professionalism and team-based consistency concretely to real-world outpatient care coordination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn framework to the broader professionalism/ethics/team-consistency principles needed in personality disorder care","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Continuity of care is clinical"}},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for personality disorders using safety, alliance, boundaries, psychoeducation, psychotherapy, and comorbidity management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man with schizotypal personality disorder is started on treatment. His psychiatrist notes his prognosis includes a meaningful risk of suicide over the long term, distinct from any single episode of transient psychosis. What long-term statistic should inform the safety planning and psychoeducation discussion for this patient?","choices":{"A":"No long-term study has reported an elevated suicide risk in schizotypal personality disorder","B":"Suicide risk in schizotypal personality disorder is confined to periods of sustained psychosis","C":"Suicide risk in schizotypal personality disorder is lower than in the general population","D":"Suicide risk in schizotypal personality disorder is roughly 1 percent over a lifetime","E":"In one long-term study, 10 percent of patients with schizotypal disorder died by suicide"},"correct":"E","explanation":"One long-term study reported that 10 percent of patients with schizotypal personality disorder eventually committed suicide, an important statistic for safety planning and psychoeducation, distinct from the disorder's more commonly discussed transient, stress-related psychotic episodes. Claiming no long-term studies have reported elevated suicide risk directly contradicts this documented finding. Suicide risk is not limited only to periods of active, sustained psychosis, since schizotypal personality disorder's psychotic symptoms are typically brief and fragmentary, yet the suicide risk is described as a broader long-term concern. This risk is not lower than the general population; a 10 percent lifetime suicide rate represents a substantially elevated risk. A 1 percent lifetime figure understates the reported risk tenfold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The 10% long-term suicide rate in schizotypal personality disorder is a specific, testable statistic that should inform safety planning independent of acute psychotic symptom status. COMLEX: Comorbid major depression (present in over half of schizotypal PD patients at some point) likely compounds this suicide risk and should be actively screened for.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — Diagnostic Features of Specific Personality Disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Claiming no long-term studies ","D":"A 1 percent lifetime figure un"}},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for personality disorders using safety, alliance, boundaries, psychoeducation, psychotherapy, and comorbidity management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with paranoid personality disorder is also being evaluated for a possible comorbid condition. Given the known comorbidity pattern of paranoid personality disorder, which condition should her psychiatrist screen for as a potential premorbid relationship?","choices":{"A":"Late-onset bipolar I disorder","B":"Generalized anxiety disorder","C":"Persecutory-type delusional disorder","D":"Early-onset Alzheimer disease","E":"Autism spectrum disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paranoid personality disorder may be a premorbid antecedent of delusional disorder, persecutory type, in addition to carrying increased risk for major depression, obsessive-compulsive disorder, agoraphobia, and substance use disorders, making this the specific premorbid relationship to screen for. Bipolar I disorder is not described as the specific premorbid condition associated with paranoid personality disorder in this framework. While anxiety disorders (agoraphobia specifically) are part of the described comorbidity pattern, generalized anxiety disorder is not named, and the framework describes several comorbidities, not an anxiety-exclusive pattern. Alzheimer disease is not part of the described comorbidity or premorbid-antecedent pattern for paranoid personality disorder. Autism spectrum disorder is not described as a premorbid-antecedent relationship for paranoid personality disorder in this framework (this relationship is more relevant to schizoid personality disorder's differential, not comorbidity, discussion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paranoid PD -> increased risk for major depression, OCD, agoraphobia, substance use disorders, and may premorbidly precede delusional disorder, persecutory type. COMLEX: Distinguishing a \"premorbid antecedent\" relationship from a co-occurring comorbidity is a subtle but testable distinction in personality disorder course and complications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — Diagnostic Features of Specific Personality Disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for personality disorders using safety, alliance, boundaries, psychoeducation, psychotherapy, and comorbidity management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with antisocial personality disorder is being treated for a co-occurring substance use disorder. His psychiatrist is cautious about pharmacotherapy choices given his history. Which broader treatment principle applies specifically because of his comorbid substance misuse history?","choices":{"A":"Medications are contraindicated in antisocial personality disorder regardless of comorbidity","B":"Substance use history has no bearing on medication selection in personality disorders","C":"Benzodiazepines should be preferred because of their rapid onset of action","D":"Medications should be chosen judiciously because of his risk of misusing prescribed drugs","E":"Psychotherapy should be abandoned once a comorbid substance use disorder is identified"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pharmacotherapy can address incapacitating symptoms such as anxiety, rage, and depression in antisocial personality disorder, but because these patients often misuse substances, medications should be used judiciously, reflecting the broader treatment principle that comorbidity (here, substance use) must directly shape safe medication selection. Medications are not absolutely contraindicated in antisocial personality disorder; anticonvulsants, beta-blockers, lithium, and antipsychotics are all described as potentially useful, just requiring judicious selection given misuse risk. Substance use history is explicitly relevant to medication selection in this population, not irrelevant, given the described misuse risk. Benzodiazepines specifically carry elevated concern in populations prone to substance misuse and disinhibition, making them a poor rather than preferred choice here. Comorbid substance use does not mean psychotherapy should be abandoned; comorbidity management calls for addressing both conditions together, not abandoning treatment for one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Judicious medication selection in the setting of comorbid substance use disorder is a core treatment principle across personality disorders, especially antisocial PD given documented rates of substance misuse. COMLEX: Anticonvulsants (especially with abnormal EEG), beta-blockers, lithium, and antipsychotics are the described safer options for aggression in this population, versus benzodiazepines.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — Diagnostic Features of Specific Personality Disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Substance use history is expli"}},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate psychotherapy approaches relevant to personality pathology, including DBT, CBT, psychodynamic therapy, mentalization-based treatment, and supportive therapy.","batch":2,"stem":"A research team compares transference-focused psychotherapy, dialectical behavior therapy, psychodynamic psychotherapy, and supportive psychotherapy for borderline personality disorder. Which conclusion is most consistent with the actual comparative evidence for these four modalities?","choices":{"A":"Dialectical behavior therapy shows measurable benefit, while the other three show none","B":"Transference-focused psychotherapy is clearly superior to the other three in every trial","C":"All four are useful with varying success, and none is universally superior","D":"Supportive psychotherapy is the sole modality with published comparative outcome data","E":"All four modalities are equally ineffective for borderline personality disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Studies comparing transference-focused psychotherapy, dialectical behavior therapy, psychodynamic psychotherapy, and supportive psychotherapy show that all are useful and all show varying degrees of success, meaning no single modality has been established as universally superior to the others for borderline personality disorder. Claiming only DBT shows benefit while the others show none contradicts the described finding that all four are useful. Claiming only TFP shows benefit similarly contradicts this finding. Supportive psychotherapy is not the only modality studied; comparative data exist across all four approaches as described. Claiming all four are equally and completely ineffective directly contradicts the described finding that all are useful with varying degrees of success.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No single evidence-based psychotherapy (TFP, DBT, psychodynamic, supportive) is definitively superior for borderline personality disorder — all show benefit, informing individualized modality selection. COMLEX: Availability, patient preference, and therapist training/expertise often guide which specific modality is selected in practice, given this comparable efficacy profile.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Defense Mechanisms in Cluster A & B Presentations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Claiming only DBT shows benefi","B":"Claiming only TFP shows benefi","E":"Claiming all four are equally "}},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate psychotherapy approaches relevant to personality pathology, including DBT, CBT, psychodynamic therapy, mentalization-based treatment, and supportive therapy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with schizotypal personality disorder begins psychotherapy. His clinician notes that treatment principles for this disorder largely mirror those for schizoid personality disorder, but require additional sensitivity in one specific area. Which specific sensitivity is required?","choices":{"A":"Care to avoid any discussion of antipsychotic or antidepressant medication options","B":"Care to avoid any use of group therapy techniques during the first year","C":"Care to avoid scheduling regular follow-up appointments that feel intrusive","D":"Care to avoid involving family members in any part of treatment planning","E":"Care to avoid ridiculing odd beliefs such as cult or religious practices"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The principles of treatment of schizotypal personality disorder do not differ from those of schizoid personality disorder, but clinicians must deal sensitively with schizotypal patients, mainly using care to avoid ridiculing or judging their odd beliefs, since these patients have peculiar patterns of thinking and some are involved in cults, strange religious practices, or the occult. Avoiding discussion of medication options entirely is not the described specific sensitivity; antipsychotic and antidepressant medications remain relevant treatment options for schizotypal personality disorder as needed. Avoiding group therapy techniques entirely is not described; group approaches remain useful across several Cluster A personality disorders, including schizoid personality disorder's treatment principles that schizotypal treatment mirrors. Avoiding regular follow-up appointments is not a described treatment principle for this disorder. Avoiding family involvement under any circumstances is not the specific sensitivity described; the framework specifically emphasizes sensitivity to the patient's odd beliefs themselves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schizotypal PD treatment mirrors schizoid PD's approach, with the added need for nonjudgmental sensitivity toward the patient's odd/magical beliefs specifically. COMLEX: A nonjudgmental stance toward unusual beliefs helps preserve the therapeutic alliance without either reinforcing or dismissively confronting them.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Defense Mechanisms in Cluster A & B Presentations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Avoiding family involvement un"}},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate psychotherapy approaches relevant to personality pathology, including DBT, CBT, psychodynamic therapy, mentalization-based treatment, and supportive therapy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with antisocial personality disorder is being evaluated for psychotherapy options. Given the limited evidence base for this population, which type of intervention appears to yield better response than standard cognitive behavioral therapy?","choices":{"A":"Transference-focused psychotherapy","B":"Contingency management programs","C":"Mentalization-based treatment","D":"Insight-oriented psychodynamic therapy","E":"Dialectical behavior therapy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"There is limited evidence to guide psychotherapeutic approaches in antisocial personality disorder, but the limited evidence available suggests these individuals respond better to contingency management and other reward-based interventions than to cognitive behavioral therapy specifically. Transference-focused psychotherapy is specifically described in the context of borderline personality disorder's object-relations-based treatment, not as the described better-response option for antisocial personality disorder. Mentalization-based treatment is likewise developed and evidenced for borderline personality disorder, not described as an antisocial-personality-disorder-specific alternative to CBT. Insight-oriented psychodynamic therapy alone is not the specific alternative described as outperforming CBT for antisocial personality disorder in this framework. Dialectical behavior therapy's standard four-mode structure is developed specifically for borderline personality disorder, not described as the antisocial-personality-disorder alternative to CBT here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antisocial personality disorder has a limited psychotherapy evidence base overall, but contingency management/reward-based approaches appear to outperform standard CBT — a useful distinguishing fact from borderline PD's richer DBT/MBT/TFP evidence base. COMLEX: This distinction matters clinically since applying a borderline-PD-specific modality (like DBT) to antisocial PD is not the evidence-supported first choice.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Defense Mechanisms in Cluster A & B Presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze when pharmacotherapy may be used symptom-targetedly for mood lability, impulsivity, aggression, anxiety, depression, or psychosis-like symptoms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with a personality disorder has emotional lability and rapid mood cycling. Per the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework, which class of medication is specifically listed as not recommended for this presentation due to a risk of triggering mania?","choices":{"A":"Lithium carbonate","B":"Lamotrigine","C":"Valproate","D":"Low-dose olanzapine","E":"Standard antidepressants"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For emotional lability and rapid cycling, the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework favors lithium, lamotrigine, valproate, or lower-dose antipsychotics such as olanzapine, aripiprazole, clozapine, or ziprasidone, while avoiding standard antidepressants because their mood-switching effect works against symptom stability, alongside tricyclic antidepressants' additional concern of \"catecholamine stress.\" Lithium has a long track record for emotional lability and rapid cycling. Lamotrigine is an anticonvulsant mood stabilizer effective for rapid mood cycling. Valproate is another anticonvulsant mood stabilizer used for the same target symptom. Lower-dose antipsychotics like olanzapine or aripiprazole are used adjunctively for this target symptom domain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emotional lability/rapid cycling = mood-stabilizer territory (lithium, lamotrigine, valproate, low-dose antipsychotics); standard antidepressants and TCAs are avoided due to mania-switching risk — mirrors the same principle used in bipolar spectrum pharmacotherapy generally. COMLEX: This target-symptom framework closely parallels standard bipolar disorder pharmacotherapy, since emotional lability crosses diagnostic boundaries.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — Comparative Mood Stabilizer & Antipsychotic Pharmacotherapy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze when pharmacotherapy may be used symptom-targetedly for mood lability, impulsivity, aggression, anxiety, depression, or psychosis-like symptoms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man with a personality disorder and a traumatic brain injury history has organic-like aggression. Per the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework, which class of medication is specifically listed as not recommended for this presentation due to a risk of disinhibition and delirium?","choices":{"A":"Benzodiazepines","B":"Beta-blockers","C":"Valproic acid","D":"Low-dose quetiapine","E":"Cholinesterase inhibitors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For organic-like aggression associated with traumatic brain injury, the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework favors beta-blockers, valproate, quetiapine, carbamazepine, tricyclic antidepressants, or cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil, while avoiding benzodiazepines because their disinhibiting effect risks worsening delirium and aggression in this specific organic population. Beta-blockers blunt the autonomic surge that drives organic-like aggression. Valproate is an anticonvulsant mood stabilizer also used for this presentation. Quetiapine is a low-dose antipsychotic option for organic-like aggression in this framework. Cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil are used for organic-like aggression in TBI or dementia-related presentations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Organic-like aggression (e.g., post-TBI) avoids benzodiazepines due to disinhibition/delirium risk, favoring beta-blockers, valproate, quetiapine, carbamazepine, or cholinesterase inhibitors instead. COMLEX: This mirrors general geriatric/organic-brain-injury psychopharmacology principles, where benzodiazepines are broadly avoided due to paradoxical disinhibition and delirium risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — Comparative Mood Stabilizer & Antipsychotic Pharmacotherapy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Beta-blockers blunt the autono","E":"Cholinesterase inhibitors such"}},{"id":"sdl29_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze when pharmacotherapy may be used symptom-targetedly for mood lability, impulsivity, aggression, anxiety, depression, or psychosis-like symptoms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with a personality disorder has ictal aggression associated with an abnormal EEG. Per the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework, which of the following is uniquely true about benzodiazepine use in this specific aggression subtype, compared to the other aggression subtypes?","choices":{"A":"Benzodiazepines are avoided in ictal aggression, as in every other aggression subtype","B":"Benzodiazepines are the sole effective treatment for ictal aggression","C":"Benzodiazepines are absolutely contraindicated in ictal aggression because they lower the seizure threshold","D":"Clonazepam is a recommended option in ictal aggression but is avoided in the other aggression subtypes","E":"Benzodiazepines carry a higher disinhibition risk in ictal aggression than in any other subtype"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For ictal aggression (abnormal EEG), the target-symptom pharmacotherapy framework recommends carbamazepine, diphenylhydantoin, valproate, or benzodiazepines such as clonazepam, while explicitly avoiding tricyclic antidepressants and low-potency typical antipsychotics due to their seizure-risk-increasing properties — making this the one aggression subtype where benzodiazepines are actually a recommended option, unlike affective, predatory, and organic-like aggression, where benzodiazepines are avoided due to disinhibition. Claiming benzodiazepines are avoided in every aggression subtype including this one contradicts the specific ictal-aggression recommendation. Benzodiazepines are not described as the only effective treatment for ictal aggression; carbamazepine, diphenylhydantoin, and valproate are also recommended options. Benzodiazepines are not contraindicated in ictal aggression; they are specifically recommended here, the opposite of contraindication. The disinhibition risk profile differs by context; in ictal aggression, clonazepam's anticonvulsant properties are specifically leveraged, unlike the disinhibition concern driving benzodiazepine avoidance in predatory aggression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ictal aggression (abnormal EEG) is the exception to the general \"avoid benzodiazepines for aggression\" rule, since clonazepam's anticonvulsant action directly addresses the seizure-related mechanism. COMLEX: Always confirm EEG findings before selecting an aggression-targeted agent, since the abnormal-versus-normal EEG distinction changes which drug classes are recommended versus avoided.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — Comparative Mood Stabilizer & Antipsychotic Pharmacotherapy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Claiming benzodiazepines are a","C":"Benzodiazepines are not contra"}},{"id":"sdl29_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret countertransference, splitting, dependency, boundary testing, and therapeutic rupture as clinical factors that affect care delivery.","batch":2,"stem":"A therapist treating a patient with borderline personality disorder observes the patient regressing quickly within sessions and displaying labile, difficult-to-analyze transferences toward the therapist. Which broader clinical challenge does this pattern represent, and what general therapeutic stance is recommended in response?","choices":{"A":"Rupture risk from regression and labile transference; a reality-oriented approach works better than deep interpretation","B":"Rupture risk from regression and labile transference; deep interpretation works better than a reality-oriented approach","C":"Clear treatment failure; therapy should be terminated and the patient referred elsewhere","D":"A need to switch to medication management with no further psychotherapy","E":"An expected, neutral finding that requires no change in the therapeutic approach"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Patients with borderline personality disorder regress quickly, act out their impulses, and show labile or fixed negative or positive transferences that are difficult to analyze, representing a genuine challenge (risk of therapeutic rupture) in the treatment relationship, and a reality-oriented approach is generally more effective than in-depth interpretations of the unconscious in managing this dynamic. Claiming deep unconscious interpretation works better than a reality-oriented approach gets the clinical guidance backward. This pattern does not represent clear treatment failure requiring immediate termination; it is a recognized, expected challenge in treating this population that calls for an adjusted therapeutic stance, not abandonment of treatment. Transitioning to medication-only management overlooks that psychotherapy alone can still address this specific rupture through an adapted technique, not elimination of psychotherapy altogether. This pattern is not therapeutically neutral; it specifically calls for the reality-oriented approach described, representing a meaningful adjustment to standard technique.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rapid regression and labile transference in borderline personality disorder treatment specifically call for a reality-oriented rather than deeply interpretive therapeutic stance, to reduce the risk of therapeutic rupture. COMLEX: Recognizing this pattern early allows the clinician to adjust technique proactively rather than reactively after a rupture has already occurred.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Transference/Countertransference & Unconscious Defense Mechanisms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Claiming deep unconscious inte","E":"This pattern is not therapeuti"}},{"id":"sdl29_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret countertransference, splitting, dependency, boundary testing, and therapeutic rupture as clinical factors that affect care delivery.","batch":2,"stem":"A therapist working with a patient with schizoid personality disorder in a group therapy setting notices the patient remaining silent for extended periods but observes that the patient continues attending sessions consistently. Which countertransference-related principle should guide the group facilitator's approach to this specific pattern?","choices":{"A":"Exclude the patient from the group because of the prolonged silence","B":"Allow other members to confront the patient about the silence to force engagement","C":"Replace the group with individual therapy, since silence shows the format has failed","D":"Protect the patient from attacks over the silence so the group can offer contact","E":"Treat the silence as a sign of active psychosis requiring a medication change"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patients with schizoid personality disorder in group therapy may remain silent for long periods yet still become involved over time, and they should be protected against aggressive attack by group members for their proclivity to be silent, since with time the group members become important to these patients and may provide their only social contact in an otherwise isolated existence. Excluding the patient entirely due to prolonged silence ignores that continued attendance itself reflects meaningful, gradual involvement, and directly contradicts the recommended protective stance. Allowing aggressive confrontation from other members is explicitly the risk the facilitator should protect against, not encourage. The patient's silence does not indicate the group format is inappropriate; group therapy specifically remains a described treatment option for schizoid personality disorder, with silence itself being an expected part of the process, not a reason to abandon it. Silence in this context is a described feature of schizoid personality disorder's group therapy participation, not a sign of active psychosis requiring urgent medication changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In group therapy for schizoid personality disorder, extended silence is expected and the facilitator's countertransference-informed task is protecting the silent patient from group members' frustration or aggression, not excluding them. COMLEX: Consistent attendance despite silence is itself a meaningful therapeutic sign for this population, reflecting gradual engagement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Transference/Countertransference & Unconscious Defense Mechanisms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The patient's silence does not"}},{"id":"sdl29_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret countertransference, splitting, dependency, boundary testing, and therapeutic rupture as clinical factors that affect care delivery.","batch":2,"stem":"A therapist working with a patient with narcissistic personality disorder notices strong feelings of irritation and a wish to challenge the patient's grandiose self-presentation directly and repeatedly during sessions. Which principle regarding countertransference should guide the therapist's handling of these feelings?","choices":{"A":"Act on the impulse and confront the patient's grandiosity directly and repeatedly in every session","B":"Recognize the feelings as countertransference and manage them reflectively without acting on them","C":"Suppress all awareness of these feelings and never reflect on them during or after sessions","D":"Transfer the patient to a different provider as soon as any irritation arises","E":"Share the irritation bluntly with the patient as the main therapeutic intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Recognizing one's own emotional reactions (countertransference) toward a patient, such as irritation with a narcissistic patient's grandiosity, and managing those feelings reflectively rather than acting on them impulsively is a core clinical skill that affects care delivery, allowing the therapist to respond therapeutically rather than reactively. Acting immediately and repeatedly on the impulse to confront the patient risks damaging the therapeutic alliance and turning the clinician's own countertransference into an unreflective enactment. Suppressing all awareness of these feelings entirely, rather than reflecting on them, forfeits the clinical value of countertransference as information about the treatment relationship. Transferring the patient immediately upon any irritation is an overreaction that abandons the opportunity to use reflective awareness therapeutically and disrupts continuity of care. Bluntly sharing irritated feelings directly with the patient as a primary intervention is not the recommended reflective approach; countertransference awareness is meant to inform the clinician's own conduct, not to be delivered as an unfiltered confrontation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Countertransference awareness (noticing one's own reactions to a patient) should inform reflective clinical judgment, not be acted out directly toward the patient — a general principle applicable across personality disorder presentations. COMLEX: Supervision and consultation are standard, recommended outlets for processing difficult countertransference reactions rather than acting on them within sessions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Transference/Countertransference & Unconscious Defense Mechanisms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Transferring the patient immed"}},{"id":"sdl29_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Evaluate suicide risk, violence risk, substance use, trauma history, and level-of-care needs in patients with personality disorder features.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with histrionic personality disorder presents to the emergency department reporting suicidal statements after a recent breakup, which the clinician notes have occurred repeatedly in the context of relationship conflicts. Which pattern should the clinician specifically consider when evaluating the function and safety implications of these statements?","choices":{"A":"Suicidal statements in histrionic personality disorder can be dismissed as attention-seeking without evaluation","B":"Suicidal statements in histrionic personality disorder never serve a coercive relational function","C":"Suicidal gestures may serve to coerce caregiving but still require careful individualized safety evaluation","D":"This recurring pattern establishes that the true diagnosis is borderline personality disorder","E":"Suicidal statements made during relationship conflict need not be taken seriously in any personality disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Complications of histrionic personality disorder may include frequent suicidal gestures and threats used to coerce better caregiving, which is important clinical context, but this described function does not mean such statements should be dismissed; they still require careful, individualized safety evaluation given the genuine risk that can accompany even function-driven suicidal communication. Automatically dismissing any suicidal statement as attention-seeking without evaluation is unsafe and ignores the genuine risk that can still be present. Suicidal statements in histrionic personality disorder are specifically described as sometimes serving this coercive relational function, so claiming no such association exists misstates the described pattern. This presentation does not require reclassifying the diagnosis as borderline personality disorder; histrionic personality disorder itself is specifically associated with this suicidal-gesture pattern, distinct from (though sometimes overlapping with) borderline personality disorder's presentation. Relationship-conflict-triggered suicidal statements should never be uniformly dismissed as never serious across any personality disorder; each presentation requires individualized safety evaluation regardless of the suspected underlying function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Histrionic personality disorder's described complications specifically include suicidal gestures/threats that may function to coerce caregiving — but recognizing this function does not eliminate the need for careful safety assessment. COMLEX: Distinguishing histrionic PD from borderline PD in this context relies on the broader symptom pattern (borderline PD adds identity diffusion and more frequent genuine psychotic-like episodes), not on the presence of suicidal statements alone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — Suicide & Violence Risk Factors for Acute Safety Planning","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Evaluate suicide risk, violence risk, substance use, trauma history, and level-of-care needs in patients with personality disorder features.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man with antisocial personality disorder is being evaluated for violence risk. His clinician notes his temperament profile of high novelty-seeking with low harm-avoidance. How does this temperament profile inform his violence risk assessment compared to a hypothetical patient with borderline personality disorder who has also committed a violent act?","choices":{"A":"This temperament is identical to the profile typically seen in borderline personality disorder criminality","B":"This temperament typifies antisocial criminality, while borderline violence pairs with high harm-avoidance","C":"Temperament has no established relationship to violence risk in either personality disorder","D":"Antisocial violence is typically linked to high harm-avoidance and low novelty-seeking","E":"Novelty-seeking is relevant to borderline violence risk but has no bearing in antisocial disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Individuals with antisocial personality disorder who commit crimes tend to display high novelty-seeking with low harm-avoidance, whereas individuals with borderline personality disorder who commit crimes tend to display high novelty-seeking with high harm-avoidance, a meaningful temperament distinction that can inform violence risk assessment and differential diagnosis in this context. Claiming the two profiles are identical directly contradicts this described distinction between high and low harm-avoidance in the two conditions. Temperament profile does have an established, described relationship to violence/criminality risk in both conditions, so claiming no relationship exists is incorrect. High harm-avoidance is specifically the borderline personality disorder-associated pattern, not the antisocial personality disorder pattern, which is characterized by low harm-avoidance instead. Novelty-seeking is relevant to both conditions' criminality-associated temperament profiles, not borderline personality disorder exclusively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antisocial PD criminality = high novelty-seeking + low harm-avoidance; borderline PD criminality = high novelty-seeking + high harm-avoidance — a subtle temperament-based distinction useful for violence risk differential assessment. COMLEX: This distinction should supplement, not replace, a comprehensive individualized violence risk assessment incorporating history, current stressors, and access to means.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — Suicide & Violence Risk Factors for Acute Safety Planning","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"High harm-avoidance is specifi"}},{"id":"sdl29_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Evaluate suicide risk, violence risk, substance use, trauma history, and level-of-care needs in patients with personality disorder features.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with borderline personality disorder presents with acute hallucinations, body image distortions, and ideas of reference following a significant interpersonal stressor. Which principle should guide the clinician's interpretation of these findings within a broader risk and level-of-care evaluation?","choices":{"A":"They are a stress-related borderline complication that warrants a broader safety evaluation","B":"They establish a primary psychotic disorder separate from her borderline personality disorder","C":"They are clinically insignificant and require no further risk evaluation","D":"They show that the borderline personality disorder diagnosis was incorrect from the start","E":"They carry no safety risk and should not influence her level-of-care determination"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Complications of borderline personality disorder may include psychotic-like symptoms such as hallucinations, body image distortions, hypnagogic phenomena, and ideas of reference in response to stress, alongside premature death or physical handicaps from suicide and suicidal gestures, failed suicide, and self-injurious behavior, meaning these findings should prompt a broader safety evaluation rather than being viewed only through a primary-psychotic-disorder lens. Automatically and exclusively attributing these findings to a primary psychotic disorder ignores their well-described relationship to borderline personality disorder's stress response specifically. Dismissing these findings as clinically insignificant ignores their documented association with elevated risk and complications in this population. These findings do not indicate a misdiagnosis; stress-related psychotic-like symptoms are a recognized, expected complication of borderline personality disorder itself, not evidence the original diagnosis was wrong. These findings do have an established relationship to broader risk and should meaningfully inform the level-of-care determination, given their association with the same complication profile that includes suicide risk and self-injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stress-related psychotic-like symptoms in borderline personality disorder (hallucinations, body image distortions, ideas of reference) are a recognized complication that should trigger comprehensive safety evaluation, not just symptom-focused psychiatric workup. COMLEX: These symptoms call for both target-symptom pharmacotherapy and a broader risk and level-of-care evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — Suicide & Violence Risk Factors for Acute Safety Planning","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"These findings do not indicate"}},{"id":"sdl29_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select patient-centered management strategies that maintain professionalism, ethics, continuity, and team-based consistency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with paranoid personality disorder is increasingly distrustful of a new resident physician rotating onto his care team, having previously built a trusting relationship with the prior resident. Which team-based continuity principle best addresses this transition?","choices":{"A":"The new resident should confront the patient's suspicions early to establish authority","B":"Brief the new resident on the established approach and let trust build without confrontation","C":"Provider transitions should be frequent so the patient does not attach to any one provider","D":"Each new provider should start fresh, without any handoff about the therapeutic approach","E":"The patient should be told that his trust in any individual provider is irrelevant to his care"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For paranoid personality disorder, continuity of the professional, non-confrontational treatment style is important, and incoming providers should be briefed on the established therapeutic approach so trust can be rebuilt gradually, since confrontation guarantees a lasting enemy and early termination of the interview, and trust with paranoid patients is difficult to establish. Immediately confronting the patient's suspicions to quickly establish authority directly risks the described damaging effect of confrontation with paranoid personality disorder patients. Frequent, deliberate provider transitions would work against, not support, the continuity this patient specifically needs given his suspiciousness and difficulty trusting new people. Providing no handoff information forces each new provider to relearn the same lessons about approach, unnecessarily risking a damaging confrontational misstep. Telling the patient his trust in a provider is clinically irrelevant contradicts the entire premise that a professional, alliance-building approach is central to this patient's care and risks further damaging the relationship.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Continuity-of-care principles for paranoid personality disorder specifically require deliberate handoff communication to preserve the established professional, non-confrontational approach across provider transitions. COMLEX: This is a concrete, team-based application of professionalism and ethics to a common real-world training/rotation scenario.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Confidentiality, Duty to Warn & Legal-Ethical Obligations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select patient-centered management strategies that maintain professionalism, ethics, continuity, and team-based consistency.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with antisocial personality disorder discloses during a session a specific, credible, and imminent plan to seriously harm a named individual. Which ethical principle should guide the clinician's next action, balancing professionalism with patient-centered care?","choices":{"A":"A duty to warn or protect the named person outweighs confidentiality given a credible, imminent threat","B":"Confidentiality is absolute and permits no exception under any circumstances","C":"The clinician should take no action, keeping the disclosure within the therapeutic relationship","D":"The clinician should terminate the therapeutic relationship without notifying anyone","E":"The disclosure should be handled like any other clinical information, with no special steps"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When a patient discloses a specific, credible, and imminent plan to seriously harm an identifiable third party, the clinician's affirmative legal and ethical duty to warn or protect that third party outweighs standard confidentiality protections, representing a specific, well-established exception to ordinary confidentiality that must guide professional action in this situation. Claiming confidentiality is absolute with no exceptions directly contradicts this well-established duty-to-warn exception. Taking no action at all fails the clinician's affirmative duty and could allow serious, preventable harm to occur. Immediately terminating the relationship without any further action or notification does not fulfill the duty to warn or protect the identified third party, which requires active steps, not simply ending the clinical relationship. Treating this disclosure identically to routine clinical information ignores its specific ethical and legal significance as a duty-to-warn trigger, which calls for a distinct, deliberate response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The duty to warn/protect an identifiable third party from serious, imminent harm is a well-established exception to standard confidentiality, directly relevant whenever violence risk assessment intersects with professional ethics. COMLEX: Specific legal requirements and thresholds for duty-to-warn vary by jurisdiction, but the underlying ethical principle of prioritizing prevention of serious imminent harm is broadly consistent.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Confidentiality, Duty to Warn & Legal-Ethical Obligations","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Taking no action at all fails ","D":"Immediately terminating the re"}},{"id":"sdl29_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select patient-centered management strategies that maintain professionalism, ethics, continuity, and team-based consistency.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with dependent personality disorder frequently requests that her case manager make decisions on her behalf regarding daily life matters unrelated to her clinical care. Which patient-centered, ethical response best balances professionalism with the treatment goal of fostering independence?","choices":{"A":"Make all such decisions for her indefinitely in order to reduce her immediate anxiety","B":"Refuse to engage with these requests, offering no explanation or support","C":"Decline to decide for her and support her own decision-making within professional boundaries","D":"Transfer decision-making authority to a family member without her input","E":"Make the decisions for her whenever she becomes visibly distressed about a choice"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Since the treatment of dependent personality disorder often successfully helps patients become more independent, assertive, and self-reliant through insight-oriented, behavioral, and supportive approaches, a case manager should decline to make unrelated daily-life decisions for the patient and instead support her in building her own decision-making skills, consistent with this broader treatment goal and maintaining appropriate professional boundaries. Making all such decisions for her indefinitely directly reinforces the dependency pattern the treatment is specifically designed to address, working against the patient's long-term interests. Refusing to engage at all, without explanation or support, is not patient-centered and abandons the therapeutic opportunity to support skill-building in a professional, structured way. Transferring decision-making authority to a family member without the patient's input undermines her autonomy and does not address the underlying dependency pattern within the treatment relationship. Selectively reinforcing the pattern only when the patient becomes distressed would inadvertently reward distress as a means of securing decision-making support, worsening rather than improving the dependency pattern over time.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ethical, patient-centered management of dependent personality disorder means declining to reinforce dependency while still providing structured, professional support toward the patient's own skill-building — a direct application of professionalism principles to daily clinical boundary-setting. COMLEX: This same boundary-maintaining principle applies across the care team, reinforcing why team-wide consistency matters.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Confidentiality, Duty to Warn & Legal-Ethical Obligations","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Refusing to engage at all, wit"}}],"30":[{"id":"sdl30_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical selection criteria and contraindications for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy models by evaluating patient-specific factors—including psychological mindedness, motivation for change, focal oedipal conflicts, and exclusion criteria —to determine appropriate candidate selection in a psychiatric encounter.","batch":1,"stem":"Sifneos's short-term anxiety-provoking psychotherapy selection criteria require, among other factors, that a patient's specific psychodynamic formulation center on which type of conflict?","choices":{"A":"An interpersonal conflict","B":"A diffuse identity-formation conflict","C":"A substance-use conflict","D":"An oedipal-focused conflict","E":"A somatic symptom conflict"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sifneos's selection criteria for short-term anxiety-provoking psychotherapy specifically require a specific psychodynamic formulation, usually a set of psychological conflicts underlying the patient's difficulties and centering on an oedipal focus, alongside a circumscribed chief complaint, one meaningful early-childhood relationship, flexible interaction with the evaluator, above-average psychological sophistication, and good motivation for change. An interpersonal conflict unrelated to oedipal-stage development is not the formulation Sifneos's criteria specify. A diffuse, chronic identity-formation conflict is not the specific formulation described in this framework. A conflict centered on active substance use is not part of Sifneos's selection criteria. A somatic symptom conflict is not the psychodynamic formulation Sifneos's criteria require.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sifneos's short-term anxiety-provoking psychotherapy specifically requires an oedipal-focused psychodynamic formulation as a selection criterion — a classic distinguishing detail among the brief psychodynamic models. COMLEX: This oedipal focus is echoed in the \"height of treatment\" phase, which emphasizes active concentration on the oedipal conflicts chosen as the therapeutic focus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — the same first-line psychotherapeutic treatment-selection framework, applied here to differentiating candidate-selection criteria across specific brief psychodynamic models","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl30_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical selection criteria and contraindications for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy models by evaluating patient-specific factors—including psychological mindedness, motivation for change, focal oedipal conflicts, and exclusion criteria —to determine appropriate candidate selection in a psychiatric encounter.","batch":1,"stem":"Per Malan's selection criteria for brief focal psychotherapy, which patient factor most reliably correlated with a successful treatment outcome?","choices":{"A":"Advanced patient age","B":"A high level of education","C":"High motivation for change","D":"Extensive prior psychiatric treatment","E":"Multiple prior hospitalizations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Malan found that high motivation invariably correlated with a successful outcome in brief focal psychotherapy, alongside his other selection criteria of eliminating absolute contraindications, assessing psychopathology, and determining the patient's capacity to consider problems in emotional terms, face disturbing material, and respond to interpretations. Advanced patient age is not the factor Malan identified as correlating with successful outcome. A high level of formal education is not the specific factor Malan's research identified. A long duration of prior psychiatric treatment is not the outcome-correlated factor described. A large number of prior hospitalizations is not the factor Malan's selection criteria emphasize.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Malan's brief focal psychotherapy research specifically found that high patient motivation, not age, education, or treatment history, invariably correlated with successful outcome. COMLEX: Malan's contraindications (serious suicide attempts, substance dependence, chronic alcohol abuse, incapacitating chronic obsessional or phobic symptoms, gross destructive acting out) are a useful companion fact set.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — the same first-line psychotherapeutic treatment-selection framework, applied here to differentiating candidate-selection criteria across specific brief psychodynamic models","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl30_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical selection criteria and contraindications for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy models by evaluating patient-specific factors—including psychological mindedness, motivation for change, focal oedipal conflicts, and exclusion criteria —to determine appropriate candidate selection in a psychiatric encounter.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is being evaluated for candidacy for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy. Which of the following would most strongly support that this patient is a suitable candidate?","choices":{"A":"The patient has extreme dishonesty and poor impulse control","B":"The patient is currently in the midst of an acute psychotic state","C":"The patient has incapacitating chronic obsessional symptoms","D":"The patient shows low motivation and wants rapid symptom relief","E":"The patient demonstrates strong psychological mindedness"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Across the brief psychodynamic models (Malan, Mann, Davanloo, Sifneos), psychological mindedness and the ability to respond to interpretation are consistently emphasized selection criteria supporting candidacy, since patients must be able to think abstractly and symbolically about the unconscious meanings of their behavior and engage productively with interpretive technique. Extreme dishonesty and poor impulse control are described contraindications, not supportive factors. An acute psychotic state is one of Mann's specific exceptions/rejection criteria for time-limited psychotherapy. Incapacitating chronic obsessional symptoms are a specific contraindication in Malan's criteria. Low motivation for change with only a desire for rapid symptomatic relief directly contradicts the high-motivation requirement common across these models.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psychological mindedness and responsiveness to interpretation are shared, cross-model selection criteria for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy candidacy. COMLEX: Recognizing shared contraindications (psychosis, poor impulse control, low motivation, severe obsessional/phobic symptoms) across Malan's and Mann's frameworks helps quickly rule out poor candidates in a clinical vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — the same first-line psychotherapeutic treatment-selection framework, applied here to differentiating candidate-selection criteria across specific brief psychodynamic models","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"An acute psychotic state is on"}},{"id":"sdl30_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate a Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) management plan for a patient with Borderline Personality Disorder and chronic parasuicidal behavior by prioritizing clinical targets according to the three-tiered treatment hierarchy (life-threatening -> treatment-interfering -> quality-of-life) and integrating its four delivery modes to replace distress-driven avoidance with adaptive coping skills.","batch":1,"stem":"In the DBT treatment hierarchy used to prioritize clinical targets, which category of behavior is addressed with the highest priority?","choices":{"A":"Life-threatening behavior","B":"Treatment-interfering behavior","C":"Quality-of-life-interfering behavior","D":"Mild emotional dysregulation","E":"Skills-training attendance issues"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DBT's treatment hierarchy prioritizes clinical targets in a specific order: self-injurious/life-threatening behavior first, followed by treatment-interfering behavior, then quality-of-life-interfering behavior, which is the same hierarchy the consultation team uses to discuss cases in their weekly meetings. Treatment-interfering behavior is addressed second, not first, in this hierarchy. Quality-of-life-interfering behavior is addressed third, the lowest of the three named tiers. Mild emotional dysregulation without self-harm is not one of the three named hierarchy tiers and would not take priority over life-threatening behavior. Skills-training attendance issues are not the top hierarchy priority.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DBT's three-tiered treatment hierarchy (life-threatening -> treatment-interfering -> quality-of-life) is the classic testable ordering principle for prioritizing clinical targets in BPD treatment. COMLEX: This same hierarchy structures the DBT consultation team's weekly case discussions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — extends the psychotherapy-modality differentiation framework (DBT vs CBT vs psychodynamic vs MBT vs TFP vs supportive) into formulating a full, hierarchy-driven DBT management plan","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Mild emotional dysregulation w"}},{"id":"sdl30_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate a Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) management plan for a patient with Borderline Personality Disorder and chronic parasuicidal behavior by prioritizing clinical targets according to the three-tiered treatment hierarchy (life-threatening -> treatment-interfering -> quality-of-life) and integrating its four delivery modes to replace distress-driven avoidance with adaptive coping skills.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is one of the four delivery modes of standard DBT treatment?","choices":{"A":"Family systems therapy sessions","B":"Inpatient milieu therapy","C":"Group psychoeducation lectures","D":"Telephone consultation","E":"Structured couples therapy sessions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The four modes of treatment in DBT are group skills training, individual therapy, telephone consultation, and the therapist consultation team; telephone consultation is available 24 hours a day so patients can call when heading toward a crisis that might lead to injurious behavior, with calls intended to be brief. Family systems therapy is not one of the four named DBT delivery modes. Inpatient milieu therapy is not one of the four modes, though hospitalization is listed as an ancillary treatment when needed. Group psychoeducation lectures are not DBT's group format; its group mode is skills training built on practice exercises. Structured couples therapy is not one of the four named DBT delivery modes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DBT's four delivery modes are group skills training, individual therapy, telephone consultation, and the therapist consultation team — a frequently tested, easily confused list. COMLEX: Telephone consultation calls are intended to be brief (about 10 minutes) and crisis-focused, distinguishing this mode from ongoing individual therapy sessions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — extends the psychotherapy-modality differentiation framework (DBT vs CBT vs psychodynamic vs MBT vs TFP vs supportive) into formulating a full, hierarchy-driven DBT management plan","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate a Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) management plan for a patient with Borderline Personality Disorder and chronic parasuicidal behavior by prioritizing clinical targets according to the three-tiered treatment hierarchy (life-threatening -> treatment-interfering -> quality-of-life) and integrating its four delivery modes to replace distress-driven avoidance with adaptive coping skills.","batch":1,"stem":"In the group skills training mode of DBT, which of the following best characterizes the format used?","choices":{"A":"Free-form unstructured group discussion of members' personal history","B":"Peer-led observations and feedback about other members' behavior","C":"A didactic approach using set exercises from a treatment manual","D":"Purely psychoeducational lectures given with no hands-on practice","E":"One-on-one exposure therapy sessions conducted within a group setting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In DBT group skills training, patients learn specific behavioral, emotional, cognitive, and interpersonal skills, and unlike traditional group therapy, observations about others in the group are discouraged; instead, DBT takes a didactic approach using specific exercises from a skills training manual, many of which emphasize control of emotional dysregulation and impulsive behavior. Free-form, unstructured discussion of personal history is explicitly not the DBT group format, which is didactic rather than open-ended. Peer-led observations and feedback about other members is specifically discouraged in DBT groups, unlike traditional group therapy. Purely psychoeducational lectures with no skills practice does not capture the exercise-based, skills-training nature of the format. One-on-one exposure therapy conducted in a group setting is not what DBT group skills training involves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DBT group skills training is didactic and manual-based, deliberately discouraging the interpersonal-observation focus of traditional group therapy — a key distinguishing feature. COMLEX: This didactic emphasis on emotional-dysregulation and impulsive-behavior control directly supports the broader DBT goal of replacing distress-driven avoidance with adaptive coping skills.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — extends the psychotherapy-modality differentiation framework (DBT vs CBT vs psychodynamic vs MBT vs TFP vs supportive) into formulating a full, hierarchy-driven DBT management plan","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Know clinical indications of biofeedback and applied tension by linking operant conditioning of autonomic functions (e.g., thermal or EMG biofeedback to reduce sympathetic tone) to stress-related somatic disorders, and isometric muscle contraction (applied tension) to counteracting the biphasic vasovagal response in blood/injection/injury phobia.","batch":1,"stem":"Biofeedback is based on the principle that the autonomic nervous system, normally involuntary, can come under voluntary control through which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Classical conditioning","B":"Systematic desensitization","C":"Flooding-based extinction","D":"Operant conditioning","E":"Participant modeling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Neal Miller demonstrated the medical potential of biofeedback by showing that the normally involuntary autonomic nervous system is susceptible to operant conditioning through the use of appropriate feedback, allowing patients to acquire information about involuntary biologic functions and learn to regulate them. Classical conditioning alone is not the mechanism biofeedback's theoretical basis relies on; the framework specifically describes operant conditioning. Systematic desensitization is a distinct behavioral technique using reciprocal inhibition, not the mechanism underlying biofeedback. Flooding-based extinction is a distinct exposure-based technique, not biofeedback's theoretical basis. Participant modeling is a separate technique involving learning by observation, not biofeedback's mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Biofeedback's theoretical basis is operant conditioning of autonomic functions (Neal Miller's foundational research) — a distinct mechanism from the classical-conditioning basis of systematic desensitization. COMLEX: Common biofeedback instruments include EMG (muscle electrical potentials), EEG (alpha waves), GSR (skin conductivity), and thermistors (skin temperature).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — extends the phobia/anxiety disorder differentiation framework to a specific conditioning-based treatment (biofeedback, applied tension) for the physiologic response underlying it","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Classical conditioning alone i"}},{"id":"sdl30_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Know clinical indications of biofeedback and applied tension by linking operant conditioning of autonomic functions (e.g., thermal or EMG biofeedback to reduce sympathetic tone) to stress-related somatic disorders, and isometric muscle contraction (applied tension) to counteracting the biphasic vasovagal response in blood/injection/injury phobia.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with blood/injection/injury phobia is being treated with applied tension. What is the underlying physiologic rationale for this technique?","choices":{"A":"Deep relaxation that lowers blood pressure","B":"Isometric muscle contraction raises blood pressure","C":"Cognitive restructuring of automatic thoughts","D":"Graded exposure without any muscle tension","E":"Biofeedback-guided peripheral thermal regulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patients with blood/injury phobia show a unique biphasic response — an initial increase in heart rate and blood pressure followed by a sudden drop in blood pressure that causes fainting — and applied tension counteracts this by having patients tense the muscles of the arms, legs, and torso, since this isometric tension raises blood pressure and prevents the fainting phase. Deep relaxation, which lowers rather than raises blood pressure, would be expected to worsen rather than prevent the fainting phase of this specific biphasic response. Cognitive restructuring of automatic thoughts is a cognitive therapy technique, not the mechanism applied tension relies on. Graded exposure alone, without any muscular tension component, is not how applied tension is described as working. Biofeedback-guided thermal regulation is a distinct technique, not the described mechanism of applied tension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Applied tension's mechanism (isometric muscle contraction raising blood pressure to prevent the second, fainting phase of the biphasic vasovagal response) is a classic, distinctive fact for blood/injection/injury phobia, unlike other phobia treatments that use relaxation. COMLEX: Patients learn to recognize early warning signs of fainting (queasiness, cold sweats, dizziness) and apply the tension technique contingent on these signs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — extends the phobia/anxiety disorder differentiation framework to a specific conditioning-based treatment (biofeedback, applied tension) for the physiologic response underlying it","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl30_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Know clinical indications of biofeedback and applied tension by linking operant conditioning of autonomic functions (e.g., thermal or EMG biofeedback to reduce sympathetic tone) to stress-related somatic disorders, and isometric muscle contraction (applied tension) to counteracting the biphasic vasovagal response in blood/injection/injury phobia.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with bruxism is treated with EMG biofeedback attached to the masseter muscle. What clinical indication category does this application of biofeedback best represent?","choices":{"A":"Operant-conditioning-based treatment of a stress-related somatic disorder","B":"A psychodynamic treatment targeting an unconscious oedipal conflict in origin","C":"An exposure-based treatment using in vivo response prevention methods","D":"A cognitive restructuring intervention targeting automatic thoughts directly","E":"A DBT skills-training intervention for emotional dysregulation symptoms"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EMG biofeedback attached to the masseter muscle in bruxism is a classic example of biofeedback's clinical application, linking operant conditioning of an autonomic/muscular function (muscle tension) to a stress-related somatic disorder, where the EMG emits a tone that reinforces learning and the condition ameliorates as the patient learns to alter the tone to indicate relaxation. This is not a psychodynamic treatment of an unconscious oedipal conflict, which describes brief psychodynamic psychotherapy models instead. It is not an exposure-based behavioral treatment using response prevention, which describes flooding/ERP rather than biofeedback. It is not a cognitive restructuring intervention targeting automatic thoughts, which describes cognitive therapy technique instead. It is not a DBT skills-training intervention, which is a distinct psychosocial treatment modality for BPD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EMG biofeedback for bruxism is a textbook example of biofeedback's operant-conditioning-based clinical indication for stress-related somatic disorders. COMLEX: Recognizing biofeedback's mechanism (operant conditioning of autonomic/muscular function) helps distinguish it from unrelated conditioning-based techniques like systematic desensitization or flooding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — extends the phobia/anxiety disorder differentiation framework to a specific conditioning-based treatment (biofeedback, applied tension) for the physiologic response underlying it","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl30_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze cognitive distortions and underlying core schemas in Major Depressive Disorder by identifying Beck's cognitive triad (negative thoughts about the self, the world, and the future) and evaluating automatic thought records to guide cognitive restructuring during therapy sessions.","batch":1,"stem":"Per Beck's cognitive triad, negative thoughts characteristic of depression are organized around which three domains?","choices":{"A":"The past, the present, and the future","B":"The self, personal relationships, and household finances","C":"The self, the world, and the future","D":"The body, the mind, and interpersonal relationships","E":"Family, work, and personal health"},"correct":"C","explanation":"From a cognitive perspective, depression can be explained by the cognitive triad, which explains that negative thoughts are about the self, the world, and the future, and this triad underlies apathy, low energy, pessimism, and hopelessness in depressed patients. The past, the present, and the future is not the specific triad Beck described; the triad centers on the self, the world, and the future rather than temporal domains. The self, relationships, and finances is not the specific domain set Beck's triad names. The body, the mind, and relationships is not the cognitive triad as described. Family, work, and health is not Beck's named triad, though negative thoughts in these areas may be examples that fall under the broader \"world\" domain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beck's cognitive triad (negative views of self, world, and future) is one of the most frequently tested facts about cognitive theory of depression. COMLEX: The cognitive triad directly explains depressive symptoms — apathy/low energy from expectation of failure, paralysis of will from pessimism and hopelessness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 5 — extends the CBT/IPT/family therapy core-mechanism comparison framework for MDD into the specific cognitive triad and automatic-thought-record techniques CBT uses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The past, the present, and the"}},{"id":"sdl30_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze cognitive distortions and underlying core schemas in Major Depressive Disorder by identifying Beck's cognitive triad (negative thoughts about the self, the world, and the future) and evaluating automatic thought records to guide cognitive restructuring during therapy sessions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient in cognitive therapy for depression is asked to record situations, automatic thoughts, and emotional reactions between sessions. Which cognitive-technique process does this exercise primarily support?","choices":{"A":"Testing maladaptive underlying assumptions","B":"Eliciting the patient's spontaneous negative cognitions","C":"Constructing a graded anxiety hierarchy","D":"Formulating a DBT treatment-target hierarchy","E":"Keeping a behavioral-activation activity diary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The automatic thought record is the tool used in the \"eliciting automatic thoughts\" step of cognitive therapy's four cognitive-technique processes (eliciting automatic thoughts, testing automatic thoughts, identifying maladaptive underlying assumptions, and testing the validity of maladaptive assumptions), capturing cognitions that intervene between external events and a patient's emotional reaction. Testing the validity of maladaptive underlying assumptions is a later, distinct step in the cognitive-technique sequence, not this initial recording exercise. Constructing a graded anxiety hierarchy is part of systematic desensitization, an unrelated behavioral technique. Formulating a DBT treatment-target hierarchy is part of DBT's target-prioritization framework, not cognitive therapy's automatic thought record. Conducting a behavioral-activation daily activity diary is a distinct technique, not the automatic thought record.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The automatic thought record supports the \"eliciting automatic thoughts\" step, the first of cognitive therapy's four cognitive-technique processes. COMLEX: Distinguishing the automatic thought record (cognitive therapy) from the activity/self-monitoring diary (behavioral activation) is a useful discrimination for board vignettes describing between-session homework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 5 — extends the CBT/IPT/family therapy core-mechanism comparison framework for MDD into the specific cognitive triad and automatic-thought-record techniques CBT uses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Testing the validity of malada"}},{"id":"sdl30_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze cognitive distortions and underlying core schemas in Major Depressive Disorder by identifying Beck's cognitive triad (negative thoughts about the self, the world, and the future) and evaluating automatic thought records to guide cognitive restructuring during therapy sessions.","batch":1,"stem":"A depressed patient tells her cognitive therapist, \"If anyone doesn't like me, I'm not lovable.\" Which cognitive-therapy concept does this statement best represent?","choices":{"A":"An automatic thought triggered by one single specific event","B":"A component of the DBT treatment hierarchy","C":"An example of applied tension for blood/injury phobia","D":"A finding recorded during a biofeedback session","E":"A maladaptive underlying assumption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"As automatic thoughts are identified, patterns become apparent representing rules or maladaptive general assumptions that guide a patient's life, and \"If anyone doesn't like me, I'm not lovable\" is given as exactly this type of example, distinct from a situation-specific automatic thought. An automatic thought triggered by a single specific event is the earlier, more situation-bound cognition (e.g., \"she doesn't like me\" after being passed in the hall), not the broader guiding rule this statement represents. A component of the DBT treatment hierarchy is unrelated to cognitive therapy's assumption-identification process. An example of applied tension is unrelated to this cognitive-therapy concept entirely. A finding from a biofeedback session is unrelated to cognitive therapy's automatic-thought and assumption framework.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maladaptive underlying assumptions are broader, rule-like beliefs (e.g., \"In order to be happy, I must be perfect\") distinct from situation-specific automatic thoughts — a key conceptual distinction in cognitive therapy. COMLEX: These maladaptive assumptions are identified as patterns emerge across multiple automatic thoughts recorded over time.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 5 — extends the CBT/IPT/family therapy core-mechanism comparison framework for MDD into the specific cognitive triad and automatic-thought-record techniques CBT uses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Select the appropriate conditioning-based behavioral technique—such as systematic desensitization (reciprocal inhibition), in vivo flooding with response prevention, or behavioral activation—based on a patient's specific presentation (e.g., phobias, obsessive-compulsive rituals, or depression-induced behavioral withdrawal).","batch":1,"stem":"Systematic desensitization relies on a relaxed physiologic state to inhibit the anxiety response elicited by a graded hierarchy of feared stimuli. What is this specific behavioral principle called?","choices":{"A":"Operant conditioning","B":"Reciprocal inhibition","C":"Response prevention","D":"Participant modeling","E":"Behavioral activation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The foundation of systematic desensitization is the behavioral principle of counterconditioning, whereby a relaxed psychophysiological state inhibits the negative reaction of anxiety, a process specifically called reciprocal inhibition, and treatment proceeds through relaxation training, hierarchy construction, and systematic desensitization of the stimulus. Operant conditioning is the mechanism underlying biofeedback, not the specific counterconditioning principle used in systematic desensitization. Response prevention is a distinct technique paired with flooding/exposure, not the mechanism of systematic desensitization. Participant modeling is a separate technique involving learning by observation, not reciprocal inhibition. Behavioral activation is a distinct technique for depression involving structured activity engagement, unrelated to reciprocal inhibition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reciprocal inhibition (a relaxed state inhibiting the anxiety response) is the specific counterconditioning principle underlying systematic desensitization, developed by Wolpe. COMLEX: Systematic desensitization's three steps (relaxation training, hierarchy construction, desensitization of the stimulus) are a classic testable sequence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 4 — extends the ERP-based step-wise OCD treatment framework to the broader family of conditioning-based behavioral techniques and their disorder-specific indications","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Participant modeling is a sepa","E":"Behavioral activation is a dis"}},{"id":"sdl30_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Select the appropriate conditioning-based behavioral technique—such as systematic desensitization (reciprocal inhibition), in vivo flooding with response prevention, or behavioral activation—based on a patient's specific presentation (e.g., phobias, obsessive-compulsive rituals, or depression-induced behavioral withdrawal).","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a specific phobia refuses gradual exposure and instead undergoes a single prolonged session of direct, full-intensity in vivo exposure to the feared stimulus without a hierarchy or relaxation training, remaining until the fear subsides. Which technique is being used?","choices":{"A":"Sustained flooding","B":"Systematic desensitization","C":"Applied tension","D":"Behavioral activation","E":"Autogenic training"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Flooding (also called implosion) involves exposing the patient to the feared object in vivo without a hierarchy and without relaxation exercises, based on the premise that escaping from an anxiety-provoking experience reinforces the anxiety through conditioning; success depends on the patient remaining in the fear-generating situation until calm, and prematurely withdrawing reinforces both conditioned anxiety and avoidance behavior. Systematic desensitization uses a graded hierarchy paired with relaxation training, which this scenario lacks entirely. Applied tension's isometric muscle contraction prevents fainting in blood/injury phobia rather than treating full-intensity exposure without a hierarchy. Behavioral activation is a technique for depression involving structured activity engagement, not exposure to a feared stimulus. Autogenic training is a relaxation method using self-suggestion, not an exposure-based technique.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Flooding is distinguished from systematic desensitization by its lack of a graded hierarchy and lack of relaxation training, relying instead on prolonged, direct exposure until fear subsides. COMLEX: Flooding is often paired with response prevention, and exposure and response prevention (ERP) together is the treatment of choice for OCD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 4 — extends the ERP-based step-wise OCD treatment framework to the broader family of conditioning-based behavioral techniques and their disorder-specific indications","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Select the appropriate conditioning-based behavioral technique—such as systematic desensitization (reciprocal inhibition), in vivo flooding with response prevention, or behavioral activation—based on a patient's specific presentation (e.g., phobias, obsessive-compulsive rituals, or depression-induced behavioral withdrawal).","batch":1,"stem":"A retired, severely depressed patient with behavioral withdrawal is treated with an approach that begins with a self-monitoring diary of daily activities, then builds a hierarchy of valued activities from easiest to most difficult for the patient to gradually reengage in. Which technique is this?","choices":{"A":"Systematic desensitization","B":"Flooding with response prevention","C":"Applied tension for blood/injury phobia","D":"Biofeedback-assisted relaxation","E":"Behavioral activation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Behavioral activation emphasizes structured attempts to increase overt behaviors that bring patients into contact with reinforcing environmental contingencies, beginning with a self-monitoring/daily diary exercise to establish baseline activities, then building an activity hierarchy from easiest to most difficult that patients progressively move through, addressing depression-induced withdrawal. Systematic desensitization addresses anxious avoidance of specific feared stimuli using relaxation and a fear hierarchy, not depression-related withdrawal. Flooding with response prevention is an anxiety/OCD-focused exposure technique, not this depression-focused activity-reengagement approach. Applied tension addresses blood/injury phobia's biphasic vasovagal response, unrelated to this scenario. Biofeedback-assisted relaxation targets autonomic/muscular physiologic parameters, not structured activity reengagement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Behavioral activation directly targets depression-induced behavioral withdrawal using a self-monitoring diary and an easiest-to-most-difficult activity hierarchy — distinct from the anxiety-hierarchy-based approach of systematic desensitization. COMLEX: This technique is grounded in the theory that depression persists partly because reinforcement for healthy behavior is low while depressed behavior may be positively or negatively reinforced.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 4 — extends the ERP-based step-wise OCD treatment framework to the broader family of conditioning-based behavioral techniques and their disorder-specific indications","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical selection criteria and contraindications for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy models by evaluating patient-specific factors—including psychological mindedness, motivation for change, focal oedipal conflicts, and exclusion criteria —to determine appropriate candidate selection in a psychiatric encounter.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman presents for evaluation for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy. She has a circumscribed chief complaint about recurring conflict with authority figures, describes one meaningful, give-and-take relationship with her grandmother during childhood, interacts flexibly with the evaluator, demonstrates above-average psychological sophistication, and expresses strong, sustained motivation for genuine change rather than just symptom relief. Her difficulties center on unresolved conflict traceable to an early relationship with her father. Which brief psychodynamic model's selection criteria does this presentation most closely match?","choices":{"A":"Sifneos's anxiety-provoking psychotherapy","B":"Malan's brief focal psychotherapy","C":"Mann's time-limited psychotherapy","D":"Davanloo's short-term dynamic psychotherapy","E":"Standard unmodified psychoanalysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation maps directly onto Sifneos's criteria: a circumscribed chief complaint, one meaningful early-childhood relationship, flexible interaction with an evaluator, above-average psychological sophistication, a specific psychodynamic formulation centering on an oedipal focus (here, the father relationship), and good motivation for change rather than mere symptom relief. Malan's criteria emphasize eliminating absolute contraindications and assessing capacity to tolerate treatment stress rather than this specific combination of features, and do not require an oedipal-focused formulation as explicitly. Mann's time-limited psychotherapy is less explicit about selection/rejection criteria and centers on determining a patient's central conflict and maturational crises rather than this oedipal-focus emphasis. Davanloo's model does classify patients by oedipal versus non-oedipal conflicts, but this scenario's oedipal-focused formulation, combined with the other Sifneos-specific criteria (circumscribed complaint, one meaningful relationship), matches Sifneos's model more precisely. Standard psychoanalysis is a much longer-term treatment, not a brief, time-limited model matching this scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing Sifneos's specific combination of criteria (circumscribed complaint, one meaningful relationship, oedipal focus, high motivation) versus Malan's or Mann's differently emphasized criteria is a classic higher-order vignette discrimination. COMLEX: This oedipal-focused presentation would proceed through Sifneos's four phases: patient-therapist encounter, early therapy, height of treatment, and termination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — First-Line Psychotherapeutic & Behavioral Anxiety Treatment Options","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Davanloo's model does classify"}},{"id":"sdl30_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical selection criteria and contraindications for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy models by evaluating patient-specific factors—including psychological mindedness, motivation for change, focal oedipal conflicts, and exclusion criteria —to determine appropriate candidate selection in a psychiatric encounter.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a long history of extreme dishonesty, poor impulse control, and low motivation to understand himself is referred for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy. He denies any significant suffering and states he only wants his symptoms to go away immediately. Which principle best explains why he is a poor candidate for this treatment?","choices":{"A":"His age in the mid-40s places him outside the range suitable for brief psychotherapy","B":"Dishonesty and poor impulse control along with weak motivation for insight are contraindications","C":"Brief psychodynamic psychotherapy has no contraindications, so he should be accepted","D":"A formal psychotic disorder is the sole factor that can disqualify a candidate","E":"His desire for rapid symptom relief strengthens his candidacy for brief treatment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The absence of suffering, poor impulse control, inability to tolerate frustration and anxiety, low motivation to understand, and extreme dishonesty are all described contraindications for psychodynamic treatment, and this patient exhibits nearly all of these features, along with wanting only rapid symptomatic relief rather than genuine self-understanding, which itself is described as disqualifying (patients must have a genuine wish to understand themselves, not a desperate hunger for symptomatic relief). Age alone does not automatically disqualify a candidate; analysts recognize patients remain analyzable well into their 60s or 70s. Brief psychodynamic psychotherapy does have well-described contraindications, so claiming none exist is incorrect. Formal psychosis is one contraindication among several (dishonesty, poor impulse control, low motivation, absence of suffering), not the only disqualifying factor. Desiring rapid symptom relief over genuine self-understanding is specifically described as working against, not strengthening, candidacy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Contraindications for psychodynamic treatment (absence of suffering, poor impulse control, low motivation, extreme dishonesty, wanting only rapid relief) commonly appear together in a single vignette to test recognition of a poor candidate. COMLEX: Antisocial personality disorder is specifically named as contraindicating analytic treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — First-Line Psychotherapeutic & Behavioral Anxiety Treatment Options","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Age alone does not automatical","C":"Brief psychodynamic psychother"}},{"id":"sdl30_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical selection criteria and contraindications for brief psychodynamic psychotherapy models by evaluating patient-specific factors—including psychological mindedness, motivation for change, focal oedipal conflicts, and exclusion criteria —to determine appropriate candidate selection in a psychiatric encounter.","batch":2,"stem":"A therapist practicing Davanloo's short-term dynamic psychotherapy notices the patient becoming increasingly dependent on the relationship and beginning to regress during sessions. According to Davanloo's described technique, how should the therapist respond?","choices":{"A":"Passively allow the dependence and regression to continue unaddressed across the remaining sessions","B":"Immediately terminate treatment at the very first sign of any dependence on the therapist","C":"Actively intervene to avoid the patient's overdependence while remaining flexible to the patient's needs","D":"Switch over to Mann's time-limited model mid-treatment without discussing the change with the patient","E":"Ignore the regressive tendencies and let the patient set the pace of the sessions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The highlights of Davanloo's psychotherapeutic approach specifically include flexibility (adapting technique to the patient's needs), control of the patient's regressive tendencies, and active intervention to avoid having the patient develop overdependence on the therapist, alongside using the patient's intellectual insight and emotional experiences in the transference. Passively allowing dependence and regression to continue unaddressed directly contradicts Davanloo's described emphasis on active intervention and control of regressive tendencies. Immediately terminating treatment at the first sign of dependence is an overly abrupt response not described in Davanloo's technique, which instead calls for active management within the ongoing treatment. Switching models mid-treatment without further discussion is not a described element of Davanloo's approach. Claiming Davanloo's technique does not address regressive tendencies directly contradicts the explicit description of this as one of the approach's highlighted features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Davanloo's technique explicitly names flexibility, control of regressive tendencies, and active intervention against therapist overdependence as core highlights — testable as a set. COMLEX: This active management of dependence contrasts with the more passive, exploratory stance sometimes associated with longer-term psychoanalytic technique.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — First-Line Psychotherapeutic & Behavioral Anxiety Treatment Options","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Claiming Davanloo's technique "}},{"id":"sdl30_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate a Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) management plan for a patient with Borderline Personality Disorder and chronic parasuicidal behavior by prioritizing clinical targets according to the three-tiered treatment hierarchy (life-threatening -> treatment-interfering -> quality-of-life) and integrating its four delivery modes to replace distress-driven avoidance with adaptive coping skills.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with borderline personality disorder has just started DBT. She asks her individual therapist when she is supposed to use the telephone consultation that is part of her treatment and what a call should look like. Which description best matches the role of phone consultation in standard DBT?","choices":{"A":"A brief call when she feels a crisis building, before acting on urges","B":"A full-length phone session after any self-harm, in place of a visit","C":"A call during office hours that is used to book extra appointments","D":"A weekly call to review her diary cards in place of individual therapy","E":"A call to discuss other group members' behavior outside skills group"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock describe DBT telephone consultation as available 24 hours a day; patients are encouraged to call when they feel themselves heading toward a crisis that might lead to injurious behavior, and calls are intended to be brief, usually about 10 minutes, so she should call early, before acting, for short coaching. Long phone sessions after self-harm reverse this purpose, which is to apply skills before injurious behavior occurs, and do not replace individual therapy. Coaching is not restricted to office hours or to scheduling. Diary cards are reviewed in the weekly 50- to 60-minute individual session, not by phone. Comments about other group members are discouraged in DBT skills training and are not the purpose of phone contact.","boardPrep":"Step 1: standard DBT has four modes: group skills training, individual therapy, telephone consultation, and a therapist consultation team; targets follow the hierarchy of life-threatening, then treatment-interfering, then quality-of-life-interfering behavior. COMLEX: DBT has the strongest evidence among psychotherapies for borderline personality disorder with recurrent self-harm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — DBT among psychotherapy approaches for personality pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate a Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) management plan for a patient with Borderline Personality Disorder and chronic parasuicidal behavior by prioritizing clinical targets according to the three-tiered treatment hierarchy (life-threatening -> treatment-interfering -> quality-of-life) and integrating its four delivery modes to replace distress-driven avoidance with adaptive coping skills.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient in DBT frequently arrives late, is minimally engaged during individual sessions, and has stopped completing her diary cards, though she has had no recent self-harm. Per the DBT treatment hierarchy, how should this pattern of behavior be classified and addressed relative to her overall treatment plan?","choices":{"A":"As life-threatening behavior","B":"As quality-of-life-interfering behavior","C":"As behavior outside the DBT hierarchy","D":"As treatment-interfering behavior","E":"As a sign DBT should be discontinued"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lateness, disengagement, and non-adherence with diary cards are classic examples of treatment-interfering behavior, the second tier of DBT's hierarchy, addressed after ruling out life-threatening/self-injurious behavior (which is absent here) but before quality-of-life-interfering behavior. This behavior is not life-threatening behavior, since there is no self-harm or safety risk described. It is also not quality-of-life-interfering behavior, which is the lowest tier addressed only after treatment-interfering behavior is managed. This behavior is explicitly part of the DBT hierarchy (as treatment-interfering behavior), not something to be ignored. Treatment-interfering behavior is exactly the kind of pattern DBT is designed to actively address, not a sign DBT should be discontinued.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Behaviors that disrupt the treatment process itself (lateness, disengagement, non-adherence) without being directly self-injurious are the hallmark of treatment-interfering behavior, DBT's second-tier hierarchy target. COMLEX: The consultation team format specifically exists to help therapists manage exactly this kind of treatment-interfering pattern without burnout.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — extends the psychotherapy-modality differentiation framework (DBT vs CBT vs psychodynamic vs MBT vs TFP vs supportive) into formulating a full, hierarchy-driven DBT management plan","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"It is also not quality-of-life","C":"This behavior is explicitly pa","E":"Treatment-interfering behavior"}},{"id":"sdl30_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate a Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) management plan for a patient with Borderline Personality Disorder and chronic parasuicidal behavior by prioritizing clinical targets according to the three-tiered treatment hierarchy (life-threatening -> treatment-interfering -> quality-of-life) and integrating its four delivery modes to replace distress-driven avoidance with adaptive coping skills.","batch":2,"stem":"A DBT individual therapist is frustrated that the skills-group leader handled her patient's outburst more permissively than she would have. At the weekly consultation team meeting she proposes calling the group leader to tell him exactly how to respond to the patient from now on. According to the DBT consultation team agreements, which approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Telling the group leader exactly how he must respond to this patient","B":"Requiring every therapist to respond to the patient in the same way","C":"Telling the patient that the group leader made a clinical mistake","D":"Pausing the patient's group skills training until the leaders agree","E":"Coaching the patient on dealing with him while accepting differences"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock list the DBT consultation team agreements, which include consulting with the patient on how to interact with other therapists rather than telling other therapists how to interact with the patient, and not expecting therapists to be consistent with one another, even with the same patient; the team also searches for nonpejorative interpretations and accepts that all therapists are fallible. Coaching the patient while accepting the leader's different style follows these agreements. Directing the group leader's responses breaks the consult-with-the-patient agreement. Requiring identical responses contradicts the agreement not to expect consistency. Criticizing a colleague to the patient is pejorative and undermines treatment. Removing the patient from skills training interrupts a core mode of treatment over a difference between therapists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the DBT consultation team meets weekly for 1 to 2 hours, reviews cases by the treatment hierarchy, and supports therapist motivation and skill. COMLEX: in team-based care of borderline personality disorder, splitting between staff is addressed in team meetings rather than by one clinician directing another.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — splitting and countertransference as clinical data in personality disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Directing the group leader's r","B":"Requiring identical responses ","C":"Criticizing a colleague to the"}},{"id":"sdl30_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Know clinical indications of biofeedback and applied tension by linking operant conditioning of autonomic functions (e.g., thermal or EMG biofeedback to reduce sympathetic tone) to stress-related somatic disorders, and isometric muscle contraction (applied tension) to counteracting the biphasic vasovagal response in blood/injection/injury phobia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with a severe blood/injection/injury phobia needs to donate blood for a medical procedure. Her clinician teaches her to tense the muscles of her arms, legs, and torso for 10 to 15 seconds, repeated five times, and to apply this maneuver upon noticing early warning signs such as queasiness or cold sweats. What is the most likely goal of this specific intervention?","choices":{"A":"Raising blood pressure via isometric muscle tension to prevent the fainting phase of her biphasic vasovagal response","B":"Lowering her heart rate through deep relaxation to prevent the initial phase of her vasovagal response","C":"Extinguishing her conditioned fear through prolonged hierarchy-free exposure to blood-related stimuli in the clinic","D":"Restructuring her automatic thoughts about blood and needles through weekly cognitive-restructuring techniques","E":"Training her autonomic nervous system through operant-conditioning-based physiologic instrument feedback"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This describes applied tension, in which isometric muscle contraction of the arms, legs, and torso raises blood pressure, and patients are taught to recognize early warning signs of fainting (queasiness, cold sweats, dizziness) and apply the technique contingent on these signs, preventing the fainting phase of the biphasic vasovagal response characteristic of blood/injury phobia. Lowering heart rate through deep relaxation would work against, not support, blood pressure maintenance and could worsen rather than prevent the fainting phase. Prolonged, hierarchy-free exposure describes flooding, a distinct technique not involving isometric muscle tension. Restructuring automatic thoughts describes cognitive therapy technique, not the physiologic mechanism of applied tension. Training the autonomic nervous system via instrument feedback describes biofeedback, a related but distinct conditioning-based technique that does not use isometric muscle tension in this manner.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Applied tension's specific mechanism (isometric tension raising blood pressure to prevent the fainting phase) distinguishes it from every other technique that instead relies on relaxation to reduce anxiety/blood pressure. COMLEX: Patients can also perform applied tension while actually donating blood or watching a surgical procedure, as in this vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Phobia & Anxiety Disorder Differentiation by Trigger and Timeline","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl30_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Know clinical indications of biofeedback and applied tension by linking operant conditioning of autonomic functions (e.g., thermal or EMG biofeedback to reduce sympathetic tone) to stress-related somatic disorders, and isometric muscle contraction (applied tension) to counteracting the biphasic vasovagal response in blood/injection/injury phobia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with recurrent migraine is treated with biofeedback. A thermistor is taped to her fingertip, and a tone rises in pitch as the measured value increases; she is told to practice making the pitch rise. Which physiologic change is she learning to produce, and how is it thought to reduce her migraines?","choices":{"A":"Raising blood pressure with isometric tension to prevent fainting","B":"Increasing skin conductance on the galvanic skin response gauge","C":"Lowering frontalis muscle tension on an electromyogram display","D":"Increasing alpha waves on an electroencephalogram recording","E":"Warming her hands by lowering sympathetic vasoconstriction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock explain that biofeedback uses operant conditioning to bring autonomic functions under voluntary control and give the example that a person can learn to raise the temperature of the hands to reduce the frequency of migraines; the thermistor measures skin temperature, which drops during tension because of peripheral vasoconstriction, so warming reflects lower sympathetic activation and self-regulation of arterial smooth muscle tone. Isometric tension that raises blood pressure is applied tension for blood-injection-injury phobia, not biofeedback. The galvanic skin response gauge shows decreased, not increased, skin conductivity during relaxation and is a different instrument. Frontalis EMG and EEG alpha-wave feedback are other biofeedback methods, but the device here is a thermistor measuring temperature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: biofeedback instruments include EMG (muscle tension, bruxism, tension headache), EEG (alpha waves in relaxation), galvanic skin response (conductance falls with relaxation), and thermistor (hand warming for migraine and Raynaud-type vasospasm). COMLEX: biofeedback can be combined with progressive relaxation and also aids pelvic floor retraining for urinary incontinence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 5 — conditioning-based behavioral techniques such as systematic desensitization","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Frontalis EMG and EEG alpha-wa","D":"Frontalis EMG and EEG alpha-wa"}},{"id":"sdl30_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Know clinical indications of biofeedback and applied tension by linking operant conditioning of autonomic functions (e.g., thermal or EMG biofeedback to reduce sympathetic tone) to stress-related somatic disorders, and isometric muscle contraction (applied tension) to counteracting the biphasic vasovagal response in blood/injection/injury phobia.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient successfully practices applied tension for blood/injury phobia but develops a headache as an adverse effect. According to the described protocol, what should be adjusted?","choices":{"A":"Discontinue treatment permanently","B":"Switch to systematic desensitization","C":"Increase the number of daily practice sessions","D":"Reduce the intensity and frequency","E":"No adjustment is needed"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An adverse effect of applied tension treatment that sometimes develops is headache, and in this case, the described protocol specifies that the patient should reduce the intensity of the muscle contraction and the frequency of treatment, rather than stopping altogether or continuing unchanged. Immediately and permanently discontinuing treatment with no further attempts is a more drastic response than the described protocol, which calls for adjusting intensity/frequency rather than stopping entirely. Switching entirely to systematic desensitization is not the described response to this specific adverse effect within the applied tension protocol. Increasing the number of daily practice sessions would likely worsen, not resolve, a headache caused by excessive muscle contraction. Making no adjustment ignores the protocol's instruction to reduce intensity and frequency when headache develops.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Applied tension's described adverse-effect management (reducing intensity and frequency for headache) is a practical, testable detail distinguishing thorough knowledge of the technique. COMLEX: This titration approach reflects the broader principle of adjusting behavioral technique intensity to patient tolerance rather than abandoning an effective treatment outright.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Phobia & Anxiety Disorder Differentiation by Trigger and Timeline","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Making no adjustment ignores t"}},{"id":"sdl30_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze cognitive distortions and underlying core schemas in Major Depressive Disorder by identifying Beck's cognitive triad (negative thoughts about the self, the world, and the future) and evaluating automatic thought records to guide cognitive restructuring during therapy sessions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with major depressive disorder tells his cognitive therapist, \"I can't concentrate, there's something seriously wrong with me, and I'll never get better.\" His therapist helps him examine whether this thought is accurate, generate alternative explanations, and reassign blame he has placed on himself for events outside his control. Which cognitive-technique process is the therapist engaging in?","choices":{"A":"Eliciting automatic thoughts as the earlier step of first identifying the thought itself","B":"Testing automatic thoughts by reviewing accuracy and generating alternatives","C":"Constructing an anxiety hierarchy as an early part of systematic desensitization","D":"Formulating the DBT treatment hierarchy as part of DBT's target-prioritization framework","E":"Conducting behavioral activation self-monitoring using a daily activity diary to track his behaviors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The patient's statement is itself an automatic thought already identified; the therapist's subsequent work — encouraging the patient to reject inaccurate or exaggerated automatic thoughts, reviewing the situation, reassigning blame, and generating alternative explanations — is specifically the \"testing automatic thoughts\" process. Eliciting automatic thoughts is the earlier step of identifying the thought itself, which has already occurred in this vignette; the therapist is now testing, not eliciting, this thought. Constructing an anxiety hierarchy is part of systematic desensitization, an unrelated behavioral technique. Formulating the DBT treatment hierarchy is part of DBT's target-prioritization framework, unrelated to this cognitive-therapy process. Behavioral activation self-monitoring uses an activity diary to track behaviors, not this thought-testing process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing \"eliciting\" (identifying the automatic thought) from \"testing\" (evaluating its validity, reassigning blame, generating alternatives) automatic thoughts is a key sequential distinction in cognitive therapy technique. COMLEX: This patient's exact automatic thought example directly illustrates Beck's cognitive model of how automatic perception, not the situation itself, drives the emotional/behavioral/physiologic reaction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — Core Mechanisms of CBT, Interpersonal Therapy & Family Therapy for MDD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze cognitive distortions and underlying core schemas in Major Depressive Disorder by identifying Beck's cognitive triad (negative thoughts about the self, the world, and the future) and evaluating automatic thought records to guide cognitive restructuring during therapy sessions.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient in cognitive therapy for depression has, over several sessions, repeatedly expressed variations of the belief \"unless I am perfect, I am worthless as a person,\" across many different specific situations. Her therapist recognizes this as a broader guiding rule rather than a situation-specific thought and begins working to test its validity and develop a more flexible alternative. Which underlying cognitive-therapy concept is being addressed?","choices":{"A":"A single isolated automatic thought","B":"The DBT quality-of-life tier","C":"A maladaptive core assumption","D":"A component of applied tension's protocol","E":"A biofeedback-measured physiologic parameter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This pattern of a broader, rule-like belief recurring across many specific situations is exactly what is meant by a maladaptive underlying assumption, distinct from a single situation-specific automatic thought, and cognitive therapy's fourth technique process, testing the validity of maladaptive assumptions, is what the therapist is beginning to do. A single, isolated automatic thought tied to one specific event describes the earlier, situation-bound cognitions from which this broader assumption pattern has already been identified, not the recurring rule itself. The DBT treatment hierarchy's quality-of-life tier is unrelated to cognitive therapy's assumption-identification process. A component of applied tension's isometric protocol is unrelated to this cognitive-therapy concept. A biofeedback-measured physiologic parameter is unrelated to the cognitive, belief-based nature of this concept.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maladaptive underlying assumptions are recognized when a rule-like belief recurs across multiple specific situations, distinguishing them from individual automatic thoughts. COMLEX: Identifying and then testing the validity of such assumptions are the third and fourth of cognitive therapy's four cognitive-technique processes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — Core Mechanisms of CBT, Interpersonal Therapy & Family Therapy for MDD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze cognitive distortions and underlying core schemas in Major Depressive Disorder by identifying Beck's cognitive triad (negative thoughts about the self, the world, and the future) and evaluating automatic thought records to guide cognitive restructuring during therapy sessions.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with major depressive disorder describes persistent thoughts that she is a failure (self), that no one can be trusted (world), and that things will never improve (future). Her cognitive therapist explains that these three domains are the classic organizing structure for negative depressive cognition and plans to use this framework didactically early in treatment. What is this organizing structure called, and what does its use accomplish at this stage of therapy?","choices":{"A":"The DBT treatment hierarchy used to prioritize her most dangerous behaviors first in each therapy session","B":"The behavioral activation activity hierarchy used to plan her easiest-to-hardest weekly activities each month","C":"The systematic desensitization anxiety hierarchy used to plan her graded exposure sequence over time","D":"Davanloo's oedipal-versus-non-oedipal conflict classification used to select her brief psychodynamic model of care at intake","E":"Beck's cognitive triad used as a teaching tool linking her symptoms to her thinking before formal restructuring begins"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This presentation is a textbook illustration of Beck's cognitive triad (negative thoughts about the self, the world, and the future), and the didactic aspects of cognitive therapy specifically include explaining the cognitive triad, schemas, and faulty logic to patients early on, providing a full explanation of the relation between symptoms and thinking, affect, and behavior before formal cognitive restructuring techniques (eliciting/testing automatic thoughts, identifying/testing assumptions) begin. The DBT treatment hierarchy prioritizes life-threatening, treatment-interfering, and quality-of-life behaviors, an entirely different framework unrelated to this cognitive-triad presentation. The behavioral activation activity hierarchy organizes activities by difficulty for depression-related behavioral withdrawal, not this three-domain cognitive framework. The systematic desensitization anxiety hierarchy organizes feared stimuli for exposure, unrelated to this cognitive presentation. Davanloo's oedipal-versus-non-oedipal classification is a brief psychodynamic psychotherapy selection framework, unrelated to Beck's cognitive model.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing Beck's cognitive triad (self/world/future) in a vignette and knowing its didactic role early in cognitive therapy, before restructuring, ties together the theory and technique of cognitive therapy for depression. COMLEX: This didactic explanation contrasts with psychoanalytically oriented therapies, which typically require far less upfront explanation to the patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — Core Mechanisms of CBT, Interpersonal Therapy & Family Therapy for MDD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Select the appropriate conditioning-based behavioral technique—such as systematic desensitization (reciprocal inhibition), in vivo flooding with response prevention, or behavioral activation—based on a patient's specific presentation (e.g., phobias, obsessive-compulsive rituals, or depression-induced behavioral withdrawal).","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with obsessive-compulsive disorder and contamination obsessions is treated by having a therapist model touching progressively more \"contaminated\" objects while the patient follows along and is prevented from performing his usual washing rituals afterward. Which combination of conditioning-based techniques best describes this approach?","choices":{"A":"Systematic desensitization combined with progressive muscle relaxation training exercises each week","B":"Applied tension combined with biofeedback-assisted relaxation instrument feedback","C":"Exposure via participant modeling combined with response prevention of his ritualized behaviors","D":"Behavioral activation combined with a structured self-monitoring activity diary","E":"Autogenic training combined with cue-controlled relaxation breathing exercises"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This scenario combines participant modeling (the therapist demonstrates touching contaminated objects, and the patient follows by imitation) with response prevention (the patient is prevented from performing washing rituals), and exposure and response prevention (ERP) together blocks the avoidance behaviors that would otherwise stop the fear from extinguishing, since avoidance behaviors that typically reduce fear must be prevented for exposure to produce extinction. Systematic desensitization with progressive muscle relaxation involves a graded hierarchy paired with a relaxed state, which is not what this direct-exposure, rituals-prevented scenario describes. Applied tension with biofeedback-assisted relaxation targets the biphasic vasovagal response of blood/injury phobia, unrelated to this OCD contamination scenario. Behavioral activation with a self-monitoring diary addresses depression-related behavioral withdrawal, not OCD ritual prevention. Autogenic training with cue-controlled relaxation are both relaxation techniques, not exposure-based OCD treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exposure and response prevention (ERP), often combined with participant modeling to demonstrate the exposure, is specifically named as the treatment of choice for OCD — a frequently tested pairing. COMLEX: Response prevention specifically means discontinuing all avoidance and anxiety-reducing behaviors (like washing rituals) during and after exposure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Step-Wise OCD Treatment: SSRIs, ERP & Adjunctive Antipsychotics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl30_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Select the appropriate conditioning-based behavioral technique—such as systematic desensitization (reciprocal inhibition), in vivo flooding with response prevention, or behavioral activation—based on a patient's specific presentation (e.g., phobias, obsessive-compulsive rituals, or depression-induced behavioral withdrawal).","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with a specific phobia of dogs is treated first by having her look at a photograph of a calm dog, then progressing over several real-life sessions to briefly petting a small, calm dog under supervision, with no relaxation training or hierarchy of imagined scenes involved. Which technique does this best represent, and how does it differ from systematic desensitization?","choices":{"A":"Flooding technique","B":"Graded exposure","C":"Applied tension","D":"Participant modeling","E":"Biofeedback relaxation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Therapeutic-graded exposure is similar to systematic desensitization except that relaxation training is not involved and treatment usually occurs in a real-life context, with the individual brought into actual contact with the feared stimulus according to a hierarchy, exactly as described (photograph, then progressively closer real-life contact with a dog). Flooding does not use a hierarchy at all (unlike this graded, stepwise scenario) and, like graded exposure, is typically in vivo rather than relying on imagined scenes, so this description does not match flooding. Applied tension specifically addresses the biphasic vasovagal response of blood/injury phobia using isometric muscle contraction, which is not described in this dog-phobia scenario. Participant modeling can be a component of graded exposure but does not, by itself, capture the graded, hierarchy-based real-life progression central to this scenario; it also does not require the therapist to perform the entire behavior first in every case. Biofeedback-assisted relaxation involves instrument-based physiologic feedback, which is not described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Therapeutic-graded exposure's key distinguishing features from systematic desensitization (no relaxation training, real-life rather than imagined context) are a classic higher-order discrimination point. COMLEX: Both graded exposure and systematic desensitization use a hierarchy, which is the key feature distinguishing both from flooding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Step-Wise OCD Treatment: SSRIs, ERP & Adjunctive Antipsychotics","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Flooding does not use a hierar","D":"Participant modeling can be a "}},{"id":"sdl30_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Select the appropriate conditioning-based behavioral technique—such as systematic desensitization (reciprocal inhibition), in vivo flooding with response prevention, or behavioral activation—based on a patient's specific presentation (e.g., phobias, obsessive-compulsive rituals, or depression-induced behavioral withdrawal).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old recently retired man presents with low mood, loss of energy, and social withdrawal, spending most days sitting at home without contact with others. His therapist has him complete a daily diary of current activities and their associated reward, difficulty, and importance, then collaboratively builds a list of valued activities ranging from \"go to one movie a week\" to more demanding goals, which he will progressively attempt starting with the easiest. Which behavioral principle explains why this approach is expected to improve his depression?","choices":{"A":"Reciprocal inhibition from a relaxed physiologic state suppresses his anxiety response to specific feared stimuli over several sessions","B":"Operant conditioning of his autonomic nervous system restores voluntary control over his sympathetic tone","C":"Increasing overt engagement with reinforcing pursuits restores the reduced reinforcement caused by his ongoing disengagement","D":"Isometric muscle contraction raises his blood pressure to prevent a sudden biphasic vasovagal response","E":"Exposure without any escape extinguishes a specific previously conditioned fear response over time"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is behavioral activation, based on the theory that depression persists because reinforcement for healthy behavior is low while behavioral withdrawal leads to loss of reinforcement from pleasurable events; increasing structured, overt behaviors that bring the patient into contact with reinforcing environmental contingencies is expected to improve mood, thoughts, and quality of life. Reciprocal inhibition describes systematic desensitization's anxiety-specific mechanism, not this depression-focused, reinforcement-based rationale. Operant conditioning of the autonomic nervous system describes biofeedback's mechanism, unrelated to this activity-engagement approach. Isometric muscle contraction raising blood pressure describes applied tension's mechanism for blood/injury phobia, unrelated to this depression scenario. Exposure without escape extinguishing conditioned fear describes flooding, an anxiety-focused technique, not this reinforcement-based depression treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Behavioral activation's rationale (restoring reinforcement from pleasurable activity to counteract depression-driven withdrawal) is distinct from every anxiety-focused conditioning mechanism (reciprocal inhibition, operant conditioning, isometric tension, extinction via exposure). COMLEX: The easiest-to-most-difficult activity hierarchy mirrors the graded structure of exposure hierarchies but serves a reinforcement-restoration purpose rather than a fear-extinction purpose.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Step-Wise OCD Treatment: SSRIs, ERP & Adjunctive Antipsychotics","isHighYield":true}],"31":[{"id":"sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate somatic symptom disorder, illness anxiety disorder, conversion disorder, psychological factors affecting medical conditions, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient believes she has a serious undiagnosed illness despite reassurance, but on review of systems she reports no significant physical symptoms at all. Which diagnosis best fits, distinguishing it from somatic symptom disorder?","choices":{"A":"Somatic symptom disorder, given a required significant physical symptom","B":"Conversion disorder, given a required neurologic incompatibility","C":"Factitious disorder, given required intentional symptom production","D":"Malingering, given a required recognizable external incentive","E":"Illness anxiety disorder, given the absent physical symptoms"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Illness anxiety disorder patients, like those with somatic symptom disorder, believe they have a serious undiagnosed disease despite evidence to the contrary, but unlike somatic symptom disorder, these individuals do not have significant physical symptoms — the disorder centers on health anxiety itself rather than symptom burden. Somatic symptom disorder specifically requires one or more significant somatic symptoms, which this patient lacks. Conversion disorder presents with what appears to be a neurologic condition (motor or sensory symptoms), not primarily health anxiety without physical symptoms. Factitious disorder involves feigning or inducing illness for the sick role, not a genuine belief in undiagnosed illness. Malingering involves consciously fabricating symptoms for an external, recognizable goal, not this genuine anxiety-driven belief pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Illness anxiety disorder's key distinguishing feature from somatic symptom disorder is the absence of significant physical symptoms — the pathology is health anxiety itself. COMLEX: These patients are often described as addicted to internet searches about their feared illness, inferring the worst from what they find.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — the same syndrome, duration, impairment, and exclusion-criteria diagnostic logic, applied here to differentiating the somatic symptom and related disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate somatic symptom disorder, illness anxiety disorder, conversion disorder, psychological factors affecting medical conditions, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient presents with acute-onset leg weakness that does not conform to any known neurologic pathway, occurring shortly after a major interpersonal conflict, and the patient does not appear to be intentionally producing the symptom. Which diagnosis best fits?","choices":{"A":"Somatic symptom disorder, given diffuse symptom-related anxiety","B":"Illness anxiety disorder, given disease conviction without symptoms","C":"Factitious disorder, given intentional symptom production","D":"Conversion disorder, given the neurologic incompatibility","E":"Malingering, given a recognizable external incentive"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Conversion disorder (functional neurologic symptom disorder) presents with apparent neurologic symptoms, motor or sensory, that are incompatible with known neurologic conditions, often preceded by conflicts or stressors, and individuals do not intentionally produce these symptoms. Somatic symptom disorder centers on preoccupation with and anxiety about somatic symptoms generally, not specifically neurologically incompatible motor/sensory findings tied to a stressor. Illness anxiety disorder centers on fear of having a disease without significant physical symptoms, not an acute neurologic-appearing deficit. Factitious disorder involves intentional production of symptoms, which is explicitly absent here. Malingering also requires conscious, voluntary symptom production for an external goal, which is explicitly absent here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conversion disorder's hallmark is a neurologic-appearing symptom that is incompatible with known neuroanatomy and not intentionally produced, often temporally linked to a psychological stressor. COMLEX: Conversion motor symptoms can mimic paralysis, ataxia, dysphagia, or nonepileptic seizures; sensory symptoms can mimic blindness, deafness, or anesthesia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — the same syndrome, duration, impairment, and exclusion-criteria diagnostic logic, applied here to differentiating the somatic symptom and related disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Somatic symptom disorder cente"}},{"id":"sdl31_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate somatic symptom disorder, illness anxiety disorder, conversion disorder, psychological factors affecting medical conditions, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with poorly controlled diabetes deliberately underdoses her insulin in an effort to lose weight, worsening her glycemic control. There is no feigning of illness and no external incentive beyond the weight-loss goal itself. Which diagnosis best fits this behavior's effect on her established medical condition?","choices":{"A":"Somatic symptom disorder, given her disproportionate symptom-related health anxiety and distress","B":"Conversion disorder, given an apparent neurologic-appearing motor or sensory symptom","C":"Psychological factors affecting other medical conditions, given her insulin manipulation","D":"Factitious disorder, given feigned illness for the sick role itself","E":"Malingering, given a clear and recognizable external incentive"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Psychological factors affecting other medical conditions requires an actual medical condition that is caused by or adversely affected by emotional or psychological factors, and the manipulation of insulin by an individual with diabetes to lose weight is specifically given as a classic example of this category. Somatic symptom disorder centers on preoccupation with and anxiety about somatic symptoms rather than a psychologically driven exacerbation of an established medical diagnosis. Conversion disorder presents with a neurologic-appearing symptom incompatible with known pathways, not psychologically driven worsening of a real, established medical condition like diabetes. Factitious disorder requires feigning, simulating, or inducing illness to receive medical attention/assume the sick role, which is not this patient's described motivation. Malingering requires a conscious, recognizable external goal such as financial gain, which is not described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psychological factors affecting other medical conditions requires a genuine, diagnosable medical condition that psychological/behavioral factors worsen — insulin manipulation for weight loss and diuretic misuse in hypertension are classic textbook examples. COMLEX: This category is distinct from factitious disorder in that no illness is fabricated — a real medical condition is genuinely made worse.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — the same syndrome, duration, impairment, and exclusion-criteria diagnostic logic, applied here to differentiating the somatic symptom and related disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic criteria using symptom burden, health anxiety, neurologic incompatibility, intentionality, external incentives, and functional impairment.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has had abdominal pain for 8 months, spends significant time and energy worrying about the symptom's seriousness, and is markedly anxious about her health as a result. Per DSM-5 criteria, what is the minimum duration requirement for diagnosing somatic symptom disorder?","choices":{"A":"At least 6 months of persistent symptoms and associated distress","B":"At least 2 years of persistent symptoms, the ICD-10 somatization disorder duration","C":"At least 1 month of persistent symptoms and associated distress","D":"At least 3 weeks of persistent symptoms and associated distress","E":"No minimum duration is specified for somatic symptom disorder in DSM-5"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DSM-5 specifies that somatic symptom disorder symptoms must be present for at least 6 months (the \"persistent\" specifier is a separate course label for more than 6 months of severe symptoms and marked impairment), and this patient's 8-month duration with excessive time and energy devoted to symptoms and health-related anxiety meets this criterion. At least 2 years is the duration requirement under the older ICD-10 concept of somatization disorder, not DSM-5's somatic symptom disorder. At least 1 month is not the DSM-5 threshold; a presentation of that duration would fall under other specified somatic symptom and related disorder instead. At least 3 weeks is also not the DSM-5 duration threshold. DSM-5 does specify a duration requirement (6 months), so claiming none exists is incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5 somatic symptom disorder requires ≥6 months duration, distinct from ICD-10's ≥2 years for somatization disorder — a classic testable numeric detail. COMLEX: States lasting fewer than 6 months are diagnosed as \"other specified somatic symptom and related disorder\" instead.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — extends the diagnosis/social-evaluation/MSE/risk-assessment clinical-formulation framework to applying symptom-burden and intentionality-based diagnostic criteria specifically","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic criteria using symptom burden, health anxiety, neurologic incompatibility, intentionality, external incentives, and functional impairment.","batch":1,"stem":"Per DSM-5 diagnostic criteria, how many somatic symptoms are required at minimum to diagnose somatic symptom disorder?","choices":{"A":"At least 3 somatic symptoms are required, with no minimum symptom count ever waived","B":"At least 8 somatic symptoms across multiple organ systems are required, per the older somatization criteria","C":"One or more somatic symptoms, with severity graded by symptom count and distress level","D":"Somatic symptom count plays no role in the diagnosis, which is graded purely by distress level","E":"Exactly 2 somatic symptoms from different organ systems are required, with severity graded separately"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DSM-5 requires only one or more somatic symptoms for the diagnosis, with severity specifiers based on count: mild is 1 symptom, moderate is 2 or more symptoms, and severe is 2 or more symptoms with multiple symptomatic complaints or one severe symptom, so the disorder does not require a large number of symptoms to qualify. At least 3 symptoms are not always required; even a single symptom qualifies for the mild specifier. At least 8 symptoms across multiple organ systems reflects the much stricter older ICD-10/DSM-IV somatization disorder concept, not DSM-5 somatic symptom disorder. Symptom count is not irrelevant; it directly determines the severity specifier (mild/moderate/severe). Exactly 2 symptoms from different organ systems is not a DSM-5 requirement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5's minimum of just 1 symptom (versus the old DSM-IV/ICD-10 requirement of multiple symptoms across organ systems) reflects the deliberate DSM-5 shift away from counting medically unexplained symptoms. COMLEX: Severity specifiers (mild/moderate/severe) are graded by symptom count and distress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — extends the diagnosis/social-evaluation/MSE/risk-assessment clinical-formulation framework to applying symptom-burden and intentionality-based diagnostic criteria specifically","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"At least 3 symptoms are not al","D":"Symptom count is not irrelevan"}},{"id":"sdl31_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic criteria using symptom burden, health anxiety, neurologic incompatibility, intentionality, external incentives, and functional impairment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which diagnostic feature is central to applying illness anxiety disorder criteria, distinguishing intentionality-based factitious presentations from this genuinely anxious presentation?","choices":{"A":"Deliberate self-induced injury, pursued for an external financial incentive","B":"Health anxiety with the absence of any voluntary, intentional symptom production by the patient","C":"A confirmed absence of any anxiety symptoms on structured assessment","D":"A requirement that at least 8 distinct organ systems be involved","E":"A requirement of a confirmed underlying malignancy, before the diagnosis can be made"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Illness anxiety disorder is centered on genuine, involuntary health anxiety about having a serious undiagnosed disease, and illness anxiety disorder differs from factitious disorder specifically in the lack of voluntary production of symptoms, making intentionality the key discriminating criterion. Deliberate self-induced injury for external financial incentive describes malingering, not illness anxiety disorder's involuntary anxiety-driven presentation. Illness anxiety disorder specifically involves prominent anxiety about health, so an absence of anxiety would directly contradict the diagnosis. There is no requirement of 8 distinct organ systems for illness anxiety disorder; that reflects the older, stricter somatization concept. Illness anxiety disorder does not require a confirmed malignancy or any actual disease; the condition centers on the fear itself despite evidence against serious illness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intentionality (voluntary vs. involuntary symptom/anxiety production) is the key criterion separating illness anxiety disorder from factitious disorder and malingering. COMLEX: This same intentionality axis (voluntary vs. involuntary) organizes the entire differential across the somatic symptom and related disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — extends the diagnosis/social-evaluation/MSE/risk-assessment clinical-formulation framework to applying symptom-burden and intentionality-based diagnostic criteria specifically","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Illness anxiety disorder speci"}},{"id":"sdl31_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze medical, neurologic, endocrine, medication-induced, and substance-related conditions before assigning a somatic or factitious diagnosis.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient presents with excessive worry about numerous somatic complaints and disproportionate health anxiety. Which category of condition must be systematically excluded before assigning a somatic symptom or related disorder diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Medical and neurologic diseases that are not easily diagnosed, such as multiple sclerosis","B":"A confirmed diagnosis of malingering, which involves a conscious external incentive","C":"A confirmed diagnosis of factitious disorder, which involves intentional symptom production","D":"A prior psychiatric hospitalization for an anxiety or mood disorder","E":"A family history of somatic symptom disorder in first-degree relatives"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatic symptom disorder must be differentiated from nonpsychiatric medical conditions that are not necessarily easily diagnosed, specifically including AIDS, endocrinopathies, myasthenia gravis, multiple sclerosis, degenerative diseases of the nervous system, systemic lupus erythematosus, and occult neoplastic disorders, before the diagnosis is assigned. A confirmed diagnosis of malingering only is not the described exclusion step; malingering is a distinct differential diagnosis, not a prerequisite exclusion category for medical disease. A confirmed diagnosis of factitious disorder only is similarly a distinct differential diagnosis, not the described medical-exclusion step. A prior psychiatric hospitalization alone is not part of the described medical-exclusion process. A family history of somatic symptom disorder alone is not the described exclusion criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A specific list of hard-to-diagnose medical conditions (AIDS, endocrinopathies, myasthenia gravis, MS, SLE, occult neoplasm) must be ruled out before diagnosing somatic symptom disorder — a classic \"don't miss\" board teaching point. COMLEX: This exclusion process is one reason building a trusting alliance and avoiding premature psychological interpretation matters clinically.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 6 — the same primary-versus-secondary psychiatric differentiation framework, applied here to ruling out medical/neurologic/endocrine/substance causes before a somatic or factitious diagnosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze medical, neurologic, endocrine, medication-induced, and substance-related conditions before assigning a somatic or factitious diagnosis.","batch":1,"stem":"A neurologic workup for a patient with suspected conversion disorder should routinely include which category of studies before the diagnosis is finalized?","choices":{"A":"No laboratory or imaging studies, relying on the clinical history","B":"Genetic testing for hereditary neuropathies and myopathies","C":"Routine laboratory studies, EEG, and imaging such as MRI","D":"Cardiac catheterization and coronary angiography","E":"Skin and muscle biopsies for small-fiber neuropathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A neurologic workup of suspected conversion disorder should include routine laboratory studies, electroencephalograms, and other special studies such as MRI to rule out organic causes, alongside careful attention to trauma, abuse history, and family history of conversion symptoms. No laboratory or imaging studies at all would fail to exclude the organic neurologic conditions that must be ruled out before a conversion disorder diagnosis is finalized. Genetic testing exclusively is not the described routine workup, which specifically includes labs, EEG, and MRI/special studies. A cardiac catheterization exclusively is not part of the described neurologic workup. A skin biopsy exclusively is not part of the described neurologic workup for conversion disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A routine conversion disorder workup (labs, EEG, MRI/special studies) exists specifically to rule out organic neurologic disease before finalizing the diagnosis — critical for avoiding a missed neurologic condition. COMLEX: EEG is particularly important given the overlap between nonepileptic seizures and true epileptic seizures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 6 — the same primary-versus-secondary psychiatric differentiation framework, applied here to ruling out medical/neurologic/endocrine/substance causes before a somatic or factitious diagnosis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"No laboratory or imaging studi","B":"Genetic testing exclusively is","E":"A skin biopsy exclusively is n"}},{"id":"sdl31_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze medical, neurologic, endocrine, medication-induced, and substance-related conditions before assigning a somatic or factitious diagnosis.","batch":1,"stem":"Before diagnosing factitious disorder, which broader category should the physician actively explore, given that repeated surgeries in these patients can predispose to genuine complications?","choices":{"A":"Malingering, given the possibility of a hidden external incentive","B":"An authentic or concomitant physical illness, given her many prior surgeries","C":"Her employment history and any recent change in her work status","D":"Her insurance coverage and how it has shaped her surgical care","E":"Her criminal record and any history of legal involvement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Any disorder in which physical signs and symptoms are prominent is part of the differential diagnosis for factitious disorder, and the physician must explore the possibility of authentic or concomitant physical illness, since a history of many surgeries in patients with factitious disorder may predispose them to genuine complications or actual diseases requiring further surgery. Exploring only the possibility of malingering, and nothing else, ignores the described need to also rule out genuine or concomitant physical illness. Exploring only employment history, and nothing else, is not the described diagnostic exploration for factitious disorder. Exploring only insurance coverage status, and nothing else, is not the described diagnostic exploration. Exploring only criminal record, and nothing else, is not the described diagnostic exploration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patients with factitious disorder can develop genuine complications or actual disease from repeated self-induced procedures, so authentic illness must always be actively considered, not assumed absent. COMLEX: This is a key patient-safety point — dismissing all complaints as fabricated risks missing real, superimposed pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 6 — the same primary-versus-secondary psychiatric differentiation framework, applied here to ruling out medical/neurologic/endocrine/substance causes before a somatic or factitious diagnosis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Exploring only the possibility"}},{"id":"sdl31_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret examination and laboratory findings to distinguish true neurologic disease from a functional neurologic symptom disorder.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient presents with apparent leg paralysis. On examination, the physician places a hand under the \"paralyzed\" leg while asking the patient to raise the opposite, unaffected leg against resistance. Pressure is felt in the examiner's hand under the paralyzed leg. What does this finding suggest?","choices":{"A":"A positive Babinski sign, confirming a true upper motor neuron lesion","B":"A normal finding with no diagnostic significance in paralysis","C":"Definitive evidence that the patient is consciously malingering","D":"A finding specific to myasthenia gravis and its neuromuscular junction pathology","E":"A positive Hoover test, suggesting a functional paralysis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Hoover test involves the examiner's hand placed under the \"paralyzed\" leg while the patient attempts straight leg raising with the opposite leg; pressure noted under the paralyzed leg indicates involuntary contralateral hip extension, a classic finding suggesting a functional rather than a true neurologic paralysis. A positive Babinski sign is a distinct test (toe extension with plantar stimulation) indicating an upper motor neuron lesion, not the Hoover test finding described here. This is not a normal, non-diagnostic finding; it is specifically described as suggestive of conversion (functional) paralysis. The Hoover test cannot by itself distinguish conversion disorder from malingering, since both involve functional (non-organic) findings; it only indicates a functional rather than a true neurologic process. This finding is not specific to myasthenia gravis, an unrelated neuromuscular junction disorder with its own distinct testing (e.g., repetitive nerve stimulation, edrophonium test).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Hoover test is a classic bedside maneuver for detecting functional (nonorganic) leg weakness — a frequently tested finding for conversion disorder. COMLEX: Other functional-weakness bedside signs include the drop test (a \"paralyzed\" hand falls next to, not onto, the face) and give-away weakness on formal strength testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — the same true-versus-functional/organic differentiation framework used for hallucinations versus illusions, applied here to neurologic examination and laboratory findings","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The Hoover test cannot by itse"}},{"id":"sdl31_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret examination and laboratory findings to distinguish true neurologic disease from a functional neurologic symptom disorder.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient reports sudden bilateral blindness following a stressful event. On examination, when the physician suddenly flashes a bright light toward the patient's eyes, the patient flinches. What does this finding most strongly suggest?","choices":{"A":"True cortical blindness, a diagnosis involving a disrupted occipital visual pathway","B":"Functional, nonorganic blindness, given intact visual-pathway function on flinch testing","C":"A relative afferent pupillary defect, a finding assessed via the swinging-flashlight test","D":"A normal, nondiagnostic finding with no interpretive significance","E":"Factitious disorder specifically, a diagnosis requiring evidence of conscious fabrication"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In evaluating functional blindness, a sudden flash of bright light causing the patient to flinch is a described bedside sign, since flinching in response to visual stimulation implies at least some intact visual pathway function inconsistent with true, complete blindness, supporting a functional (nonorganic, conversion) etiology instead. True cortical blindness from an occipital lesion would not be expected to produce a flinch response to a sudden visual stimulus, since the visual pathway itself is disrupted. A relative afferent pupillary defect is tested by the swinging flashlight (Marcus Gunn) test and reflects optic nerve pathology, a different finding than the flinch response described here; conversion blindness is specifically associated with the absence of such a defect. This is not a nondiagnostic finding present equally in true and functional blindness; it specifically distinguishes the two. This finding alone cannot distinguish conversion disorder from factitious disorder, since both are nonorganic; it only indicates functional rather than true blindness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A flinch response to sudden bright light in a patient reporting blindness is a classic bedside sign supporting functional (nonorganic) vision loss over true cortical blindness. COMLEX: In conversion blindness, patients also typically walk without collisions, have reactive pupils, and show normal cortical-evoked potentials on formal testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — the same true-versus-functional/organic differentiation framework used for hallucinations versus illusions, applied here to neurologic examination and laboratory findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret examination and laboratory findings to distinguish true neurologic disease from a functional neurologic symptom disorder.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient presents with an apparent generalized convulsive episode. Which laboratory finding, if present after the event, would most support a true epileptic seizure rather than a nonepileptic seizure (NES)?","choices":{"A":"A normal serum prolactin concentration, drawn shortly after the episode","B":"A normal electromyogram, obtained as part of the broader neurologic and metabolic workup","C":"An elevated postictal serum prolactin concentration, drawn shortly after the episode","D":"Preserved pupillary and gag reflexes on postictal neurologic examination","E":"A completely normal neurologic examination performed between episodes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patients with true epileptic seizures typically show a postictal increase in serum prolactin concentration, whereas patients with nonepileptic seizures (a conversion disorder symptom) characteristically have no postseizure increase in prolactin and retain pupillary and gag reflexes after the seizure-like activity. A normal serum prolactin concentration would be more consistent with NES, not a true epileptic seizure, so this does not support a true seizure. A normal electromyogram is relevant to motor conversion symptoms like paralysis, not to distinguishing seizure types via a postictal marker. Preserved pupillary and gag reflexes after the episode is specifically described as characteristic of NES rather than a marker supporting a true epileptic seizure. A completely normal neurologic examination between episodes can occur in both true epilepsy and NES and is not the specific postictal marker distinguishing the two.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postictal serum prolactin elevation supports a true epileptic seizure, while its absence, combined with preserved pupillary/gag reflexes, supports nonepileptic seizures — a classic, frequently tested lab-based discrimination. COMLEX: Some patients with NES also have a coexisting true epileptic disorder, which can complicate the clinical picture and workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — the same true-versus-functional/organic differentiation framework used for hallucinations versus illusions, applied here to neurologic examination and laboratory findings","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Preserved pupillary and gag re"}},{"id":"sdl31_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare factitious disorder and malingering using motivation, sick role, external incentives, and clinical context.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient repeatedly seeks hospitalization and undergoes numerous invasive procedures with no apparent external reward, purposely misleading health professionals in order to be seen as ill. What is this patient's primary motivation according to the described diagnostic framework?","choices":{"A":"To secure financial compensation from a lawsuit or insurance claim, a recognizable external goal","B":"To assume the sick role itself, without any external, tangible incentive","C":"To evade criminal prosecution or arrest, a concrete environmental incentive","D":"To obtain free room and board for the night, a tangible external benefit","E":"To avoid an unwanted work obligation, a specific goal defining malingering"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In factitious disorder, the primary motivation is not the avoidance of duties, financial gain, or anything concrete; the aim is simply to take on the sick role and receive medical care and attention, with no apparent external reward or motivation outside the illness role itself. Securing financial compensation, evading prosecution, obtaining free room and board, and avoiding work obligations are all examples of manifest, recognizable environmental goals characteristic of malingering, not factitious disorder's described sick-role-seeking motivation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Factitious disorder's motivation (assuming the sick role itself, with no external incentive) versus malingering's motivation (a concrete, recognizable external goal) is the single most tested discriminating fact in this differential. COMLEX: This same distinction extends to factitious disorder imposed on another, where the perpetrator (not the victim) receives the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — extends the specific-disorder diagnostic-feature framework (including antisocial personality disorder's overlapping differential) to comparing factitious disorder and malingering","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Securing financial compensatio"}},{"id":"sdl31_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare factitious disorder and malingering using motivation, sick role, external incentives, and clinical context.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient facing an upcoming criminal trial develops apparent memory loss that conveniently resolves once legal proceedings conclude, and his history contains numerous inconsistencies. Which feature of this presentation most strongly favors malingering over factitious disorder?","choices":{"A":"A clear and recognizable external goal that resolves once no longer needed","B":"A complete absence of conscious control over the symptoms themselves","C":"A documented history of extensive prior surgical procedures undergone","D":"A demonstrated eagerness to undergo additional invasive testing procedures","E":"An extreme unwillingness to grant outside informants any meaningful access"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Malingerers have a manifest, recognizable environmental goal in producing symptoms (here, evading legal consequences), and unlike factitious disorder, these patients can usually stop producing signs and symptoms when they are no longer useful or when risk becomes too high — exactly matching the described resolution of memory loss once the legal proceedings concluded. Both malingering and factitious disorder involve symptoms under conscious, voluntary control, so an absence of conscious control would argue against both diagnoses, not specifically favor malingering. Extensive prior surgical history and eagerness for invasive testing are described clues more characteristic of factitious disorder's pursuit of the sick role, not malingering. Extreme unwillingness to allow outside informant access is also a described factitious disorder clue (an inconsistent, selective, or misleading informant resisting outside information access), not a malingering-specific feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A goal-directed symptom pattern that resolves once the external incentive (here, legal jeopardy) disappears is the hallmark distinguishing malingering from factitious disorder's sick-role-seeking, harder-to-voluntarily-stop pattern. COMLEX: A malingerer's history is also typically more inconsistent and contradictory than that of a patient with a genuine conversion disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — extends the specific-disorder diagnostic-feature framework (including antisocial personality disorder's overlapping differential) to comparing factitious disorder and malingering","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare factitious disorder and malingering using motivation, sick role, external incentives, and clinical context.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has undergone treatment at numerous different hospitals, is an inconsistent and misleading informant who resists granting access to outside records, and has been noted to predict clinical deterioration shortly before a scheduled hospital discharge. These features should raise suspicion for which diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Somatic symptom disorder","B":"Illness anxiety disorder","C":"Psychological factors affecting other medical conditions","D":"Factitious disorder","E":"Functional neurological symptom disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Seeking treatment at various different hospitals, being an inconsistent, selective, or misleading informant who resists outside-record access, and predicting deteriorations shortly before scheduled discharge are all specifically listed clues that should trigger suspicion of factitious disorder. Somatic symptom disorder is not characterized by this specific pattern of deliberate informant inconsistency and hospital-hopping behavior. Illness anxiety disorder centers on health anxiety without significant physical symptoms or this described deceptive, sick-role-seeking behavior pattern. Psychological factors affecting a medical condition involves a genuine medical illness worsened by psychological factors, not this described pattern of deceptive, engineered presentations. Functional neurological symptom disorder (conversion disorder) patients are not usually conversant with medical terminology and hospital routines in the way this multi-hospital, sophisticated pattern suggests, and their symptoms are not intentionally produced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A specific list of \"red flag\" clues (multi-hospital care-seeking, misleading/inconsistent informant behavior, deterioration timed before discharge) is highly testable for recognizing factitious disorder in a vignette. COMLEX: Additional listed clues include a healthcare-field work history, gratuitous self-aggrandizing lying, and opposition to psychiatric assessment while pursuing medical/surgical intervention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — extends the specific-disorder diagnostic-feature framework (including antisocial personality disorder's overlapping differential) to comparing factitious disorder and malingering","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply communication strategies that validate symptoms while avoiding unnecessary testing, iatrogenic harm, stigma, and adversarial confrontation.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a long history of somatic complaints and multiple prior negative workups arrives with a \"thick\" chart of prior tests and procedures. Which communication approach is recommended for building a trusting therapeutic alliance with this patient?","choices":{"A":"Receptive listening, tolerance for repetition, and a neutral, nonconfrontational stance","B":"Immediate, firm confrontation about the psychological nature of the symptoms","C":"Effusive reassurance at the first visit that nothing serious could be wrong","D":"Avoiding any physical examination at subsequent visits to prevent reinforcement","E":"Pointing out each time the patient repeats a complaint already investigated"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Building a trusting alliance with patients who have severe, protracted somatic syndromes must begin with respect for the patient's symptoms and acknowledgment of their validity, and active, receptive listening, tolerance for repetition, and a neutral approach that avoids being dismissive, confrontational, or overly reassuring are described as essential skills. Immediate, firm confrontation about the psychological nature of symptoms is explicitly the kind of adversarial approach that should be avoided. Overly effusive, premature reassurance can be perceived by the patient as disinterest or dismissiveness, which is explicitly cautioned against. Complete avoidance of physical examination at every visit is not recommended; briefer physical assessments should still follow at subsequent visits. Open dismissiveness toward repeated complaints directly contradicts the described need for tolerance of repetition and respect for symptom validity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A \"neutral\" communication stance (neither dismissive nor confrontational nor overly reassuring) is the specifically recommended approach for building trust with somatic symptom disorder patients. COMLEX: The physician should keep an open mind despite forewarnings in prior medical records and perform an independent assessment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 2 — the same empathy-versus-identification, boundary-preserving clinical stance, applied here to validating symptoms without adversarial confrontation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Open dismissiveness toward rep"}},{"id":"sdl31_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply communication strategies that validate symptoms while avoiding unnecessary testing, iatrogenic harm, stigma, and adversarial confrontation.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician suspects factitious disorder in a hospitalized patient after reviewing inconsistent records. According to the recommended management guidelines, which approach should guide how the clinician raises the possibility of psychiatric involvement with the patient?","choices":{"A":"A blunt, immediate accusation of lying in front of the full care team","B":"Complete avoidance of ever raising the topic of psychiatric involvement","C":"A threat of immediate discharge unless the patient confesses","D":"An anonymous written note left for the patient to find","E":"An empathic, nonconfrontational, face-saving discussion of the findings"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Management guidelines for factitious disorder specifically recommend steering the patient toward psychiatric treatment in an empathic, nonconfrontational, face-saving manner, avoiding aggressive direct confrontation, since pointed or accusatory questioning may provoke truculence, evasion, flight from the hospital, or even provoke frank psychosis in some instances. A blunt, immediate accusation of lying in front of the care team is exactly the aggressive direct confrontation the guidelines specifically warn against. Complete avoidance of raising the topic at all fails the described goal of steering the patient toward needed psychiatric treatment. A punitive threat of discharge unless the patient confesses is an adversarial, confrontational approach inconsistent with the recommended empathic, face-saving stance. An anonymous written note is not a described or recommended communication method in this framework.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Factitious disorder management specifically calls for an empathic, nonconfrontational, face-saving approach to psychiatric referral — vigorous confrontation risks provoking flight from care or even frank psychosis. COMLEX: Other management guidelines include minimizing unnecessary invasive testing, regular interdisciplinary staff meetings to manage countertransference and splitting, and treating underlying psychiatric disturbances.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 2 — the same empathy-versus-identification, boundary-preserving clinical stance, applied here to validating symptoms without adversarial confrontation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply communication strategies that validate symptoms while avoiding unnecessary testing, iatrogenic harm, stigma, and adversarial confrontation.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with conversion disorder is anxious that her physicians believe she is faking her symptoms. Which statement reflects the recommended communication approach early in treatment?","choices":{"A":"Explaining in detail the unconscious conflict behind her symptoms at the first visit","B":"Telling her that her symptoms are imaginary and purely psychological","C":"Telling her directly that she is malingering and wasting the team's time","D":"Reassuring her that key tests are normal and symptoms are likely to improve","E":"Withholding her test results until a psychiatrist has seen her"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Psychological interpretations or explanations do not work well early in the treatment process for conversion disorder, but reassuring patients that critical tests are normal and that symptoms will eventually improve may be helpful, and any implication to the patient that she is malingering is described as very counterproductive. Explaining the specific unconscious psychological conflict in detail is explicitly described as not working well early in the process. Telling the patient her symptoms are not real and entirely psychological risks the same counterproductive, invalidating effect the framework warns against. Directly telling the patient she is malingering is explicitly named as very counterproductive. Refusing to discuss test results at all is not the recommended approach; sharing reassuring, normal test results is specifically what is recommended.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Early conversion disorder communication should reassure about normal test results and expected improvement while explicitly avoiding any implication of malingering — psychological interpretation comes later, if at all. COMLEX: Behavioral interventions instead focus on improving self-esteem, emotional expression, assertiveness, and comfortable communication with others.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 2 — the same empathy-versus-identification, boundary-preserving clinical stance, applied here to validating symptoms without adversarial confrontation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Telling the patient her sympto","E":"Refusing to discuss test resul"}},{"id":"sdl31_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate somatic symptom disorder, illness anxiety disorder, conversion disorder, psychological factors affecting medical conditions, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a 9-month history of multiple somatic complaints (abdominal pain, palpitations, joint aches) with disproportionate time and energy devoted to worrying about their seriousness, and she is markedly anxious about her health, despite unremarkable workups. She has no history of feigned symptoms, no external incentive, and her symptoms are not confined to any single neurologic pattern. Which diagnosis best fits?","choices":{"A":"Illness anxiety disorder","B":"Somatic symptom disorder","C":"Conversion disorder","D":"Factitious disorder","E":"Malingering"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient meets the DSM-5 criteria for somatic symptom disorder: one or more somatic symptoms (here, multiple) causing significant distress, with excessive thoughts, feelings, or behaviors related to the symptoms (disproportionate worry, health-related anxiety, and excessive time/energy devoted to symptoms), persisting beyond 6 months, with no intentional production and no external incentive. Illness anxiety disorder is distinguished by the absence of significant physical symptoms, which this patient has (multiple somatic complaints), making somatic symptom disorder the better fit. Conversion disorder requires a specific neurologic-appearing motor or sensory symptom incompatible with known pathways, not this diffuse, multi-system somatic symptom pattern. Factitious disorder requires intentional feigning or production of symptoms, which is explicitly absent here. Malingering requires a conscious, recognizable external incentive, which is also explicitly absent here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing somatic symptom disorder (physical symptoms present, ≥6 months, health anxiety, no intentionality) from illness anxiety disorder (health anxiety without significant physical symptoms) is a classic vignette discrimination. COMLEX: This patient's presentation would also require ruling out hard-to-diagnose medical mimics (e.g., SLE, endocrinopathies) before finalizing the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — DSM-5 Diagnostic Logic: Syndrome, Duration, Impairment & Exclusion Criteria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate somatic symptom disorder, illness anxiety disorder, conversion disorder, psychological factors affecting medical conditions, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has had five admissions for episodes of lethargy with low blood glucose, each starting at home or during her mother's visits and never while she is observed alone by staff. During the latest episode her insulin level is high and C-peptide is low. Her mother, a former nursing assistant, is attentive and asks for more testing, and the father has no concerns. Which diagnosis should be recorded for the mother?","choices":{"A":"Factitious disorder imposed on self","B":"Psychological factors affecting other medical conditions","C":"Factitious disorder imposed on another","D":"Somatic symptom disorder","E":"Illness anxiety disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypoglycemia with high insulin and low C-peptide points to exogenous insulin, and episodes that occur only in the mother's presence suggest she is inducing the illness. Kaplan and Sadock define factitious disorder imposed on another as intentionally producing physical signs or symptoms in a person under one's care, most often a mother deceiving staff about her child, and state that the perpetrator receives the diagnosis, not the victim, so the mother is diagnosed. Factitious disorder imposed on self applies when a person falsifies or induces his or her own illness, which is not what the mother is doing. Psychological factors affecting other medical conditions requires a medical condition in the person diagnosed that is worsened by psychological factors; the mother has no such illness. Somatic symptom disorder involves excessive thoughts and worry about one's own genuine symptoms, and illness anxiety disorder involves fear of having a serious disease oneself; neither involves deliberately producing illness in someone else. Malingering would require an external incentive, such as money, which is absent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: insulin injection = high insulin with low C-peptide; sulfonylurea use = high insulin and high C-peptide. COMLEX: suspected factitious disorder imposed on another is child abuse, so protect the child, document carefully, and involve child protective services.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 5 — factitious disorder versus malingering by motivation and external incentive","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate somatic symptom disorder, illness anxiety disorder, conversion disorder, psychological factors affecting medical conditions, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman presents with sudden bilateral leg weakness after witnessing a traumatic car accident involving a family member. Examination reveals a positive Hoover sign, normal reflexes, no muscle atrophy, and normal electromyography. She appears remarkably unconcerned about her inability to walk. Which diagnosis best fits, and which associated feature is illustrated by her calm demeanor?","choices":{"A":"Conversion disorder, illustrating la belle indifference toward her weakness","B":"Somatic symptom disorder, illustrating excessive health-related anxiety and worry","C":"Factitious disorder, illustrating deliberate, intentional symptom fabrication","D":"Malingering, illustrating a goal-directed, incentive-driven presentation","E":"Illness anxiety disorder, illustrating persistent fear of undiagnosed serious disease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation matches conversion disorder: a neurologic-appearing symptom (leg weakness) incompatible with known pathways (positive Hoover sign, normal reflexes, no atrophy, normal EMG), preceded by a significant stressor (witnessing the accident), and her remarkably unconcerned demeanor illustrates la belle indifference, the inappropriately cavalier attitude toward severe symptoms often associated with (though not pathognomonic of) conversion disorder. Somatic symptom disorder is characterized by health anxiety and symptom preoccupation, which is inconsistent with this patient's described indifference and with the specific neurologic-incompatibility findings (Hoover sign) more characteristic of conversion disorder. Factitious disorder requires intentional fabrication, which is not indicated here; the presentation is described as an involuntary conversion reaction to a stressor. Malingering requires a conscious, goal-directed motivation, which is not described or supported here. Illness anxiety disorder is characterized by fear of undiagnosed disease without significant physical symptoms, not this acute, stressor-triggered neurologic-appearing weakness with a positive functional sign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: La belle indifference (inappropriately calm affect toward apparently severe symptoms) is classically associated with conversion disorder, though it is explicitly not pathognomonic and can occur in some genuinely ill, stoic medical patients. COMLEX: The positive Hoover sign, normal reflexes/EMG, and lack of atrophy together support a functional rather than true neurologic paralysis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — DSM-5 Diagnostic Logic: Syndrome, Duration, Impairment & Exclusion Criteria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic criteria using symptom burden, health anxiety, neurologic incompatibility, intentionality, external incentives, and functional impairment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has a single, severe somatic complaint (chronic pain) that consumes significant time, energy, and worry, meeting DSM-5 criteria for somatic symptom disorder. Per the DSM-5 severity specifiers, how would this presentation, involving only one but severe symptom, most likely be classified?","choices":{"A":"Mild, since the presentation involves a single symptom without any escalation for its severity level","B":"Moderate, since exactly 2 symptoms are required regardless of any one symptom's severity","C":"This presentation, involving a single severe symptom, cannot be classified under any specifier","D":"Severe, since severe can be met by 2 or more symptoms or by 1 symptom that is especially severe","E":"Persistent, since this course specifier reflects symptom duration, not symptom count or severity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Per DSM-5 severity specifiers for somatic symptom disorder, mild requires 1 symptom, moderate requires 2 or more symptoms, and severe requires 2 or more symptoms with multiple symptomatic complaints, OR 1 symptom that is especially severe — so a single, especially severe, life-consuming symptom like this patient's chronic pain can meet the severe specifier despite the symptom count being only one. Mild requires 1 symptom, but severity is not solely count-based; an especially severe single symptom escalates beyond mild to severe. Moderate does typically involve 2 or more symptoms, but this is not the best fit given the described severity and consuming nature of this single symptom. This presentation absolutely can be classified, specifically under the severe specifier via its single-especially-severe-symptom pathway. \"Persistent\" is a distinct DSM-5 course specifier defined by duration (more than 6 months of severe symptoms and impairment), not a severity specifier based on symptom count, so it does not correctly answer this severity-classification question.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The severe specifier for somatic symptom disorder can be met either by ≥2 symptoms with multiple complaints or by a single especially severe symptom — a nuanced but testable detail. COMLEX: Course specifiers (like \"persistent,\" defined by duration) are distinct from severity specifiers (mild/moderate/severe, defined by symptom count/severity) and should not be conflated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the disorder-differentiation framework into DSM-5's specific severity-grading criteria","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"This presentation absolutely c"}},{"id":"sdl31_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic criteria using symptom burden, health anxiety, neurologic incompatibility, intentionality, external incentives, and functional impairment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman presents with fear of having pancreatic cancer despite two negative imaging workups over the past year. She reports minimal bodily symptoms herself but is consumed by worry, checks online medical information daily, and has begun avoiding medical appointments out of fear of what might be found. Applying the diagnostic criteria for illness anxiety disorder, which finding best supports this diagnosis over somatic symptom disorder?","choices":{"A":"Prominent health anxiety with few or no physical symptoms","B":"The presence of multiple, severe physical symptoms across organ systems","C":"A confirmed external financial incentive tied to a pending disability claim","D":"Documented evidence that her physical findings were self-induced","E":"Fear that resolves completely after a single negative test"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Illness anxiety disorder is diagnosed when a patient believes they have a serious but undiagnosed disease despite evidence to the contrary, with minimal physical symptoms, addiction to internet health searches, and either excessive reassurance-seeking or (as in this case) avoidance of medical appointments out of fear — exactly matching this vignette, and this near-absence of significant physical symptoms combined with prominent health anxiety is the key criterion distinguishing it from somatic symptom disorder. The presence of multiple, severe physical symptoms would instead point toward somatic symptom disorder, not illness anxiety disorder, since this patient explicitly has minimal physical symptoms. A confirmed external financial incentive is not described and would instead raise consideration of malingering, not this diagnosis. Documented evidence of self-induced findings is not described and would instead raise consideration of factitious disorder. Illness anxiety disorder does not require that fear resolve completely after a single negative test; in fact, convictions typically persist despite negative results and reassurance, as seen with her two negative workups.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Illness anxiety disorder's core distinguishing feature — health anxiety and disease conviction without significant physical symptoms — combined with either appointment avoidance or excessive reassurance-seeking, is a classic exam pattern. COMLEX: Internet health-search behavior and inferring the worst from search results is a specifically described, testable clinical detail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the disorder-differentiation framework into DSM-5's specific diagnostic-criteria application","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic criteria using symptom burden, health anxiety, neurologic incompatibility, intentionality, external incentives, and functional impairment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man is being evaluated for possible conversion disorder after developing sudden arm weakness. Applying the diagnostic criteria, which specific finding is required to support this diagnosis over a true neurologic condition?","choices":{"A":"A confirmed absence of any psychological stressor in the months before onset","B":"A motor or sensory finding incompatible with recognized neurologic or medical conditions","C":"A confirmed external financial incentive, such as a pending disability claim","D":"Evidence that the patient intentionally produced the weakness to gain attention","E":"Symptoms that have persisted continuously for at least 6 months before evaluation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Conversion disorder (functional neurologic symptom disorder) requires the presence of one or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function that is clinically incompatible with recognized neurologic or medical conditions, distinguishing it from a true neurologic disease where findings do follow known anatomic and physiologic patterns. A confirmed absence of a preceding psychological stressor is not required; while conversion disorder is often preceded by stressors, this is a commonly associated feature rather than a strict, required diagnostic criterion in the way neurologic incompatibility is. A confirmed external financial incentive would instead point toward malingering, not conversion disorder. Evidence of intentional production would argue against conversion disorder (where symptoms are not intentionally produced) and toward factitious disorder or malingering instead. A 6-month duration requirement is the somatic symptom disorder criterion, not a conversion disorder diagnostic requirement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The core DSM-5 criterion for conversion disorder is neurologic/medical incompatibility of the motor or sensory finding — this is the diagnostic anchor, tested via bedside signs like the Hoover test or drop test. COMLEX: Unlike somatic symptom disorder, conversion disorder has no minimum duration requirement in DSM-5.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the disorder-differentiation framework into DSM-5's specific diagnostic-criteria application","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze medical, neurologic, endocrine, medication-induced, and substance-related conditions before assigning a somatic or factitious diagnosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman presents with fatigue, diffuse joint pain, and a facial rash, and is being evaluated for possible somatic symptom disorder given her preoccupation with these symptoms. Before finalizing this diagnosis, which specific hard-to-diagnose medical condition should be prioritized in the workup given her particular symptom pattern?","choices":{"A":"Systemic lupus erythematosus","B":"Multiple sclerosis","C":"Myasthenia gravis","D":"Primary hyperparathyroidism","E":"Occult pancreatic carcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatic symptom disorder must be differentiated from nonpsychiatric medical conditions that are not necessarily easily diagnosed, and systemic lupus erythematosus is specifically named among these (alongside AIDS, endocrinopathies, myasthenia gravis, multiple sclerosis, and occult neoplastic disorders); this patient's fatigue, joint pain, and facial rash are classic SLE features that should prioritize this specific medical exclusion. Multiple sclerosis is also a named mimic, but it presents with relapsing focal neurologic deficits such as optic neuritis or sensory loss rather than arthralgia and a rash. Myasthenia gravis, another named mimic, causes fatigable weakness with ptosis and diplopia, not joint pain or a facial rash. Primary hyperparathyroidism, an endocrinopathy, can cause fatigue and bone pain but not a facial rash. Occult pancreatic carcinoma presents with weight loss, fatigue, and vague abdominal or back pain rather than arthritis and a rash.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE's classic triad-adjacent presentation (fatigue, joint pain, malar rash) should specifically raise concern for missed autoimmune disease before attributing symptoms to somatic symptom disorder — a named exclusion condition. COMLEX: The other named hard-to-diagnose mimics (AIDS, endocrinopathies, myasthenia gravis, MS, occult neoplasm) each have their own classic symptom clusters worth recognizing in a similar vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the disorder-differentiation framework into the specific medical/neurologic exclusion workup","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze medical, neurologic, endocrine, medication-induced, and substance-related conditions before assigning a somatic or factitious diagnosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman presents with progressive muscle weakness and easy fatigability that worsens throughout the day and with repeated exertion, initially attributed to somatic symptom disorder after a normal basic workup. Which specific hard-to-diagnose neuromuscular condition should be prioritized in her exclusion workup reflecting this fatigability pattern?","choices":{"A":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","B":"Guillain-Barré syndrome","C":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","D":"Inclusion body myositis","E":"Myasthenia gravis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Myasthenia gravis is specifically named among the hard-to-diagnose medical conditions that must be excluded before diagnosing somatic symptom disorder, and this patient's classic fatigability pattern (weakness worsening with exertion throughout the day) is the hallmark presentation of myasthenia gravis, making it the condition to prioritize. Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome also causes proximal weakness, but strength transiently improves with repeated effort and it is accompanied by autonomic symptoms and hyporeflexia, the opposite of this worsening-with-use pattern. Guillain-Barré syndrome is an acute ascending polyneuropathy with areflexia, not day-to-day fatigable weakness. Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis produces progressive weakness with mixed upper- and lower-motor-neuron signs (fasciculations, atrophy, hyperreflexia) that does not fluctuate with exertion. Inclusion body myositis is a slowly progressive myopathy of older adults affecting the finger flexors and quadriceps, not fatigable weakness in a young woman.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myasthenia gravis's classic fatigability pattern (weakness worsening with repeated use/throughout the day) is a distinctive, specifically named exclusion diagnosis before labeling a presentation as somatic symptom disorder. COMLEX: Formal testing for suspected myasthenia gravis includes repetitive nerve stimulation studies and acetylcholine receptor antibody testing, distinct from the neurologic workup described for conversion disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the disorder-differentiation framework into the specific medical/neurologic exclusion workup","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze medical, neurologic, endocrine, medication-induced, and substance-related conditions before assigning a somatic or factitious diagnosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with well-documented alcohol use disorder presents with new-onset confusion and irritability, initially raising concern for a primary psychiatric mood disorder. Before assigning a primary psychiatric diagnosis, which category of factors should be systematically analyzed and excluded first, considering his background?","choices":{"A":"His childhood family history of psychiatric illness","B":"Substance intoxication or withdrawal as a cause","C":"His current employment status and recent job stress","D":"His marital relationship history and recent conflicts","E":"His educational background and baseline literacy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Before assigning a somatic or psychiatric diagnosis, medical, neurologic, endocrine, medication-induced, and substance-related conditions must be analyzed and excluded, and given this patient's documented alcohol use disorder, substance intoxication or withdrawal (e.g., alcohol withdrawal, which classically presents with confusion and irritability) must be systematically ruled out before attributing his presentation to a primary psychiatric mood disorder. Childhood family history alone, without addressing his substance use history, would miss this specific and clinically urgent exclusion. Current employment status alone is not the described exclusion category relevant to his presentation. Marital relationship history alone is not the described exclusion category. Educational background alone is not the described exclusion category relevant to this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Substance intoxication and withdrawal must always be systematically excluded before a primary psychiatric diagnosis is assigned, especially in a patient with a known substance use history — a foundational \"medical clearance\" principle. COMLEX: This same principle underlies the broader primary-versus-secondary psychiatric differentiation taught throughout the MSE and DSM-5 diagnostic framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the disorder-differentiation framework into the specific medical/substance exclusion workup","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret examination and laboratory findings to distinguish true neurologic disease from a functional neurologic symptom disorder.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman presents with apparent inability to walk. When asked to walk, she displays a wildly ataxic, staggering gait with gross, irregular, jerky truncal movements and thrashing arm movements, but when distracted with a request to dance, her movements become notably more coordinated. She has never actually fallen during these episodes. Which examination finding pattern is this, and what does it suggest?","choices":{"A":"Astasia-abasia, suggesting a functional gait disorder","B":"A true cerebellar ataxia from a posterior fossa lesion","C":"A normal gait variant within the age-appropriate range","D":"Malingering, since a nonorganic gait implies deception","E":"A parkinsonian gait with a shuffling, festinating stride"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Astasia-abasia is a wildly ataxic, staggering gait accompanied by gross, irregular, jerky truncal movements and thrashing arm movements, seen in conversion disorder; patients with this symptom rarely fall, and if they do, they are generally not injured, and with suggestion, those who cannot walk may still be able to dance, exactly as described, supporting a functional rather than a true neurologic gait disorder. A true cerebellar ataxia from an organic lesion would be expected to produce a consistent, non-suggestible gait disturbance without this dramatic improvement when distracted by dancing, and would carry real fall risk. This is not a normal, non-diagnostic gait pattern; it is a specifically named, diagnostically significant functional finding. This finding alone cannot definitively distinguish conversion disorder from malingering, since both are nonorganic in origin; it only indicates a functional rather than a true neurologic process, and no intentional/goal-directed feature is described here. A classic parkinsonian gait is characterized by shuffling, festination, and reduced arm swing, not this ataxic, thrashing, distractible pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Astasia-abasia (wildly ataxic, non-injurious gait that improves with distraction, such as dancing) is a classic functional gait finding for conversion disorder. COMLEX: The preserved ability to dance despite reported inability to walk is a specific, testable \"alteration with suggestion\" detail distinguishing functional from true neurologic gait disorders.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — True vs. Functional/Organic Differentiation of Perceptual & Examination Findings","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"This is not a normal, non-diag"}},{"id":"sdl31_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret examination and laboratory findings to distinguish true neurologic disease from a functional neurologic symptom disorder.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man reports profound monocular blindness in his right eye following a stressful event. On examination, the physician swings a penlight rhythmically between his two eyes: with each swing, both pupils constrict symmetrically and equally, with no lag, dilation, or asymmetry in either pupil's response as the light moves from the unaffected to the \"blind\" eye. What does this finding suggest about the reported blindness?","choices":{"A":"This finding confirms a true optic nerve lesion as the cause of his monocular blindness","B":"This finding is expected in both true and functional blindness to the same degree","C":"This finding confirms cortical blindness from an occipital lesion","D":"The absent pupillary defect argues against optic nerve disease and favors a functional cause","E":"This finding is diagnostic of early open-angle glaucoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The swinging flashlight test (Marcus Gunn test) evaluates for a relative afferent pupillary defect, and in evaluating profound monocular blindness, the absence of a relative afferent pupillary defect (which would be expected with a true, significant optic nerve lesion causing profound vision loss in one eye) argues against an organic cause and supports a functional (conversion) etiology instead. This finding does not confirm a true optic nerve lesion; a genuine optic nerve lesion causing profound monocular blindness would typically produce a relative afferent pupillary defect, which is specifically absent here. This is not a nondiagnostic, equally-expected finding in both true and functional blindness; its presence or absence specifically helps distinguish the two. This finding does not confirm cortical blindness from an occipital lesion; cortical (bilateral, post-chiasmal) blindness would not typically produce a relative afferent pupillary defect either, but this patient's reported blindness is monocular, and the described finding pattern in this framework is used to support a functional rather than a cortical process. This is not a finding diagnostic of glaucoma, an unrelated optic neuropathy with its own distinct examination findings (elevated intraocular pressure, optic disc cupping).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Absence of a relative afferent pupillary defect on swinging flashlight testing, in a patient reporting profound monocular blindness, is a classic functional-vision-loss finding. COMLEX: For reported bilateral blindness, binocular visual field testing that shows sufficient vision in the \"bad eye\" to preclude normal physiologic blind-spot plotting in the \"good eye\" is another described functional finding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — True vs. Functional/Organic Differentiation of Perceptual & Examination Findings","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"This finding does not confirm a true opt"}},{"id":"sdl31_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret examination and laboratory findings to distinguish true neurologic disease from a functional neurologic symptom disorder.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman is being evaluated for reported paralysis of her right arm. On examination, the examiner checks her motor strength directly and notes an inconsistent pattern in which her resistance intermittently gives way and then returns to near-full strength within the same testing maneuver. What is this examination finding called, and what does it suggest?","choices":{"A":"Spasticity, suggesting an upper motor neuron lesion","B":"Flaccidity, suggesting a lower motor neuron lesion","C":"Give-away weakness, suggesting a functional paralysis","D":"A normal strength finding with no diagnostic significance","E":"Fasciculations, suggesting lower motor neuron degeneration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Give-away weakness, an inconsistent motor strength pattern found by checking motor strength directly and noting the resistance intermittently give way rather than remaining smoothly and consistently weak, is a described examination finding suggesting functional rather than true neurologic paralysis or paresis. Spasticity is a distinct finding (increased tone with a \"clasp-knife\" quality) associated with true upper motor neuron lesions, not the inconsistent give-way pattern described here. Flaccidity is a distinct finding (decreased tone) associated with true lower motor neuron lesions or acute spinal shock, not this inconsistent strength-testing pattern. This is not a normal, nondiagnostic finding; it is specifically described as a functional-weakness indicator. Fasciculations are visible muscle twitches associated with active lower motor neuron degeneration (e.g., ALS), an entirely different examination finding not described in this vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Give-away weakness (inconsistent effort during formal strength testing) is a classic bedside sign of functional (nonorganic) weakness, distinguishable from the smooth, reproducible weakness of a true neurologic lesion. COMLEX: Reflexes typically remain normal in conversion paralysis, and there is no muscle atrophy or fasciculations except after long-standing conversion paralysis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — True vs. Functional/Organic Differentiation of Perceptual & Examination Findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare factitious disorder and malingering using motivation, sick role, external incentives, and clinical context.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with a chaotic lifestyle, history of disturbed interpersonal relationships, identity disturbance, and self-damaging acts is found to have surreptitiously injected herself with insulin to induce hypoglycemic episodes, pursuing repeated hospital admissions with no outside reward beyond medical attention itself. Which cluster B diagnosis warrants consideration alongside this presentation, and why does her reason for wanting care point to one explanation rather than the other?","choices":{"A":"Antisocial personality disorder, with her motivation best explained as evading legal consequences","B":"Histrionic personality disorder, with her motivation best explained as seeking financial compensation","C":"No personality disorder, since factitious disorder rarely co-occurs with personality pathology","D":"Narcissistic personality disorder, with her motivation best explained as obtaining prescription drugs","E":"Borderline personality disorder, with her wish for the sick role pointing to factitious disorder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A chaotic lifestyle, disturbed interpersonal relationships, identity disturbance, and self-damaging acts should lead clinicians to consider borderline personality disorder in patients with factitious disorder, and despite this personality overlap, her described motivation, seeking repeated hospitalization and medical attention with no apparent external incentive, still identifies factitious disorder (sick-role-seeking) rather than malingering (which requires a concrete, recognizable external goal). Antisocial personality disorder is also part of the differential given pathologic lying and manipulative behavior, but antisocial individuals typically do not volunteer for invasive procedures or resort to long-term hospitalization the way this patient does, and her motivation described here is sick-role-seeking, not goal-directed evasion of legal consequences, which is not mentioned in this vignette. Histrionic personality disorder is also in the differential given attention-seeking, but nothing in the vignette suggests financial compensation, which would point to malingering. Narcissistic personality disorder is not the cluster B pattern described, and obtaining prescription drugs is an external incentive the vignette excludes. Factitious disorder does not always occur independent of personality pathology; borderline, antisocial, and histrionic personality disorders are all specifically described as commonly considered in this differential. Presuming her motivation is financial gain contradicts the vignette, which explicitly describes no external incentive beyond medical attention itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Borderline, antisocial, and histrionic personality disorders are all specifically described as part of the differential/comorbidity picture in factitious disorder, but personality overlap does not change the core motivation-based distinction from malingering. COMLEX: Insulin-induced hypoglycemia and other surreptitious self-harm are described examples of how factitious disorder patients may inflict painful or life-threatening injuries on themselves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — Diagnostic Features Distinguishing Specific Personality Disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare factitious disorder and malingering using motivation, sick role, external incentives, and clinical context.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man involved in a workplace injury lawsuit reports severe, debilitating back pain that prevents him from working. Video surveillance obtained during the litigation shows him engaging in physically demanding activity without apparent difficulty when he believes he is not being observed. Once the lawsuit settles, his reported symptoms resolve entirely. Which diagnosis best fits, and what clinical context most directly supports it?","choices":{"A":"Factitious disorder, supported by his desire to assume the sick role","B":"Conversion disorder, supported by an apparent neurologic incompatibility","C":"Malingering, supported by the lawsuit and symptom resolution once it ended","D":"Somatic symptom disorder, supported by his excessive health-related anxiety","E":"Psychological factors affecting a genuine but unconfirmed back injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation is a classic malingering pattern: a clear, manifest external goal (financial compensation from the lawsuit), inconsistent behavior when unobserved (functioning normally on surveillance), and resolution of symptoms once the external incentive is no longer relevant (after the lawsuit settles), exactly matching the described pattern of malingerers producing symptoms for a recognizable environmental goal and stopping when no longer useful. Factitious disorder is specifically motivated by assuming the sick role itself with no external incentive, which directly contradicts this vignette's clear, concrete financial/legal incentive. Conversion disorder requires an involuntary, non-goal-directed neurologic-appearing symptom incompatible with known pathways, not this observed, inconsistent, incentive-linked pattern with normal function when unobserved. Somatic symptom disorder centers on genuine health anxiety and symptom preoccupation, not this externally incentivized, inconsistent presentation that resolves precisely when the incentive disappears. Psychological factors affecting other medical conditions requires a genuine underlying medical condition being psychologically worsened, which is not supported here given the described normal function on surveillance and full resolution tied to the legal outcome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A clear external incentive, inconsistent behavior when unobserved, and symptom resolution once the incentive disappears together form the classic malingering triad — one of the most frequently tested vignette patterns in this differential. COMLEX: This same pattern (goal-directed, stoppable symptoms) is the core feature separating malingering from every other diagnosis in the somatic symptom differential.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — Diagnostic Features Distinguishing Specific Personality Disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare factitious disorder and malingering using motivation, sick role, external incentives, and clinical context.","batch":2,"stem":"A hospitalized patient with an extensive history of self-induced hypoglycemic episodes across multiple different hospital systems is confronted by the care team. She becomes evasive, denies any wrongdoing despite laboratory evidence of exogenous insulin use, and abruptly leaves the hospital against medical advice shortly afterward. Which clinical context is most characteristic of factitious disorder as described in this scenario, distinguishing the overall clinical course from malingering?","choices":{"A":"The clear achievement of a specific financial settlement immediately before leaving, a goal-directed pattern","B":"A documented request for early release from a criminal sentence, reflecting a recognizable external goal","C":"An explicit demand for compensation from the hospital before departure, reflecting a recognizable external goal","D":"Abruptly eluding meaningful therapy by leaving care, with no recognizable external goal achieved","E":"A scheduled return to gainful employment immediately after discharge, arguing strongly against any work-avoidance motive"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patients with factitious disorder ultimately elude meaningful therapy by abruptly leaving the hospital or failing to keep follow-up appointments, a clinical paradox in which they simulate severe illness and submit to unnecessary treatment while denying their actual (factitious) illness to themselves and others, exactly matching this patient's evasive denial and abrupt departure without any external goal achieved. A clear financial settlement achieved immediately before leaving would instead be characteristic of malingering's goal-directed pattern, not factitious disorder's sick-role-seeking, therapy-eluding pattern described here. A documented request for early release from a criminal sentence would similarly point toward malingering's recognizable external goal, not this scenario. An explicit demand for compensation before departure would also point toward malingering rather than the described factitious disorder pattern. A scheduled return to gainful employment immediately after discharge is not the described pattern; it would actually argue against malingering motivated by work avoidance, and is not the characteristic factitious disorder feature illustrated here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The \"eluding meaningful therapy\" pattern (abrupt departure or missed follow-up, without any external goal achieved) is a specifically described hallmark of factitious disorder's clinical course, distinct from malingering's goal-achievement-then-stop pattern. COMLEX: Confrontation in factitious disorder can provoke evasion or flight, which is why an empathic, nonconfrontational approach is specifically recommended when addressing suspected cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — Diagnostic Features Distinguishing Specific Personality Disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply communication strategies that validate symptoms while avoiding unnecessary testing, iatrogenic harm, stigma, and adversarial confrontation.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician is seeing a new patient with a thick chart documenting years of somatic complaints and multiple prior negative workups from other providers. Before this first visit, the physician reviews the chart and forms a strong prior impression that the patient's symptoms are entirely psychological. Which approach best balances thorough care with avoiding the communication pitfalls described for this patient population?","choices":{"A":"Relying on the prior negative workups and skipping a new physical examination","B":"Telling the patient her symptoms are psychological based on the chart review without an exam","C":"Keeping an open mind and doing an independent assessment with a fresh physical exam","D":"Ordering a full battery of repeat invasive tests at the first visit to reassure her","E":"Declining to review the prior chart before the visit begins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The prospect of reviewing \"thick\" charts full of redundant tests and procedures often leads to a negative attitude from the physician, but the physician should keep an open mind despite forewarnings in the medical records and perform an independent assessment of the patient, including a fresh physical and neurologic examination at the initial visit, while avoiding an emphasis on psychological questioning and interpretations at this early stage. Relying entirely on prior negative workups and skipping any new physical examination fails the described need for an independent assessment and thorough initial exam. Immediately telling the patient her symptoms are known to be psychological based on chart review alone reflects exactly the premature, dismissive judgment the framework warns against, and risks being perceived as invalidating. Ordering an extensive battery of repeat invasive tests regardless of prior negative workups is not the recommended approach either, since unnecessary testing and iatrogenic harm should be avoided; the recommended approach is a reasonable independent assessment, not indiscriminate repeat invasive testing. Declining to review the prior chart at all is not the recommended approach; reviewing the chart is expected, but it should not replace an independent, open-minded assessment or bias the physician's stance toward the patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The recommended balance is an open-minded, independent initial assessment, including a fresh physical exam, without either dismissing the patient based on chart history or ordering indiscriminate repeat invasive testing. COMLEX: Briefer physical assessments should follow at subsequent visits as the therapeutic relationship continues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 2 — the same empathy-versus-identification, boundary-preserving clinical stance, applied here to validating symptoms without adversarial confrontation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply communication strategies that validate symptoms while avoiding unnecessary testing, iatrogenic harm, stigma, and adversarial confrontation.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with somatic symptom disorder becomes visibly upset when her physician offers what she perceives as quick, dismissive reassurance early in the very first visit. Which principle explains why this approach backfired, and what should the physician do differently going forward?","choices":{"A":"Reassurance should not be offered at any point in treatment, even once meaningful trust has been established","B":"The physician should have instead immediately explained the underlying psychological conflict driving her symptoms","C":"The physician should have instead firmly told her that her symptoms are not real, an invalidating approach","D":"The physician's approach was correct, and the patient's reaction reflects a diagnosis-inconsistent overreaction","E":"Premature reassurance can feel dismissive; the physician should prioritize active, receptive listening"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The patient may perceive premature reassurance as disinterest or dismissiveness, and the recommended approach instead emphasizes active, receptive listening, tolerance for repetition, and respect for the validity of the patient's symptoms as the foundation for building a trusting alliance, particularly early in the relationship. Reassurance is not banned entirely from treatment; later, once trust is established, reassurance about normal test results can be helpful (as described for conversion disorder), but premature reassurance early on risks feeling dismissive. Explaining the underlying psychological conflict immediately is explicitly described as not working well early in treatment. Firmly telling the patient her symptoms are not real is an invalidating, confrontational approach explicitly cautioned against. The patient's reaction is consistent with, not inconsistent with, the described risk of premature reassurance being perceived as dismissive, so the physician's early approach, not the patient's reaction, is what the framework identifies as the problem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Premature reassurance, though well-intentioned, can be perceived as dismissive by somatic symptom disorder patients — active, receptive listening should come first, especially early in the relationship. COMLEX: This principle directly extends the broader \"neutral approach\" (avoiding dismissive, confrontational, or overly reassuring communication) recommended for this patient population.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 2 — the same empathy-versus-identification, boundary-preserving clinical stance, applied here to validating symptoms without adversarial confrontation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply communication strategies that validate symptoms while avoiding unnecessary testing, iatrogenic harm, stigma, and adversarial confrontation.","batch":2,"stem":"A care team overseeing a patient with suspected factitious disorder is experiencing significant internal tension, with some staff members feeling sympathetic and protective of the patient even as others feel angry and want to discharge her immediately. Which specific approach addresses this team dynamic?","choices":{"A":"Assigning a single staff member to handle all contact with the patient going forward","B":"Regular team meetings to reduce staff splitting and address countertransference","C":"Instructing staff to avoid discussing the case with one another","D":"Immediately transferring the patient to a different facility to reset the team","E":"Allowing the disagreement among staff to resolve on its own"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Among the guidelines for management and treatment of factitious disorder, arranging regular interdisciplinary meetings to reduce conflict and splitting among staff, while managing staff countertransference, is specifically recommended to address exactly this kind of team dynamic (some staff sympathetic/protective, others angry/wanting to discharge), which reflects staff splitting in response to the patient's presentation. Assigning a single staff member exclusively to avoid team discussion does not address the described splitting dynamic and removes the collaborative, interdisciplinary communication the guidelines recommend. Instructing staff to avoid discussing the case with each other entirely would worsen, not reduce, the fragmentation and inconsistency underlying staff splitting. Immediately transferring the patient to a different facility does not address the underlying team dynamic and is not a described management guideline for this scenario. Allowing the disagreement to resolve itself without structured intervention contradicts the specific recommendation for regular interdisciplinary meetings to actively manage this dynamic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Staff splitting (sympathetic/protective versus angry/rejecting reactions) is a recognized team dynamic in factitious disorder care, specifically managed through regular interdisciplinary meetings and countertransference awareness. COMLEX: This same splitting concept parallels the staff-splitting dynamic described in borderline personality disorder inpatient care, both addressed through team-based consistency and communication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 2 — the same empathy-versus-identification, boundary-preserving clinical stance, applied here to validating symptoms without adversarial confrontation","isHighYield":true}],"32":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for somatic symptom and related disorders using scheduled follow-up, functional goals, therapeutic alliance, and avoidance of excessive testing.","batch":1,"stem":"A physician is establishing a long-term treatment plan for a patient with somatic symptom disorder. What should the initial therapeutic goals be, per recommended treatment principles?","choices":{"A":"Complete elimination of all somatic complaints, achieved within the first month","B":"Immediate discontinuation of all future appointments, ending ongoing follow-up","C":"Referral to as many specialists as possible, for a comprehensive simultaneous workup","D":"A modest, attainable standard geared toward gradual progress","E":"A guarantee that no somatic symptom will ever recur, framed as a formal promise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Therapeutic goals for somatic symptom disorder should be modest at first and limited to small, attainable gains such as a decrease in medical visits, a commitment to a single primary care physician, and the avoidance of unnecessary tests and procedures, with the physician preparing for a long-term commitment to the patient. Complete elimination of all somatic complaints within the first month sets an unrealistic, non-modest goal inconsistent with the described approach. Immediate discontinuation of all future medical appointments entirely is not the recommended approach; a decrease in visits, not complete cessation, is the goal. Referral to as many specialists as possible directly contradicts the recommendation to limit specialist consultations unless there is evidence of comorbid physical conditions. A guarantee of zero future symptoms is an unrealistic goal inconsistent with the modest, attainable-gains framework.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Somatic symptom disorder treatment goals are specifically modest and attainable (fewer visits, one PCP, less unnecessary testing) rather than symptom elimination — a key testable treatment-philosophy point. COMLEX: Physicians should also limit specialist consultations unless comorbid physical conditions are evident.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 6 — extends the neutral, nonconfrontational communication framework into concrete scheduled-follow-up and functional-goal treatment principles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A guarantee of zero future sym"}},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for somatic symptom and related disorders using scheduled follow-up, functional goals, therapeutic alliance, and avoidance of excessive testing.","batch":1,"stem":"A \"consultation letter\" intervention for primary care physicians managing patients with multiple medically unexplained symptoms has demonstrated which effect?","choices":{"A":"Improved functional capacity and decreased health service utilization","B":"Increased frequency of hospitalization and unnecessary invasive procedures","C":"No measurable effect on either function or health service use","D":"Worsened patient satisfaction and a marked increase in medical visits","E":"Increased physician use of dismissive phrases like \"it is all in your head\""},"correct":"A","explanation":"The use of a brief consultation letter intended for primary care physicians has demonstrated effectiveness in improving patients' functional capacity and decreasing their utilization of health services, by urging physicians to see patients during regularly scheduled appointments, perform brief focused exams, avoid unnecessary procedures, and allow patients to discuss stressors. Increased frequency of hospitalization and invasive procedures is the opposite of this letter's described effect, which is to avoid unnecessary diagnostic procedures, invasive treatments, and hospitalizations. This intervention has a measurable, demonstrated effect, not no effect at all. Worsened patient satisfaction and increased medical visits contradicts the described improvement in functional capacity and decreased health service utilization. The consultation letter specifically instructs physicians to avoid using statements like \"symptoms are all in your head,\" not increase their use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The primary care \"consultation letter\" intervention is a well-studied, low-cost management technique shown to improve function and reduce unnecessary healthcare utilization in somatic symptom and related disorders. COMLEX: The letter specifically instructs scheduled (not as-needed) appointments, brief focused exams, and avoiding invalidating language.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 6 — extends the neutral, nonconfrontational communication framework into concrete scheduled-follow-up and functional-goal treatment principles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Worsened patient satisfaction "}},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for somatic symptom and related disorders using scheduled follow-up, functional goals, therapeutic alliance, and avoidance of excessive testing.","batch":1,"stem":"Which scheduling approach for follow-up visits is recommended for patients with somatic symptom and related disorders, rather than allowing the patient to seek care only when symptoms flare?","choices":{"A":"Appointments made whenever the patient calls in with a newly reported symptom","B":"A single initial appointment with no planned follow-up","C":"Regularly scheduled appointments at fixed intervals","D":"Appointments scheduled through emergency department visits","E":"Appointments scheduled after a new specialist referral is completed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The consultation letter approach specifically urges physicians to see these patients during regularly scheduled appointments rather than only in response to new symptom flares, since regularly scheduled visits (paired with brief, focused exams) reduce the reinforcing link between symptom reporting and receiving medical attention. Appointments only when the patient calls with a new symptom is exactly the as-needed, symptom-triggered pattern the recommended approach seeks to avoid. A single appointment with no planned follow-up contradicts the described need for a long-term, ongoing commitment to the patient. Appointments scheduled exclusively through the emergency department is not the recommended primary care-based approach. Appointments scheduled only after a new specialist referral contradicts the recommendation to limit unnecessary specialist consultations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Regularly scheduled (not symptom-triggered) follow-up visits are a core, specifically recommended treatment principle for somatic symptom and related disorders, reducing the reinforcing link between new complaints and medical attention. COMLEX: This scheduling principle pairs directly with brief, focused physical exams at each visit rather than comprehensive re-workups.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 6 — extends the neutral, nonconfrontational communication framework into concrete scheduled-follow-up and functional-goal treatment principles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate appropriate reassurance, CBT, physical rehabilitation, psychiatric referral, and treatment of comorbid anxiety or depression by clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"Per the reviewed randomized controlled trial evidence, which treatment modality has shown the highest efficacy for somatoform disorders overall?","choices":{"A":"Antidepressant monotherapy","B":"Electroconvulsive therapy","C":"Sodium amytal interview","D":"Inpatient hospitalization","E":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Reviews of controlled studies (34 randomized controlled trials including 13 CBT trials, 5 antidepressant trials, and 16 other-modality trials) found that CBT showed the highest efficacy, with virtually all reported studies showing a significant effect of CBT on symptoms compared to no treatment. Antidepressant pharmacotherapy alone showed efficacy specifically in painful syndromes (with older tricyclics favored over newer SSRIs), but did not show the highest overall efficacy compared to CBT. Electroconvulsive therapy is not a described first-line or evidence-supported treatment for somatoform disorders in this framework. Sodium amytal interview alone is listed only as one component of intensive treatment once chronicity has developed in conversion disorder, not as the highest-efficacy modality overall. Hospitalization alone is not the highest-efficacy modality described; it is one component of intensive, multimodal treatment for chronic conversion disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CBT has the strongest RCT evidence base among treatments for somatoform disorders, outperforming antidepressants and other modalities in reviewed trials. COMLEX: Antidepressant efficacy specifically applies to painful syndromes, with older tricyclics favored over newer SSRIs in the reviewed data.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the six-disorder differentiation framework into matching each disorder to its appropriate treatment modality","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Antidepressant pharmacotherapy"}},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate appropriate reassurance, CBT, physical rehabilitation, psychiatric referral, and treatment of comorbid anxiety or depression by clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which treatment is described as the leading, go-to therapy specifically for illness anxiety disorder?","choices":{"A":"Fluoxetine monotherapy","B":"Physical therapy and biofeedback","C":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy","D":"Sodium amytal interview","E":"Inpatient hospitalization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Several controlled trials have demonstrated efficacy for CBT interventions in health anxiety, making CBT the prototype, first-line treatment for illness anxiety disorder, while other therapies (mindfulness, exposure therapy, acceptance and commitment therapy) have also shown some efficacy and pharmacologic data remain more limited. Fluoxetine has demonstrated efficacy in a randomized controlled trial for hypochondriasis, but it is not described as the preferred first-line treatment; CBT holds that specific designation, and adding CBT to fluoxetine conferred additional benefit. Physical therapy and biofeedback are not the first-line approach for illness anxiety disorder; these are more associated with conversion disorder's chronic-phase management. Sodium amytal interview is a conversion disorder chronic-phase treatment element, not the first-line illness anxiety disorder treatment. Inpatient hospitalization is not the described first-line approach for illness anxiety disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CBT is specifically named the prototype, first-line treatment for illness anxiety disorder based on controlled trial evidence — a frequently tested designation. COMLEX: Limited pharmacologic data exist, though a randomized trial demonstrated fluoxetine's efficacy and safety, with added CBT conferring a slight extra benefit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the six-disorder differentiation framework into matching each disorder to its appropriate treatment modality","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate appropriate reassurance, CBT, physical rehabilitation, psychiatric referral, and treatment of comorbid anxiety or depression by clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with somatic symptom disorder also has a well-defined, distinct major depressive episode. Which principle should guide the use of medication in this specific case?","choices":{"A":"Medication should be avoided entirely, even for clearly delineated depressive symptoms","B":"Medication should be reserved for clearly delineated comorbid depressive symptoms","C":"A pharmacologic approach targeting the somatic symptoms directly should be sufficient","D":"Medication should be first-line monotherapy for the somatic symptoms themselves","E":"Electroconvulsive therapy should be attempted first, given this comorbidity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In general, medications should be avoided when treating patients with somatic symptom disorder except in the presence of clearly delineated anxious, depressive, or psychotic symptoms, which directly applies to this patient's clearly delineated comorbid major depressive disorder as an appropriate indication for pharmacotherapy. Avoiding medication entirely, even for a clearly delineated comorbid depression, contradicts the specific exception described for comorbid anxious, depressive, or psychotic symptoms. A purely pharmacologic approach targeting the somatic symptoms themselves is explicitly described as insufficient, since there is no evidence that a purely pharmacologic approach targeting somatic symptom disorder itself will be sufficient. Medication as first-line monotherapy for the somatic symptoms regardless of comorbidity contradicts the described general avoidance of medication except for comorbid conditions. Electroconvulsive therapy is not the described first treatment for this comorbidity; it is not the standard approach for either somatic symptom disorder or straightforward comorbid depression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medications in somatic symptom disorder are reserved specifically for clearly delineated comorbid anxiety, depression, or psychosis — not for the somatic symptoms themselves. COMLEX: Despite newer agents (dual reuptake inhibitors, gabapentin), no evidence supports a purely pharmacologic approach to somatic symptom disorder itself.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the six-disorder differentiation framework into matching each disorder to its appropriate treatment modality","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Avoiding medication entirely, "}},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze factors that perpetuate somatic preoccupation, including reinforcement, trauma, health anxiety, medical uncertainty, and clinician response.","batch":1,"stem":"One proposed model explains somatic symptom disorder as a request for admission to the sick role by a person facing seemingly insurmountable problems, since the sick role offers an escape from unwelcome duties. What is this model called?","choices":{"A":"The somatic amplification model","B":"The neuropsychologic deficit model","C":"The social learning model","D":"The psychoanalytic compromise-formation model","E":"The biologic family-inheritance model"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The social learning model proposes that symptoms of somatic symptom disorder represent a request for admission to the sick role made by a person facing seemingly insurmountable and unsolvable problems, since the sick role offers an escape allowing the patient to avoid noxious obligations, postpone unwelcome challenges, and evade usual duties. The somatic amplification model instead describes patients augmenting and amplifying somatic sensations due to low thresholds for physical discomfort and faulty cognitive schemes, a distinct proposed mechanism. The neuropsychologic deficit model describes an inherent deficit in certain brain functions and is specifically discussed in relation to conversion disorder's biologic factors, not this sick-role reinforcement model. The psychoanalytic compromise-formation model describes conversion symptoms as achieving primary gain through partial, unconscious conflict expression, a distinct model from the social learning/sick-role framework. The biologic family-inheritance model refers to early family studies of hysteria/Briquet syndrome and antisocial personality in male relatives, a distinct etiologic thread, not the sick-role reinforcement mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The social learning/sick-role model explains how somatic preoccupation can be reinforced by the functional escape the sick role provides from unwelcome obligations — a key perpetuating-factor concept. COMLEX: This same social learning model has also been proposed to apply similarly to illness anxiety disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 4 — extends the social-determinants/trauma-exposure modifier framework to the specific factors that perpetuate somatic preoccupation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze factors that perpetuate somatic preoccupation, including reinforcement, trauma, health anxiety, medical uncertainty, and clinician response.","batch":1,"stem":"Which proposed mechanism suggests that patients with somatic symptom disorder have a low threshold for and low tolerance of physical discomfort, causing them to interpret ordinary sensations (such as abdominal pressure) as pain?","choices":{"A":"Somatic amplification of bodily sensations due to a faulty cognitive scheme","B":"The social learning/sick-role model, centered on escaping unwelcome obligations","C":"Childhood sexual or physical trauma exposure, proposed in some but not all studies","D":"A confirmed structural brain lesion localized to the dominant hemisphere","E":"Secondary gain achieved through control over close interpersonal relationships"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some have postulated that persons with somatic symptom disorder augment and amplify their somatic sensations, having low thresholds for and low tolerance of physical discomfort, so that what most people perceive as abdominal pressure is instead experienced as pain, reflecting a faulty cognitive scheme in how bodily sensations are interpreted. The social learning/sick-role reinforcement model is a distinct proposed mechanism centered on escaping unwelcome obligations, not sensory threshold/amplification. Childhood sexual or physical trauma exposure has been proposed as a risk factor specifically for conversion disorder in some (though not all) studies, not as the somatic-amplification mechanism described here. A confirmed structural brain lesion in the dominant hemisphere is part of the neuropsychologic conceptualization discussed for conversion disorder, not this cognitive-amplification mechanism. Secondary gain from interpersonal control is part of the behavioral theory of conversion disorder, a distinct proposed mechanism from somatic amplification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The somatic amplification model (low sensory threshold, faulty cognitive interpretation of bodily sensations) is a distinct etiologic thread from the social learning/sick-role model — both apply to somatic symptom disorder but describe different mechanisms. COMLEX: This amplification concept helps explain why patients with somatic symptom disorder experience ordinary sensations as alarming or painful.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 4 — extends the social-determinants/trauma-exposure modifier framework to the specific factors that perpetuate somatic preoccupation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze factors that perpetuate somatic preoccupation, including reinforcement, trauma, health anxiety, medical uncertainty, and clinician response.","batch":1,"stem":"Which factor has been suggested by some (though not all) studies as associated with a later propensity for developing conversion disorder?","choices":{"A":"A family history of Briquet syndrome in several first-degree relatives","B":"Adult-onset occupational stress in a high-demand job","C":"A comorbid substance use disorder beginning in adulthood","D":"Childhood traumatization by sexual or physical abuse","E":"A confirmed personal history of factitious disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Some studies have suggested a strong relationship between childhood traumatization by sexual or physical abuse and a later propensity for conversion disorder, although other studies do not confirm such an association, making this a suggested but not universally confirmed etiologic factor. A confirmed family history of hysteria/Briquet syndrome is instead described in relation to somatic symptom disorder's (not conversion disorder's) early family studies. Adult-onset occupational stress with no childhood component does not match the described childhood-trauma-specific association. A confirmed comorbid substance use disorder is not the specific factor described in this etiologic discussion of conversion disorder. A confirmed personal history of factitious disorder is not the described risk factor for conversion disorder in this framework.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Childhood sexual or physical abuse has been proposed (with mixed evidence) as a risk factor for later conversion disorder — a nuanced, \"suggested but not confirmed\" association worth recognizing rather than treating as definitive. COMLEX: This uncertain evidence base is itself testable — avoid overstating this association as a firmly established causal link.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 4 — extends the social-determinants/trauma-exposure modifier framework to the specific factors that perpetuate somatic preoccupation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare management of conversion disorder, illness anxiety disorder, somatic symptom disorder, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":1,"stem":"Comparing the overall treatment approach for factitious disorder to that of somatic symptom disorder, which best characterizes the primary treatment focus for factitious disorder?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy aimed at remission","B":"Antidepressant therapy aimed at remission","C":"Confrontation aimed at eliciting a confession","D":"Discharge without ongoing medical follow-up","E":"Long-term management without expectation of cure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"No specific psychiatric therapy has been effective in treating factitious disorders, and treatment is thus best focused on management rather than on cure, given the clinical paradox that patients simulate illness while denying their actual factitious condition and often elude meaningful therapy by leaving the hospital or missing follow-up. CBT holds prototype first-line status for somatic symptom disorder and illness anxiety disorder specifically, not for factitious disorder, where no specific therapy has been shown effective. No medication is described as effective for factitious disorder itself, so antidepressant therapy aimed at remission is not the treatment focus; medication is reserved for any comorbid psychiatric disorder. Confronting the patient to extract a confession usually leads the patient to leave care and seek treatment elsewhere, rather than engaging them in ongoing management. Discharge without follow-up abandons the described need for good liaison between psychiatrists and medical/surgical staff and working with the primary care physician.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Factitious disorder treatment is explicitly management-focused, not cure-focused, unlike somatic symptom disorder and illness anxiety disorder, which have established first-line therapies (CBT). COMLEX: Working in concert with the patient's primary care physician is described as more effective than working with the patient in isolation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 5 — extends the factitious-disorder-versus-malingering motivation comparison into a full cross-disorder management comparison","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare management of conversion disorder, illness anxiety disorder, somatic symptom disorder, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":1,"stem":"Comparing management across this differential, which disorder's treatment specifically and uniquely emphasizes avoiding any implication to the patient that they are malingering, since such an implication is described as very counterproductive?","choices":{"A":"Illness anxiety disorder","B":"Factitious disorder","C":"Conversion disorder","D":"Malingering","E":"Somatic symptom disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In conversion disorder management, reassuring patients that critical tests are normal and symptoms will improve may be helpful early on, but any implication to the patient that he or she is malingering is described as very counterproductive, since conversion symptoms are not intentionally produced and such an implication is invalidating and damaging to the therapeutic relationship. Illness anxiety disorder management centers on CBT and addressing health anxiety, without this specific malingering-implication caution described for conversion disorder. Factitious disorder management instead emphasizes an empathic, nonconfrontational, face-saving approach to psychiatric referral, a related but distinct caution about aggressive confrontation rather than this specific malingering-implication warning. Malingering itself is not a disorder requiring this caution; by definition it involves conscious, goal-directed symptom production, unlike conversion disorder's involuntary symptoms. Somatic symptom disorder management centers on regularly scheduled visits with a single primary care physician and CBT, without this specific malingering-implication caution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conversion disorder's treatment specifically warns against implying malingering, since symptoms are involuntary — distinct from factitious disorder's broader nonconfrontational-referral caution. COMLEX: This distinction matters because conversion disorder and factitious disorder require different communication nuances despite both needing an empathic approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 5 — extends the factitious-disorder-versus-malingering motivation comparison into a full cross-disorder management comparison","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Malingering itself is not a di"}},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare management of conversion disorder, illness anxiety disorder, somatic symptom disorder, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the five disorders in this differential (conversion disorder, illness anxiety disorder, somatic symptom disorder, factitious disorder, malingering) has no established psychiatric treatment approach at all, since the \"symptoms\" are consciously produced for a recognizable external goal rather than reflecting genuine psychopathology requiring treatment?","choices":{"A":"Conversion disorder","B":"Malingering","C":"Illness anxiety disorder","D":"Somatic symptom disorder","E":"Factitious disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Malingering is the only one of the five in which symptoms are consciously produced in the service of a manifest, recognizable external goal — avoiding work, military service or prosecution, or obtaining drugs, money or shelter. Because the behavior serves that goal rather than reflecting psychopathology, malingering is not itself a psychiatric disorder and has no established psychiatric treatment; management consists of recognizing the goal-directed pattern rather than treating an underlying illness. Each of the other four is a psychiatric disorder with a described treatment approach. Conversion disorder involves genuine, involuntary symptom production, and is managed with reassurance, CBT, physical therapy and behavioral techniques. Illness anxiety disorder involves genuine, excessive health-related distress, for which CBT is the prototype first-line treatment. Somatic symptom disorder involves genuine, distressing symptom preoccupation, for which CBT has the highest demonstrated efficacy among reviewed treatments. Factitious disorder is the one most easily confused with malingering, because its symptoms are also consciously produced. The difference is the motivation: an unconscious psychological need to occupy the sick role rather than a manifest external reward. That keeps it a psychiatric diagnosis with described management guidelines addressing the underlying emotional needs and comorbid psychiatric illness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Malingering is unique among this five-disorder differential in not being a genuine psychiatric condition requiring treatment — the goal-directed symptom production is the entire explanation, with no underlying psychopathology to treat via CBT or similar approaches. COMLEX: This distinction is why malingering management centers on recognizing the pattern and addressing the underlying incentive/context, not psychiatric therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 5 — extends the factitious-disorder-versus-malingering motivation comparison into a full cross-disorder management comparison","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Conversion disorder involves g","C":"Illness anxiety disorder invol","D":"Somatic symptom disorder invol","E":"That keeps it a psychiatric di"}},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret red flags that require medical workup despite a suspected functional or somatic disorder.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a long-established diagnosis of somatic symptom disorder reports a fever measured at 41.5°C during a self-monitored episode at home. Which principle should guide the clinical response to this specific finding, despite the established functional diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"The finding should be dismissed outright, since it fits her known somatic diagnosis","B":"No further evaluation is ever needed, once a functional diagnosis is established","C":"The finding should be assumed to be a normal physiologic variant, needing no workup","D":"The finding should be handled purely psychologically, skipping objective verification","E":"A fever this high suggests factitious manufacture, warranting objective testing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A fever higher than 41°C is specifically listed as a sign suggestive of factitious symptom manufacture (e.g., via injecting infectious material), and confirmatory testing involves monitoring while the temperature is taken, so this extreme, unusually high self-reported fever should prompt objective verification rather than automatic acceptance, regardless of a prior somatic or factitious diagnosis. Entirely dismissing this finding as consistent with her known diagnosis ignores the described red-flag significance of a fever this high and the possibility of a superimposed factitious or genuine infectious process requiring evaluation. Claiming no further evaluation is ever needed once a diagnosis is established directly contradicts the principle that authentic or concomitant illness must always be considered. Assuming a normal physiologic variant with no workup ignores the specifically described diagnostic significance of this extreme temperature. Treating the finding purely psychologically without any objective verification ignores the specific recommended confirmatory test (monitored temperature-taking) for this red flag.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A reported fever above 41°C is a specifically named red flag suggestive of factitious fever manufacture, warranting monitored verification even in a patient with an established somatic or factitious diagnosis. COMLEX: Other described factitious fever techniques include ingesting thyroid hormone, drinking hot fluids before measurement, and manipulating thermometers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — extends the medical/neurologic/endocrine exclusion-workup framework into recognizing red flags requiring workup mid-course","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret red flags that require medical workup despite a suspected functional or somatic disorder.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with long-standing conversion disorder and previously normal neurologic exams develops a genuinely new, objectively abnormal finding on formal strength testing (true, consistent weakness rather than give-away weakness) along with new muscle atrophy. What should this finding prompt, despite her established diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"No further workup, since her diagnosis is assumed to explain any new finding permanently","B":"A new medical/neurologic workup, since these findings do not fit conversion disorder","C":"Immediate psychiatric hospitalization, forgoing any renewed medical evaluation","D":"Simple reassurance, framing this as an expected progression of her known disorder","E":"Referral for applied tension therapy, indicated specifically for blood/injection/injury phobia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"True, consistent weakness (rather than give-away weakness) and new muscle atrophy are not the described findings of conversion disorder, which typically shows normal reflexes, no fasciculations, and no atrophy except after long-standing conversion paralysis; new objective findings inconsistent with the functional pattern should prompt a new medical/neurologic workup, since a somatic or functional diagnosis never permanently excludes the later development of authentic disease. Assuming her conversion disorder diagnosis permanently explains any new finding ignores the principle that authentic or concomitant illness must always be considered, especially with objectively inconsistent new findings. Immediate psychiatric hospitalization with no medical evaluation fails to address the possibility of a new, genuine neurologic process suggested by these findings. Reassurance alone that this is expected progression contradicts the description that conversion disorder findings (normal reflexes, no atrophy) do not match this new objective pattern. Applied tension therapy is specifically indicated for blood/injection/injury phobia, not for a change in motor exam findings in conversion disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: New, objectively true (non-give-away) weakness with atrophy in a patient with established conversion disorder is inconsistent with the typical functional exam pattern and is a red flag requiring renewed medical workup. COMLEX: This reflects the broader principle that a psychiatric or functional diagnosis is never a permanent excuse to stop considering new organic pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — extends the medical/neurologic/endocrine exclusion-workup framework into recognizing red flags requiring workup mid-course","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret red flags that require medical workup despite a suspected functional or somatic disorder.","batch":1,"stem":"A resident caring for a patient with an established diagnosis of conversion disorder asks why the attending insists on a thorough medical and neurologic workup and on reassessing any new or changing symptom. Based on follow-up data, about what proportion of patients classified as having conversion disorder eventually receive a neurologic or other medical diagnosis that could have caused their earlier symptoms?","choices":{"A":"About 25 to 50 percent","B":"Less than 1 percent","C":"About 3 to 5 percent","D":"About 75 to 90 percent","E":"Essentially 100 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock estimate that 25 to 50 percent of patients classified as having conversion disorder eventually receive diagnoses of neurologic or nonpsychiatric medical disorders that could have caused their earlier symptoms, and that evidence of a current or previous neurologic or systemic disease affecting the brain occurs in 18 to 64 percent of hospitalized patients with conversion disorder; for this reason a thorough medical and neurologic workup is essential in all cases. Figures below 1 percent or around 3 to 5 percent understate the risk and would wrongly justify skipping evaluation. A figure of 75 to 90 percent overstates it, and essentially all cases is incorrect, since many patients never receive another diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: conversion disorder mimics include multiple sclerosis, myasthenia gravis, polymyositis, Guillain-Barré syndrome, periodic paralysis, optic neuritis, and early neurologic AIDS. COMLEX: a functional diagnosis never ends the medical evaluation; new, objective, or changing findings require a fresh workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 3 — medical and neurologic conditions to exclude before a somatic diagnosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select a patient-centered plan that balances diagnostic safety, cost-effectiveness, empathy, ethics, and interdisciplinary care.","batch":1,"stem":"A physician is designing a comprehensive care plan for a patient with a somatic symptom and related disorder. Which combination of considerations should the plan balance, per patient-centered planning principles?","choices":{"A":"Diagnostic safety, since missing authentic illness is the single greatest clinical risk","B":"Diagnostic safety, cost-effectiveness, empathy, ethics, and interdisciplinary care","C":"Cost-effectiveness, since unnecessary testing is the most visible system-level burden","D":"Interdisciplinary care, since specialty coordination is the most visible logistic piece","E":"Ethics, since respecting patient autonomy is the foundational clinical principle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A patient-centered plan for somatic symptom and related disorders has to balance all five considerations at once: diagnostic safety (not missing authentic or concomitant illness), cost-effectiveness (avoiding unnecessary tests and procedures), empathy (a neutral, nondismissive stance), ethics (avoiding both under- and over-testing, and respecting patient autonomy), and interdisciplinary care (liaison between psychiatry, primary care and specialists). Each of the other options states something true and then treats it as sufficient by itself, which is where it fails. Diagnostic safety does carry the greatest risk for the individual patient, but pursued alone it produces excessive, costly, fragmented care and the iatrogenic harm of repeated testing. Unnecessary testing is the most visible system-level burden, but cost-effectiveness alone risks missing authentic illness, a specifically described danger in a population that does go on to develop real disease. Specialty coordination is the most visible logistic piece, but interdisciplinary care without empathy or ethics produces a technically coordinated yet invalidating approach. Respecting autonomy is foundational, but ethics alone is an incomplete framework that ignores the practical clinical and financial dimensions of care. No single element substitutes for the others.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A patient-centered plan integrates multiple simultaneous considerations (safety, cost, empathy, ethics, interdisciplinary coordination) rather than optimizing any single element alone — a synthesis point for somatic symptom and related disorders. COMLEX: This balanced framework directly builds on the neutral communication stance and medical-exclusion workup principles taught for these disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into the broader ethics/cost-effectiveness/interdisciplinary-care planning principles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select a patient-centered plan that balances diagnostic safety, cost-effectiveness, empathy, ethics, and interdisciplinary care.","batch":1,"stem":"A care team managing a complex patient with overlapping somatic symptom disorder and factitious disorder features is coordinating across psychiatry, primary care, and specialty medicine. Which principle best supports effective interdisciplinary care in this context?","choices":{"A":"A good liaison between psychiatry and the medical/surgical team","B":"Psychiatry assuming sole responsibility for all medical decisions","C":"Each specialty keeping a separate care plan for its own concerns","D":"Limiting psychiatry to a single consultation at admission","E":"Routing communication between clinicians through the patient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"There must be a good liaison between psychiatrists and the medical or surgical staff, and working in concert with the patient's primary care physician is described as more effective than working with the patient (or, by extension, managing the case) in isolation. Psychiatry assuming sole responsibility for medical decisions removes the primary care and specialty clinicians the patient needs, failing the described liaison principle. Separate care plans kept by each specialty fragment care and invite duplicated testing and staff splitting. A single psychiatric consultation at admission is not the ongoing, coordinated liaison described. Routing communication between clinicians through the patient is the fragmented, isolated pattern that working in concert is meant to prevent, and with factitious features it hands the patient control of what each team hears.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Good liaison between psychiatry and medical/surgical staff, with coordinated rather than isolated management, is a specifically recommended interdisciplinary care principle. COMLEX: This same coordination principle also underlies the recommendation for regular interdisciplinary meetings to manage staff splitting and countertransference in factitious disorder care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into the broader ethics/cost-effectiveness/interdisciplinary-care planning principles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select a patient-centered plan that balances diagnostic safety, cost-effectiveness, empathy, ethics, and interdisciplinary care.","batch":1,"stem":"A physician is weighing whether to order an additional round of imaging for a patient with a well-established somatic symptom disorder diagnosis and a long history of unremarkable workups, who has no new or red-flag findings. Which principle should guide this specific decision?","choices":{"A":"Repeating every possible test regardless of red-flag status, to stay maximally safe","B":"Never testing again for this patient, under any future clinical circumstances","C":"Basing the decision solely on the patient's request, regardless of indication","D":"Cost-effectiveness and avoiding unnecessary testing, absent any new red-flag findings","E":"Delegating the decision entirely to a specialist, with no primary care input"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patient-centered planning balances diagnostic safety with cost-effectiveness, and treatment principles specifically recommend avoiding unnecessary tests and procedures; absent any new or red-flag findings, ordering additional imaging in a patient with a well-established diagnosis and a long history of unremarkable workups would represent exactly the unnecessary testing this framework recommends avoiding. Always repeating every possible test regardless of red-flag status contradicts the specific recommendation to avoid unnecessary tests and procedures. Never testing again under any future circumstances is also incorrect, since new red-flag findings should always prompt renewed evaluation; the principle is contextual, not absolute. Basing the decision solely on patient request, regardless of clinical indication, does not reflect the balanced, evidence-based decision-making this framework describes. Delegating the decision entirely to a specialist with no primary care input contradicts the described interdisciplinary, coordinated approach with the primary care physician central to ongoing management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cost-effectiveness and avoidance of unnecessary testing specifically apply when there are no new red-flag findings — this is a contextual, not absolute, principle that must be weighed against ongoing vigilance for genuine new disease. COMLEX: This same balance (avoiding unnecessary testing while remaining alert to red flags) is the core tension in managing somatic symptom and related disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into the broader ethics/cost-effectiveness/interdisciplinary-care planning principles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for somatic symptom and related disorders using scheduled follow-up, functional goals, therapeutic alliance, and avoidance of excessive testing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with a 3-year history of somatic symptom disorder has been seeing a rotating group of urgent care providers whenever new symptoms arise, undergoing repeated redundant testing. Her new primary care physician wants to restructure her care per recommended treatment principles. Which specific changes should be prioritized first?","choices":{"A":"Referring her immediately to five specialists, for a comprehensive simultaneous workup now","B":"Instructing her to seek care at the emergency department going forward","C":"Discharging her from care, until she accepts a purely psychological explanation","D":"Continuing the same rotating-provider pattern, while simply adding more testing","E":"Committing her to one physician, with regularly scheduled, not symptom-triggered visits"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Recommended treatment principles specifically emphasize a commitment to a single primary care physician and regularly scheduled appointments (rather than symptom-triggered, as-needed visits with rotating providers) as core, modest, attainable first steps in restructuring care for somatic symptom disorder, directly addressing this patient's described fragmented, symptom-triggered pattern. Referring her immediately to five different specialists for a simultaneous comprehensive workup contradicts the recommendation to limit specialist consultations unless comorbid physical conditions are evident. Instructing her to seek care only at the emergency department is not a recommended treatment principle and would likely increase, not decrease, unnecessary testing and fragmented care. Discharging her from all care until she accepts a purely psychological explanation is inconsistent with the described long-term commitment to the patient and the caution against psychological interpretations working poorly early on. Continuing the same rotating-provider, symptom-triggered pattern while adding more tests is the opposite of the recommended restructuring and would likely worsen, not improve, her care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Restructuring fragmented, symptom-triggered, multi-provider care into a single-PCP, regularly-scheduled model is a core, evidence-supported first step in somatic symptom disorder management. COMLEX: This restructuring directly parallels the described consultation-letter intervention, which showed improved functional capacity and decreased health service use.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 6 — extends the neutral, nonconfrontational communication framework into concrete treatment restructuring","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for somatic symptom and related disorders using scheduled follow-up, functional goals, therapeutic alliance, and avoidance of excessive testing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with somatic symptom disorder has begun keeping a symptom and activity diary as part of his treatment plan, a technique borrowed from a specific evidence-based therapy. Which therapy does this diary-keeping technique originate from, and how does it fit into his overall primary care management?","choices":{"A":"CBT, whose elements like diary keeping belong in the primary care strategy","B":"Dialectical behavior therapy, whose diary cards should fully replace primary care","C":"Sodium amytal interview, whose sessions supply the diary content","D":"Transference-focused psychotherapy, which uses diaries to track transference","E":"Applied tension, whose practice logs are a form of diary keeping"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some elements of CBT, such as diary keeping, should be incorporated into the primary care physician's treatment strategy for somatic symptom disorder, reflecting CBT's demonstrated efficacy and its practical adaptability into ongoing primary care management rather than requiring a separate specialist referral for every element. Dialectical behavior therapy does use diary cards (for BPD), but this is a distinct therapy from the one described here for somatic symptom disorder, and diary cards in DBT do not replace, but supplement, its structured four-mode treatment; this option also incorrectly claims diary keeping should replace primary care involvement. Sodium amytal interview is a distinct conversion disorder chronic-phase technique unrelated to diary-keeping. Transference-focused psychotherapy is a personality disorder treatment modality that does not use this diary-keeping technique in the context described here. Applied tension is a distinct technique for blood/injury phobia, unrelated to diary-keeping.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CBT's diary-keeping technique is specifically described as adaptable into primary care management for somatic symptom disorder, illustrating how evidence-based psychotherapy elements can be integrated into non-specialist care. COMLEX: This integration reflects the broader principle that specialist referral should be limited unless comorbid physical conditions are evident.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 6 — extends the neutral, nonconfrontational communication framework into concrete treatment restructuring","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply treatment principles for somatic symptom and related disorders using scheduled follow-up, functional goals, therapeutic alliance, and avoidance of excessive testing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with somatic symptom disorder asks her physician whether complementary approaches like yoga, exercise, or massage might help alongside standard treatment. How should the physician respond, per described treatment principles?","choices":{"A":"These approaches have no described role and should be actively discouraged","B":"These approaches should replace psychiatric involvement in her ongoing care","C":"These approaches may help and are better accepted than psychological treatments","D":"These approaches are specifically contraindicated in somatic symptom disorder","E":"These approaches match CBT's evidence strength and should replace it as first-line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Recommendations for exercise, yoga, relaxation, meditation, and massage may be useful for somatic symptom disorder and are generally better accepted by patients than psychological treatments, making them a reasonable adjunctive suggestion to offer this patient. These approaches are not described as having no role or as something to actively discourage; they are specifically named as potentially useful. These approaches are not described as a replacement for all psychiatric involvement; they are adjunctive, not a substitute for the broader treatment framework (scheduled visits, limited specialist referral, CBT elements). These approaches are not described as contraindicated; the opposite is true, they are specifically recommended as potentially beneficial. These approaches are not described as equivalent in evidence strength to CBT, which has the strongest demonstrated RCT evidence base; they are a complementary, better-accepted addition, not a replacement for CBT's first-line status.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complementary approaches (exercise, yoga, relaxation, meditation, massage) are specifically noted as potentially useful and better accepted than psychological treatments in somatic symptom disorder — useful for patient-centered treatment planning. COMLEX: These remain adjunctive to, not replacements for, the core evidence-based framework (CBT, scheduled visits, limited specialist referral).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 6 — extends the neutral, nonconfrontational communication framework into concrete treatment restructuring","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"These approaches are not described as co"}},{"id":"sdl32_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate appropriate reassurance, CBT, physical rehabilitation, psychiatric referral, and treatment of comorbid anxiety or depression by clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man has chronic conversion paralysis that has developed muscle contractures after a prolonged, entrenched course. Which specific treatment modality is described as necessary for this particular chronic complication?","choices":{"A":"Sodium amytal interview","B":"Applied tension","C":"Electroconvulsive therapy","D":"Physical therapy","E":"Behavioral activation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With chronic conversion disorder, muscle contractures can occur, and physical therapy using slowly progressive exercises and activity is described for this situation, helping to restore functioning even in the absence of contractures for muscular symptoms or balance problems. Sodium amytal interview alone is one component of intensive, multimodal treatment for chronic conversion disorder generally, but it does not address the physical mechanics of the muscle contracture complication itself. Applied tension alone uses isometric muscle contraction to raise blood pressure for blood/injection/injury phobia, not for conversion disorder muscle contractures. Electroconvulsive therapy targets treatment-resistant mood or psychotic illness, not conversion disorder or its contracture complications. Behavioral activation alone is a depression-focused technique for behavioral withdrawal, not a treatment for physical contractures in chronic conversion disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physical therapy with slowly progressive exercises addresses muscle contractures that can develop in chronic, entrenched conversion disorder — a distinguishing detail among the many listed treatment modalities. COMLEX: Physical therapy is also broadly helpful for conversion disorder muscular symptoms or balance problems even without frank contractures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the disorder-differentiation framework into matching each disorder to its treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate appropriate reassurance, CBT, physical rehabilitation, psychiatric referral, and treatment of comorbid anxiety or depression by clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has psychological factors affecting her poorly controlled asthma, with anxiety-driven exacerbations. Beyond addressing any underlying psychiatric disorder, which treatment element is specifically described for this category of condition?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy for health anxiety","B":"Sodium amytal interview for symptom relief","C":"Physical therapy with graded exercises","D":"Hypnotic suggestion to remove the symptoms","E":"Psychoeducation on symptom-aggravating factors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Treatment for psychological factors affecting other medical conditions frequently involves communication with the patient's primary medical team and family, with a psychoeducational intervention that clarifies the role emotional and behavioral factors play in aggravating the underlying medical condition, alongside medication for any underlying psychiatric disorder if necessary. CBT is described as the prototype first-line treatment specifically for illness anxiety disorder, not as the described primary approach for psychological factors affecting other medical conditions. Sodium amytal interview is a conversion disorder-specific chronic-phase technique, not the described approach here. Hypnotic suggestion is used to relieve conversion symptoms, not to address how anxiety aggravates a real medical illness. Physical therapy is described as a conversion disorder treatment element, not the primary described approach for this category.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psychological factors affecting other medical conditions treatment centers on psychoeducation and communication with the primary medical team about how emotional/behavioral factors worsen the real medical condition — distinct from the CBT-centered approaches used for the other disorders. COMLEX: Medication may still be necessary for an underlying psychiatric disorder alongside this psychoeducational approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the disorder-differentiation framework into matching each disorder to its treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CBT is described as the protot"}},{"id":"sdl32_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate appropriate reassurance, CBT, physical rehabilitation, psychiatric referral, and treatment of comorbid anxiety or depression by clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with new-onset conversion disorder presents shortly after a significant stressor, with an acute, benign-appearing course. Per described treatment principles for this early presentation, what is the most appropriate initial approach, and why does timing matter?","choices":{"A":"Detailed psychological interpretation of her unconscious conflict, delivered right away","B":"Reassurance and support, delivered promptly while the course is still acute","C":"Aggressive confrontation about the psychological nature of her symptoms, to speed things up","D":"Deferring any intervention, until symptoms have persisted for at least six months","E":"Immediate hospitalization and intensive multimodal treatment, regardless of acuity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Many conversion syndromes have an acute, benign course and may remit spontaneously with understanding and support, and early intervention can specifically forestall potential chronicity and progression into a well-entrenched somatization disorder, making prompt, supportive reassurance the appropriate initial approach for this acute presentation. Immediate, detailed psychological interpretation is explicitly described as not working well early in the process, directly contradicting the claim that it always works best delivered early. Aggressive confrontation about the psychological nature of symptoms is not a recommended approach at any stage and risks being invalidating and counterproductive. Deferring any intervention until symptoms persist for 6 months contradicts the described benefit of early intervention in forestalling chronicity; waiting risks the very entrenchment early support is meant to prevent. Immediate hospitalization and intensive multimodal treatment regardless of acuity is described as appropriate once chronicity has developed, not as the first approach for an acute, benign-appearing presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Early, supportive intervention in acute conversion disorder can forestall chronicity — timing matters, and intensive multimodal treatment is reserved for cases that have already become chronic. COMLEX: Psychological interpretation specifically should be deferred, not attempted early, distinguishing initial supportive management from later-stage insight-oriented approaches.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 1 — extends the disorder-differentiation framework into matching each disorder to its treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze factors that perpetuate somatic preoccupation, including reinforcement, trauma, health anxiety, medical uncertainty, and clinician response.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with somatic symptom disorder finds that each time he reports a new symptom, his family excuses him from household responsibilities and increases attention and caretaking toward him. Over time, his symptom reporting becomes more frequent. Which perpetuating factor does this scenario best illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Reinforcement via the sick role's escape from obligations, the described mechanism","B":"Somatic amplification, due to a low threshold for ordinary physical discomfort","C":"A confirmed structural brain lesion, affecting dominant-hemisphere verbal association","D":"Childhood traumatization by sexual or physical abuse, per inconsistent evidence","E":"A family history of hysteria/Briquet syndrome, in multiple first-degree relatives"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This scenario illustrates the social learning/sick-role model of perpetuation, in which symptoms function as a request for admission to the sick role that offers an escape from unwelcome obligations (household responsibilities) and is reinforced by increased attention and caretaking, driving more frequent symptom reporting over time. Somatic amplification describes a distinct cognitive/sensory mechanism (low threshold for discomfort, misinterpretation of sensations), not this externally reinforced escape-from-obligations pattern. A confirmed structural brain lesion affecting verbal association is part of the neuropsychologic conceptualization discussed for conversion disorder, an unrelated biologic mechanism not illustrated by this behavioral reinforcement scenario. Childhood traumatization by sexual or physical abuse is a distinct, separately described (and inconsistently supported) etiologic factor, not what this scenario of ongoing family reinforcement illustrates. A family history of hysteria/Briquet syndrome is a distinct familial/genetic etiologic thread from early family studies, not the behavioral reinforcement pattern described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Family or clinician responses that reward symptom reporting (increased attention, excused obligations) directly illustrate the social learning/sick-role reinforcement model perpetuating somatic preoccupation. COMLEX: Recognizing this reinforcement pattern is clinically important for designing interventions (e.g., family psychoeducation) that avoid inadvertently rewarding illness behavior.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 4 — extends the social-determinants/trauma modifier framework to somatic preoccupation perpetuation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze factors that perpetuate somatic preoccupation, including reinforcement, trauma, health anxiety, medical uncertainty, and clinician response.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with chronic, unexplained abdominal pain has seen numerous physicians, none of whom can definitively explain her symptoms, leading to escalating anxiety, more frequent visits, and further testing that also proves inconclusive. Which perpetuating factor from the described framework does this specific cycle best illustrate?","choices":{"A":"A confirmed underlying structural brain lesion, driving her symptom reporting","B":"Deliberate, conscious symptom fabrication, for a clearly recognizable external goal","C":"Medical uncertainty, which fuels worry and continued care-seeking","D":"A clearly established comorbid substance use disorder, driving her presentation","E":"A confirmed family history of antisocial personality disorder in male relatives"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ongoing diagnostic uncertainty (repeated inconclusive workups without a definitive explanation) is a recognized factor that perpetuates somatic preoccupation, since the persistence of unexplained symptoms without resolution or clear answers can escalate health anxiety and drive continued, and sometimes excessive, care-seeking behavior, exactly as illustrated in this vignette's escalating cycle. A confirmed underlying structural brain lesion is not described or supported in this vignette, which centers on diagnostic uncertainty rather than an identified organic lesion. Deliberate, conscious symptom fabrication for an external goal describes malingering, not this vignette's described pattern of genuine, escalating anxiety amid unexplained symptoms. A clearly established comorbid substance use disorder is not described in this vignette. A confirmed family history of antisocial personality disorder in male relatives is a distinct etiologic thread described for somatic symptom disorder generally (early family studies), not the specific mechanism illustrated by this diagnostic-uncertainty cycle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medical uncertainty (repeated inconclusive workups) is a recognized perpetuating factor for somatic preoccupation, escalating health anxiety and driving further care-seeking in a self-reinforcing cycle. COMLEX: This factor is one reason a consistent, single primary care physician and modest, attainable treatment goals are emphasized — to interrupt this uncertainty-driven escalation cycle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 4 — extends the social-determinants/trauma modifier framework to somatic preoccupation perpetuation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze factors that perpetuate somatic preoccupation, including reinforcement, trauma, health anxiety, medical uncertainty, and clinician response.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician repeatedly tells a patient with somatic symptom disorder that \"there is nothing wrong with you\" and appears visibly frustrated and dismissive during visits. Over subsequent months, the patient's symptom reporting and healthcare-seeking intensify. Which perpetuating factor does this scenario best illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Somatic amplification, due solely to a faulty cognitive scheme","B":"A confirmed genetic or family predisposition to somatic symptoms","C":"Deliberate, conscious symptom fabrication, undertaken solely by the patient","D":"A confirmed comorbid primary psychotic disorder in the patient","E":"Clinician response itself, via a dismissive, invalidating communication style"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Clinician response is itself a recognized factor that can perpetuate somatic preoccupation, since a dismissive, invalidating approach (such as bluntly saying \"nothing is wrong\") is exactly the kind of communication the framework specifically warns against, given that patients may perceive dismissiveness as confirmation their concerns are not being taken seriously, which can intensify rather than resolve symptom-focused care-seeking. Somatic amplification alone is a distinct, patient-internal cognitive/sensory mechanism, not this externally driven clinician-communication factor. A confirmed genetic/family predisposition alone is a distinct etiologic thread unrelated to this scenario's described physician behavior. Deliberate, conscious symptom fabrication by the patient is not indicated in this vignette, which centers on the physician's dismissive communication style as the described perpetuating factor, not patient fabrication. A confirmed comorbid primary psychotic disorder is not described or supported anywhere in this vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clinician communication style itself (dismissive vs. validating) is a recognized perpetuating factor for somatic preoccupation, directly supporting the recommended neutral, nondismissive, nonconfrontational communication approach for these disorders. COMLEX: This is a key patient-safety and therapeutic-alliance concept — the physician's own behavior can worsen, not just respond to, the clinical picture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 4 — extends the social-determinants/trauma modifier framework to somatic preoccupation perpetuation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare management of conversion disorder, illness anxiety disorder, somatic symptom disorder, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":2,"stem":"A treatment team is comparing management approaches across the five-disorder differential (conversion disorder, illness anxiety disorder, somatic symptom disorder, factitious disorder, malingering) for a teaching conference. Which statement correctly compares the evidence-based, structured psychotherapy status of these disorders?","choices":{"A":"CBT is described as equally well studied and equally effective across all five listed disorders","B":"CBT is strongest for somatic symptom disorder, unproven for factitious, moot for malingering","C":"Factitious disorder is described as having the single strongest CBT evidence base of these five","D":"Malingering has an established, effective structured psychotherapy, just as somatic symptom disorder does","E":"No disorder among these five has any described evidence-based treatment approach"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CBT specifically has the strongest reviewed evidence base for somatic symptom disorder and is described as the prototype first-line treatment for illness anxiety disorder, while factitious disorder management is explicitly management-, not cure-, focused given the absence of proven effective specific therapy, and malingering, lacking genuine underlying psychiatric illness, is not treated with psychiatric therapy at all — an important, accurate cross-disorder comparison. Claiming CBT is equally first-line and equally effective across all five disorders ignores these described distinctions, particularly factitious disorder's lack of proven specific therapy and malingering's non-psychiatric nature. Claiming factitious disorder has the strongest CBT evidence base directly contradicts the description that no specific psychiatric therapy has proven effective for factitious disorder. Claiming malingering has an established, effective first-line psychiatric therapy contradicts its described nature as goal-directed, consciously produced symptoms without genuine underlying psychopathology requiring treatment. Claiming no disorder in this differential has any described evidence-based treatment ignores CBT's well-established evidence base for somatic symptom disorder and illness anxiety disorder specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Treatment evidence and approach vary substantially across the five-disorder somatic differential — CBT-strong for somatic symptom disorder/illness anxiety disorder, management-only for factitious disorder, and non-psychiatric for malingering. COMLEX: Recognizing which disorders have an actual \"treatment\" versus which require \"management\" is a key synthesis point for this differential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 5 — extends the factitious-vs-malingering motivation comparison into a full cross-disorder management comparison","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Claiming factitious disorder h","E":"Claiming no disorder in this d"}},{"id":"sdl32_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare management of conversion disorder, illness anxiety disorder, somatic symptom disorder, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":2,"stem":"A resident is confused about why psychological interpretation is discouraged early in treating conversion disorder but psychoeducation about emotional/behavioral factors is specifically emphasized early in treating psychological factors affecting other medical conditions. What best explains this apparent difference in management approach between the two disorders?","choices":{"A":"There is actually no meaningful management difference at all between these two disorders","B":"This other category is always more severe, therefore requiring more aggressive early care","C":"Conversion symptoms are involuntary, so early interpretation feels invalidating","D":"Conversion disorder patients are always less psychologically minded than these patients","E":"Psychoeducation and psychological interpretation are, in practice, the identical technique"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Conversion disorder's symptoms are not intentionally produced and typically have symbolic/unconscious significance, so premature psychological interpretation early in treatment does not work well and can feel invalidating, whereas psychological factors affecting other medical conditions involves a genuine, identifiable medical illness (e.g., diabetes, asthma, hypertension) where patients can more concretely understand how specific emotional or behavioral patterns (such as medication manipulation or stress-triggered exacerbations) are worsening a real, already-diagnosed condition, making direct psychoeducation appropriate earlier in this context. Claiming no meaningful management difference exists ignores this described distinction in timing and approach to psychological content between the two conditions. Claiming psychological factors affecting other medical conditions is always more severe and requires more aggressive intervention is not supported; severity is not the described basis for this difference in approach. Claiming conversion disorder patients are always less psychologically minded is an unsupported overgeneralization. Claiming psychoeducation and psychological interpretation are the same technique ignores their distinct roles: psychoeducation directly explains a concrete emotional-behavioral-medical link, while premature psychological interpretation in conversion disorder involves interpreting deeper unconscious conflict, which is specifically discouraged early on.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The involuntary, symbolically-mediated nature of conversion disorder symptoms explains why early interpretation is avoided there, while the concrete, identifiable behavioral link to a real medical illness in psychological factors affecting other medical conditions supports earlier, direct psychoeducation — a nuanced cross-disorder management comparison. COMLEX: This distinction reinforces why \"one size fits all\" psychological messaging is inappropriate across this differential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 5 — extends the factitious-vs-malingering motivation comparison into a full cross-disorder management comparison","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare management of conversion disorder, illness anxiety disorder, somatic symptom disorder, factitious disorder, and malingering.","batch":2,"stem":"A hospital ethics committee is comparing the management challenges of factitious disorder versus malingering. Which statement accurately compares the two in terms of the clinical paradox each presents?","choices":{"A":"Both disorders present the identical clinical paradox","B":"Malingering presents this treatment-seeking-while-denying paradox, and factitious disorder does not","C":"Neither disorder presents any recognized clinical paradox in this framework","D":"Factitious disorder presents a paradox of seeking treatment while denying the illness itself","E":"The paradox in factitious disorder is that patients openly admit fabrication from the outset"},"correct":"D","explanation":"It is a clinical paradox that patients with factitious disorder simulate severe illness and seek and submit to unnecessary treatment while they deny to themselves and others their actual (factitious) illness, thus avoiding possible treatment for it — a paradox that does not apply to malingering, where the goal-directed motivation, once uncovered, straightforwardly explains the symptom pattern without this deny-while-seeking-treatment contradiction. Claiming both disorders present an identical paradox in every respect ignores this specific, described distinction between factitious disorder's self-deceptive paradox and malingering's straightforwardly goal-directed pattern. Claiming malingering presents this paradox while factitious disorder does not reverses the described attribution; the paradox is specifically described for factitious disorder. Claiming neither disorder presents any paradox ignores the explicitly named clinical paradox of factitious disorder. Claiming factitious disorder patients openly admit to fabricating symptoms from the outset directly contradicts the described denial of their actual illness to themselves and others, which is the core of the paradox.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Factitious disorder's described clinical paradox (unnecessary treatment-seeking combined with denial of the factitious nature of the illness) is a distinguishing, testable feature not shared by malingering's more straightforwardly goal-directed pattern. COMLEX: This paradox also explains why factitious disorder patients often elude meaningful therapy by abruptly leaving care rather than confronting this contradiction directly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 5 — extends the factitious-vs-malingering motivation comparison into a full cross-disorder management comparison","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Claiming malingering presents ","C":"Claiming neither disorder pres"}},{"id":"sdl32_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret red flags that require medical workup despite a suspected functional or somatic disorder.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with a 10-year history of somatic symptom disorder, previously well-controlled with regular primary care visits, presents with a genuinely new symptom: unintentional weight loss of 15 pounds over 2 months, which she had not previously reported in this pattern. How should this new finding be approached, given her established diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"As a red flag warranting a new and focused medical workup","B":"As an expected feature of her long-standing disorder","C":"As proof that her original diagnosis was incorrect","D":"As a reason to discharge her from the physician's panel","E":"As an indication for urgent psychiatric admission"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A genuinely new, objective finding like unintentional weight loss, atypical for her established somatic symptom disorder pattern, is a red flag that requires medical workup despite the suspected functional/somatic disorder, reflecting the broader principle that authentic or concomitant illness must always be considered, even in patients with well-established somatic or functional diagnoses. Treating this as an expected feature requiring no further evaluation ignores its atypical, red-flag nature relative to her known pattern. Treating this as evidence her original diagnosis was incorrect from the start is an overcorrection; a new finding warrants new evaluation, not necessarily invalidation of a previously well-established diagnosis. Treating this as grounds for immediate discharge from care is not a described or appropriate response to a new red-flag finding. Treating this as an indication for exclusively psychiatric evaluation, with no medical workup, ignores the specifically described need for medical exclusion of objective, atypical findings like unintentional weight loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Unintentional weight loss is a classic, broadly recognized medical red flag that should prompt renewed workup regardless of a psychiatric or functional diagnosis history — directly testable in a \"don't anchor\" vignette. COMLEX: This principle reflects the same medical-exclusion vigilance emphasized for somatic symptom disorders, now applied to a new symptom arising mid-course rather than at initial diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — extends the medical/neurologic/endocrine exclusion-workup framework to red flags mid-course","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Treating this as grounds for i"}},{"id":"sdl32_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret red flags that require medical workup despite a suspected functional or somatic disorder.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with a longstanding diagnosis of conversion disorder presenting as leg weakness now reports a new symptom of focal facial droop with slurred speech that began acutely one hour ago. Which approach is most appropriate given this new finding?","choices":{"A":"Reassurance, framing this as simply a new manifestation of his known disorder","B":"Scheduling a routine, non-urgent follow-up appointment several weeks from now","C":"Immediate psychiatric hospitalization, with no acute medical evaluation","D":"Urgent medical evaluation for possible stroke, due to this new focal deficit","E":"Applied tension therapy, the appropriate intervention for a different presentation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An acute, focal presentation like facial droop with slurred speech is a classic red flag requiring urgent medical workup for a time-sensitive condition such as stroke, and even in a patient with an established conversion disorder diagnosis, a new, acute, focal neurologic presentation like this should never be assumed to represent conversion disorder without first urgently excluding an emergent organic cause. Reassuring the patient that this is simply a new manifestation of his known conversion disorder risks a dangerous delay in diagnosing and treating a potential time-sensitive stroke. Scheduling a routine, non-urgent follow-up in several weeks is dangerously inappropriate for an acute, potentially time-sensitive neurologic presentation. Immediate psychiatric hospitalization with no acute medical evaluation fails to address the urgent need to rule out a time-sensitive organic process. Applied tension therapy is specifically indicated for blood/injection/injury phobia's biphasic vasovagal response, entirely unrelated to this acute neurologic presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An acute focal neurologic presentation (facial droop, slurred speech) is always a medical emergency requiring urgent workup, regardless of any prior conversion disorder diagnosis — never assume conversion disorder for a new, acute, focal deficit without urgent exclusion of an emergent cause first. COMLEX: This is among the highest-stakes \"red flag\" teaching points in this differential, given the time-sensitive nature of stroke.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — extends the medical/neurologic/endocrine exclusion-workup framework to red flags mid-course","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret red flags that require medical workup despite a suspected functional or somatic disorder.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman with a history of factitious disorder presents to the emergency department reporting severe abdominal pain, and laboratory testing reveals a genuinely elevated white blood cell count and CT imaging shows evidence of a true bowel obstruction. Given her psychiatric history, how should this presentation be approached?","choices":{"A":"As certainly fabricated findings requiring no surgical evaluation","B":"As a genuine confirmed surgical crisis requiring standard management","C":"As requiring psychiatric clearance before any medical or surgical intervention can proceed","D":"As an opportunity to finally confront her definitively about her factitious disorder before any treatment","E":"As a presentation that should be dismissed until her outpatient psychiatrist can be reached for confirmation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A history of many surgeries or a prior factitious disorder diagnosis may predispose patients to genuine complications or actual disease, and the physician must always explore the possibility of authentic or concomitant physical illness; objective findings here (elevated white blood cell count, CT evidence of true bowel obstruction) represent genuine, confirmable pathology requiring standard emergency surgical management regardless of her psychiatric history. Assuming the findings are certainly fabricated and require no surgical evaluation ignores the objective, confirmatory laboratory and imaging evidence described and risks catastrophic harm from an untreated true surgical emergency. Requiring psychiatric clearance before any medical or surgical intervention dangerously delays urgent surgical care for a confirmed, objective emergency. Using this moment to confront her about her factitious disorder before treatment is an inappropriate, harmful delay of urgent care and also reflects the kind of aggressive confrontation specifically cautioned against. Dismissing the presentation until an outpatient psychiatrist can be reached similarly and dangerously delays urgent, objectively confirmed surgical care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Objective, confirmatory findings (elevated WBC, CT-confirmed obstruction) always warrant standard emergency management, even in a patient with a known factitious disorder history — a psychiatric label never overrides objective medical evidence. COMLEX: This reflects the described principle that repeated surgeries/factitious history can predispose patients to genuine complications requiring careful, non-dismissive evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 — extends the medical/neurologic/endocrine exclusion-workup framework to red flags mid-course","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select a patient-centered plan that balances diagnostic safety, cost-effectiveness, empathy, ethics, and interdisciplinary care.","batch":2,"stem":"A hospital is developing a systemwide protocol for managing patients with somatic symptom and related disorders. Which combination of design principles should the protocol prioritize to best reflect patient-centered planning?","choices":{"A":"Scheduled care that balances diagnostic safety against cost and empathy","B":"Comprehensive testing at each visit to rule out missed organic disease","C":"Symptom-triggered visits, deferring testing until symptoms escalate","D":"Referral to a new specialist for each new symptom","E":"Early psychiatric referral in place of further primary care follow-up"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A patient-centered plan balances diagnostic safety, cost-effectiveness, empathy, ethics, and interdisciplinary care, and a well-designed protocol should reflect structured, regularly scheduled care with a consistent, limited care team (a single primary care physician, limited specialist involvement) while still balancing thorough safety screening for red flags against the costs of unnecessary testing and the empathetic, nonconfrontational communication approach recommended for these disorders. Comprehensive testing at every visit contradicts the described emphasis on cost-effectiveness and avoiding unnecessary tests and procedures, and repeated negative workups tend to reinforce illness worry. Symptom-triggered visits reward symptom escalation with attention, the opposite of the regularly scheduled visits recommended, and deferring testing until symptoms escalate compromises diagnostic safety. Referring to a new specialist for each new symptom multiplies providers and tests instead of keeping a consistent, limited care team. Psychiatric referral should supplement, not replace, the consistent primary care relationship, and patients who feel dismissed to psychiatry often disengage from care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A well-designed protocol operationalizes all five patient-centered planning elements together (safety, cost, empathy, ethics, interdisciplinary care) rather than optimizing any single element at the expense of the others. COMLEX: This systems-level synthesis question ties together nearly every management principle for somatic symptom and related disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into the broader ethics/cost-effectiveness/interdisciplinary-care planning principles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select a patient-centered plan that balances diagnostic safety, cost-effectiveness, empathy, ethics, and interdisciplinary care.","batch":2,"stem":"A care team is debating whether to order an expensive, invasive diagnostic procedure for a patient with a well-established somatic symptom disorder diagnosis who has developed a new but low-specificity symptom (mild, nonspecific fatigue) with no other red-flag features. How should the team weigh diagnostic safety against cost-effectiveness and empathy in this specific decision?","choices":{"A":"The most invasive procedure available, ordered immediately regardless of specificity","B":"No workup of any kind, performed for this or any future new symptom she reports","C":"Whichever test is least expensive, chosen regardless of clinical appropriateness","D":"A decision made unilaterally, without any patient discussion or informed consent","E":"A conservative, less invasive workup fits this low-specificity symptom for now"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Patient-centered planning balances diagnostic safety with cost-effectiveness and empathy, and for a new but low-specificity symptom without red-flag features, a more conservative, less invasive initial workup appropriately balances these considerations, reserving more invasive or costly escalation for situations where red-flag findings actually emerge. Always ordering the most invasive procedure available for any new symptom regardless of specificity ignores cost-effectiveness and would represent exactly the unnecessary, potentially harmful overtesting this framework cautions against. Never performing any workup at all for any new symptom ignores diagnostic safety and the principle that genuinely new findings deserve appropriate evaluation, even if conservative. Basing the decision solely on cost, regardless of clinical appropriateness, ignores diagnostic safety and could miss an actual red flag if it were present. Proceeding without any patient discussion or informed consent ignores the empathy and ethics components of patient-centered planning, which involve the patient in decisions about their own care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diagnostic safety and cost-effectiveness are balanced by matching the intensity of workup to the specificity and red-flag status of the new finding, not by defaulting to either extreme. COMLEX: This graduated, red-flag-triggered escalation approach is the practical clinical application of the balanced patient-centered planning principle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into the broader ethics/cost-effectiveness/interdisciplinary-care planning principles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Basing the decision solely on "}},{"id":"sdl32_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Select a patient-centered plan that balances diagnostic safety, cost-effectiveness, empathy, ethics, and interdisciplinary care.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with somatic symptom disorder feels her psychiatric referral was handled in a stigmatizing way by a prior clinician, who framed it as proof \"it's all in her head.\" Her new physician wants to reintroduce psychiatric involvement in an ethical, empathetic, patient-centered manner. Which approach best achieves this?","choices":{"A":"Avoiding any mention of psychiatric involvement again, to prevent further offense","B":"Framing psychiatric care as part of her care plan, not a sign symptoms are fake","C":"Repeating the same \"it is all in your head\" framing, just delivered more gently","D":"Making psychiatric referral mandatory, as a condition of continued primary care","E":"Discussing the referral with her family, entirely bypassing the patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A patient-centered plan balances empathy and ethics with interdisciplinary care, and framing psychiatric involvement as one coordinated part of a broader care team supporting her overall wellbeing, rather than as proof her symptoms are not real, respects the validity of her physical symptoms while still incorporating needed psychiatric support, consistent with the described need for good liaison between psychiatry and medical staff and a neutral, non-invalidating communication stance. Avoiding any mention of psychiatric involvement ever again fails the interdisciplinary care component and may deprive her of potentially beneficial CBT or other support. Repeating the same invalidating framing, even delivered more gently, still fails the core problem, implying her symptoms are not real, rather than correcting it. Making the referral mandatory as a condition of continued primary care is coercive and inconsistent with an empathetic, ethical, patient-centered approach. Discussing the referral only with her family, bypassing the patient, is inconsistent with the patient-centered, autonomy-respecting approach this framework calls for.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reintroducing psychiatric care as a coordinated, non-invalidating part of a broader interdisciplinary team, rather than as evidence symptoms are \"not real,\" is the ethical, empathetic approach that patient-centered planning calls for. COMLEX: This synthesis point directly connects nondismissive communication with broader interdisciplinary planning.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into the broader ethics/cost-effectiveness/interdisciplinary-care planning principles","isHighYield":true}]};
